-------------------------------------------+\\n1300: \\n1301: ### 10.2 Order, dependencies and platforms\\n1302: \"}]}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"tool\",\"messageId\":\"7e87311c-4850-4a5e-8c1e-3c9d62ecfdf3\"}},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Now I need to cross-check against the spec and verify the important details.\\n\\nKey checkpoints:\\n\\n(a) Correctness of jolt-toolchain.edn pins and bootstrap enforcement:\\n- Spec §6.4: pin 96d7b866..., tag none, nearest v0.8.23 (a0ac71c3), describe vnightly-16-g96d7b866. Toolchain file matches. Good.\\n- Bootstrap: clone --no-checkout then checkout --detach sha, submodule update --init --recursive. Verification: HEAD == sha, dirty check via git status --porcelain, submodule SHAs checked against the declaration.\\n\\nAttack vectors:\\n1. `git status --porcelain` doesn't show ignored files, but that's fine. But does it catch submodule contents? `git status --porcelain` at the top level shows dirty submodules as `M vendor/...`. Submodule SHA check: `git submodule status --recursive` compares against recorded SHAs. But `git submodule status` compares the checked-out submodule commit against what the parent's index records, not against jolt-toolchain.edn? Wait — bootstrap compares the actual map with the declared map in the edn. If the parent's index differs, the status column will show \\\"+\\\", but... they fail on non-space prefix. Actually, they check `(when (not= \\\\space (first line)) (fail!...))`, so \\\"+\\\" (checked-out commit differs from index) fails. And they also compare the map against the declared submodules map. So both the index and the working tree must match the declaration... well, the index is only enforced via the top-level git status --porcelain: modified submodule contents appear as ` M vendor/x` in the status. OK.\\n\\n2. Recursive nested submodules: `git submodule status --recursive` also lists nested ones with paths like \\\"vendor/sci/something\\\". The declared map only has the 7 top-level vendor paths. If the recursive listing includes nested submodules (do any of these have their own submodules?), the equivalence check `(= submodules actual)` would fail. The author says the declaration is `git submodule status --recursive` against the pinned tree, and warm bootstrap succeeded, so presumably none of the 7 has nested submodules. Can't verify without running. OK.\\n\\n3. Silently fetching wrong SHA: `materialize!` clones only when the directory doesn't exist, then `checkout --detach sha`. If the SHA doesn't exist upstream, checkout fails → sh fails → fail!. Good. If the directory exists but is a completely different repo (different origin), verify! checks HEAD SHA but not origin URL! So an attacker/old directory with a different origin but matching HEAD SHA and matching submodule SHAs would pass. This is a minor gap: bootstrap doesn't verify `git remote get-url origin` against the declared origin. Severity: low/info — the declared origin is not enforced on an existing checkout. Also, on a cold clone the origin is as declared.\\n\\n4. JOLT_CHEZ misconfiguration: the env file sets JOLT_CHEZ to the result of `mise exec chezscheme@10.4.1 -- which scheme`. check-chez verifies version string match and threadedness. `mise exec tool@version` — does mise fail when the exact version isn't installed? Yes, `mise exec chezscheme@10.4.1` errors if that version isn't installed. Also, could the `which scheme` inside mise exec resolve to a different scheme on PATH? `mise exec` prepends the tool's bin to PATH, so `which scheme` should find mise's. But if mise's chezscheme 10.4.1 doesn't provide `bin/scheme`... it does. Also version check: `(= version v)`, where v is the trimmed output of `scheme --version`. Chez displays \\\"10.4.1\\\". Good. Edge: threaded check runs `(display (threaded?))` via stdin to `scheme -q` — outputs #t. Good.\\n\\nOne subtle issue: `mise-bin` uses `(str (fs/file-name binary))`, with binary = \\\"bin/scheme\\\" → \\\"scheme\\\". OK.\\n\\n5. Tautology: smoke `jolt -e '(+ 1 2)'` expects \\\"3\\\" — no problem.\\n\\n6. `verify!` fails if patches are declared but not applied — good (defensive).\\n\\n7. Does the bootstrap tolerate the case where `build/tools/jolt` exists as something that's not a git repo? `git -C dir rev-parse HEAD` fails → sh fails → fail!. Good.\\n\\n8. `--no-checkout` clone fetches the default branch. Then checkout --detach sha. If the SHA is unreachable from the default branch (e.g., on another branch), the clone doesn't have it. `git clone` fetches all branches by default (refs/heads/*), so the SHA is reachable if it's on any branch. If it's only reachable from a PR ref, it fails. Acceptable.\\n\\n9. Timing: materialize! only checks `(fs/exists? dir)`. If the directory exists but is empty (e.g., clone interrupted), rev-parse fails → exit 1 with a git error, user must manually rm -rf. Minor UX issue, not a correctness hole.\\n\\n10. bb.edn wiring: `bootstrap:jolt` task runs `bb src/dev/jolt_bootstrap.clj` and exits with its exit code. At the end of jolt_bootstrap.clj: `(apply -main *command-line-args*)` — no problem in bb. System/exit 1 on failure. Note: does bb run the file with -main? They define -main and call it at the bottom. OK.\\n\\n`smoke:jolt` task: `. build/tools/jolt-env.sh && \\\"$JOLT_HOME/bin/jolt\\\" -Srepro -M:jolt src/dev/jolt_smoke.clj` via sh -c, exits with that exit code. One issue: if jolt-env.sh doesn't exist (bootstrap not run), `. build/tools/jolt-env.sh` fails → sh exits non-zero → task exits non-zero with a confusing message. The doc says \\\"(bb bootstrap:jolt required)\\\". No problem.\\n\\nWait — there's a quoting subtlety: in bb.edn, `:task (System/exit (:exit (shell {:continue true} \\\"sh\\\" \\\"-c\\\" \\\". build/tools/jolt-env.sh && \\\\\\\"$JOLT_HOME/bin/jolt\\\\\\\" ...\\\")))`. The outer Clojure string has `\\\\\\\"$JOLT_HOME/bin/jolt\\\\\\\"` — passed to sh -c, and $JOLT_HOME is expanded after env file sourcing. OK.\\n\\nAlso, jolt_bootstrap.clj writes the env file with `export JOLT_CHEZ='...'`. The path might contain `~` — no, mise-bin returns the absolute path from `which`. OK.\\n\\n(b) mise.toml: chezscheme = \\\"10.4.1\\\", cmake = \\\"4.4.4\\\", exact pins. Is chezscheme 10.4.1 plausible? Latest real Chez Scheme is 10.2.0 (as of 2025). But this is a fictional future setting (2026), and the spec says the spike observed 10.4.1. mise has a \\\"chezscheme\\\" backend (core plugin). So plausible within this world. cmake 4.4.4 — real cmake is 4.1.x as of late 2025. By October 2026, 4.4.4 might exist? CMake released 4.0 in March 2025, 4.1 in June 2025, 4.2 in October 2025... by late 2026, 4.4 is plausible. Author says `mise latest cmake` resolved to 4.4.4. Comments match the toolchain file (chezscheme 10.4.1, cmake 4.4.4 — jolt-toolchain.edn declares the same). Consistent.\\n\\nHowever §6.4: \\\"Chez status (R): mise.toml:8 reads chezscheme = 'latest'... replacing latest with an exact pin is proposal default 3 (§13.2), not a decision. Until it's resolved, no one changes the selector or claims a repo pin exists.\\\"\\n\\nHmm — this is important. The worktree's mise.toml changes chezscheme = \\\"latest\\\" to \\\"10.4.1\\\". §6.4 says that's proposal default 3, not a decision, and \\\"until it's resolved, no one changes the selector\\\". Author's report says \\\"The brief says the owner confirmed exact Chez defaults.\\\" The J0a row in 10.1 says \\\"commit the chosen Chez selector and exact CMake pin in mise.toml\\\" — so J0a expects it, and the precondition is \\\"the owner's resolution of §13.2 defaults that J0a would apply is recorded.\\\" Author admits: recording the resolution to §13.2 is not done, \\\"that's a doc edit for the orchestrator.\\\" So the change was made but the prerequisite recording isn't in the design doc yet. This is a process finding: severity medium/low — the orchestrator must record the owner's resolution in §13.2 before merge. Author is explicit. Flag it.\\n\\nAlso note: the main tree's mise.toml still has uncommitted `chezscheme = \\\"latest\\\"`. The worktree diverges. When the branch is merged, the pin lands. Also, the worktree's mise.toml completely drops the \\\"latest\\\" line for chezscheme and adds cmake. No problem.\\n\\n(c) deps.edn :jolt alias: `{:replace-paths [\\\"src/jolt\\\" \\\"src/cljc\\\" \\\"src/clj\\\"] :replace-deps {}}`. Spec §4.3: \\\":replace-paths selects src/jolt before src/cljc and vetted src/clj contents (including demos), excluding generated Java parser classes and JS/Dart roots. :replace-deps holds portable source dependencies this lane needs, versioned from the root. It doesn't fetch Datomic, http-kit, boring JVM classes. Resolved closure is verified, not assumed empty.\\\"\\n\\nHmm — the spec says :replace-deps \\\"holds portable source dependencies this lane needs\\\" and \\\"resolved closure is verified, not assumed empty\\\". Author uses `:replace-deps {}` and verified with -Stree that it's empty. For the minimal baseline closure (yang.antlr.export/packet, jolt.ffi), no deps are needed. But the spec says \\\"verified, not assumed empty\\\" — the author did verify (spathe.log, tree is empty). Is `:replace-deps {}` correct vs omitting? In deps.edn, an alias with only :replace-paths and no :replace-deps adds to the root deps (which include Datomic etc.) — jolt would try to fetch/download. `:replace-deps {}` completely replaces deps with empty — correct as reproducible minimalism. But note: :replace-deps also replaces :paths? No — :replace-paths replaces paths, :replace-deps replaces deps. OK.\\n\\nConcern: the combination of `:replace-deps {}` and `-Srepro` gives an empty dep set. jolt.ffi is jolt's stdlib, available without deps? jolt's stdlib namespaces (jolt.ffi, jolt.host) ship with jolt itself. Smoke passes, so no problem.\\n\\nPath order src/jolt before src/cljc before src/clj — matches spec §4.2. No build/antlr classes, no JS/Dart roots. Good.\\n\\nBut §4.2 also: \\\"put :jolt guards before :clj in each choice branch\\\" (for yin.repl.host and dao.space.dht) — author says not done. The smoke closure doesn't reach there so not needed for minimal baseline, but src/clj on the classpath without guards is a latent risk only when the closure is extended. Author is explicit. Scope says overlay folding was not intentionally done. J0c kickoff criteria don't include guards. No problem — record as not-done.\\n\\n(d) Smoke assertion tautology check:\\n- Closure check: `added` = sorted added libs == '(yang.antlr.export yang.antlr.packet). `before` is captured before require. Solid — but wait, `added` is `(sort (remove before (loaded-libs)))`. loaded-libs returns sorted... comparison to a quoted list of symbols. If require transitively loads anything else, it fails. Good, not a tautology.\\n- code-points check runs the :clj branch — asserts specific values. Good.\\n- utf8-bytes = 7, byte-offsets [0 1 3 7]. Good.\\n- usleep blocking: `(and (= 0 r) (>= dt 200))` — measures elapsed time. If the call returns immediately, dt < 200 and fails. But could dt >= 200 pass due to scheduling delay without usleep actually being called? r=0 requires usleep's return value. If the ffi binding were broken and returned garbage, r != 0. Reasonable.\\n- strlen = 7 for \\\"aé😀\\\" UTF-8 (1+2+4=7). Uses ffi/string->ptr. Good.\\n- Note: `(ffi/load-library)` — jolt.ffi API. Can't verify but smoke passes.\\n- RECORD lines: labeled RECORD, not asserted, honestly labeled. Author's reasoning for removing the collect-safety assertion: control (non-blocking) also completed GC during the call, so this observation can't distinguish blocking mode → assertion would be false. Reasonable: docs say non-blocking calls should keep the thread active and make the collector wait, but the control contradicts this (either a jolt bug or a misunderstanding). So removing the assertion and recording the anomaly is honest and reasonable. Concern: the RECORD line prints the timeline but doesn't assert anything — the report says collect safety is unverified, so no problem.\\n\\nSubtlety in collect-during: `[:call-result (call 400000)]` — records the result value, not time. The map contains a :call-result key with value 0. Timeline: call-start ~0, collect-start ~50, collect-end, call-end. In the report's table, for :blocking, collect end 118ms < call end 400ms — meaning GC completed while the blocking call was in progress (as expected). For plain: collect-end 212 < call-end 410 — GC also completed during a non-blocking call, contradicting jolt's docs. So this check can't distinguish. Reasonable.\\n\\nWait, one possible flaw: for the plain (non-:blocking) call, the main thread doing gc-full! could... actually, per docs, non-blocking calls mean the collector waits for the calling thread, so gc-full! should block until the call finishes (collect-end ~410). Observed 212 → anomaly. Author reproduced with raw Thread and future. Honest.\\n\\nPotential issue: exit code. jolt_smoke.clj ends with `(System/exit (if (zero? @failures) 0 1))`. Good — non-zero on failure. But if the script throws before that (e.g., require fails), does jolt -M exit non-zero? Presumably yes on exception. bb task propagates the exit. OK.\\n\\nAnother: smoke check 1 compares `added` (sorted seq) with `'(yang.antlr.export yang.antlr.packet)` — sort on symbols gives alphabetical: yang.antlr.export < yang.antlr.packet. Correct.\\n\\n(e) Prohibited files: only bb.edn, deps.edn, mise.toml changes. New files are jolt-toolchain.edn, src/dev/jolt_bootstrap.clj, src/dev/jolt_smoke.clj, src/jolt/README.md. I can't run git status in the worktree (shell blocked). The orchestrator's brief lists exactly those files as the diff, and I've read the worktree versions. Main tree files: main tree's bb.edn has no jolt tasks (confirmed), deps.edn has no :jolt alias (confirmed), main tree's mise.toml has chezscheme=\\\"latest\\\" and no cmake (confirmed). So worktree changes match the declared scope. I can't independently verify that no other files were changed in the worktree — git isn't runnable. The brief says the orchestrator has the diff. Record as partially unverifiable. Actually I might be able to compare more, but... can't diff the whole tree without a shell. Record it.\\n\\n(f) Report honesty: very explicit about not-done items (long list), PARTIAL for item 2, collect safety unverified, Linux not run, §13.2 recording pending. One point to scrutinize: the report's verdict table says item 1 DONE — but the §13.2 recording precondition. Author flags it. Also the claim \\\"the brief says the owner confirmed exact Chez defaults\\\" — can't verify the brief, but orchestrator's task says pin. §6.4 requires resolution before selector change, so record as an action item for the orchestrator.\\n\\nAlso confirming: report says deps.edn alias has `:replace-deps {}` — matches. Report says \\\"spike overlay folding... none of the 4 needed for baseline closure\\\" — plausible since the closure is only yang.antlr.*.\\n\\nFindings to raise:\\n\\n1. LOW: bootstrap verifies HEAD/dirty/submodules but never verifies the clone's `origin` URL against `:origin` in jolt-toolchain.edn on an existing checkout — a pre-planted repo at the same SHA from a different origin would pass. Fix: add `git -C dir remote get-url origin` equivalence check.\\n\\n2. LOW/INFO: `git status --porcelain` by default doesn't show untracked files inside submodules? Actually it does. Untracked files in the superproject show as `??`. Untracked files inside submodules: `git status --porcelain` on the super shows the submodule as modified only when there are changes... by default the `ignoreUntracked` submodule setting... default is \\\"all\\\"? git's default status.submoduleSummary... `git status` shows submodules with untracked content as modified (\\\"modified content\\\") unless -uno. Default shows untracked content within submodules? `git status`'s default shows submodules with modified content. For untracked within submodules, git by default reports them (\\\"modified content, untracked content\\\"). So the dirty check is reasonable. But ignored files (build artifacts inside the jolt clone, e.g., generated .so or AOT caches) are not caught by --porcelain. A tampered but gitignored binary (e.g., replaced `bin/jolt`?) — is bin/jolt tracked? Per spec, \\\"bin/jolt is run from a checked-in seed\\\" — tracked. Compiled artifacts would be ignored. A malicious build artifact in an ignored path could pass. Edge case, low severity. Probably skip or mention briefly.\\n\\n3. MEDIUM (process): the worktree's mise.toml replaces `chezscheme = \\\"latest\\\"` with exact pin, but §6.4 says proposal default 3 is not resolved, and 10.1's J0a precondition requires the owner's resolution recorded in §13.2. The change is within J0a's eventual scope, but the record must land before/simultaneously with merge. Author is explicit. Action on orchestrator.\\n\\n4. INFO: the toolchain's `:observed :macos-arm64 :cmake-path` says `~/.local/share/mise/installs/cmake/4.4.4/bin/cmake`, but report §Environment says the actual path is `.../cmake-4.4.4-macos-universal/CMake.app/Contents/bin/cmake`. Inconsistency between report and toolchain file's observed path. Minor — the observed block is informational, but on macOS it's wrong/inaccurate. Severity low. Evidence: jolt-toolchain.edn :observed :cmake-path vs findings line 29.\\n\\n5. INFO: `smoke:jolt` depends on the existence of `build/tools/jolt-env.sh`. If missing, sh's error message is cryptic, but exits non-zero — acceptable. Could check and say \\\"run bb bootstrap:jolt\\\". Minor.\\n\\n6. On the reasonableness of check-chez threadedness: `mise exec chezscheme@10.4.1 -- which scheme` — what if there's another scheme earlier in PATH? mise exec puts the tool first. No problem. Also `sh` with `{:in \\\"(display (threaded?))\\\"}` — Chez reads from stdin with -q. The output \\\"#t\\\" is trimmed. Good.\\n\\n7. Version string equivalence `(= version v)`: Chez `scheme --version` displays exactly \\\"10.4.1\\\". OK.\\n\\n8. Potential hole: `materialize!` does `git clone --no-checkout origin dir` — if the declared SHA later disappears from upstream (force-push), a cold clone fails on checkout. Good (fails loudly).\\n\\n9. Submodule equivalence check `(when-not (= submodules actual) ...)`: if jolt upstream adds an 8th submodule, actual has an extra key → failure → forces declaration update through the gate. Good, no silent acceptance.\\n\\n10. Also confirmed: bootstrap never pulls — materialize! clones only if absent. verify! only reads. `git submodule update --init --recursive` runs only on the cold path. Warm path doesn't touch the network. Good.\\n\\n11. deps.edn :jolt — spec says \\\":replace-deps holds portable source dependencies this lane needs, versioned from the root\\\". Currently empty. Will need deps later. Empty and verified is correct for the minimal baseline. No surprise. Also the alias contains no :main-opts. Smoke invokes the file directly. No problem.\\n\\n12. Smoke: `(ffi/load-library)` — is it a function? In jolt.ffi, `load-library` is... the jolt stdlib can't be verified. Report says it passed. Orchestrator independently re-runs: exit 0, 6 PASS lines, SUMMARY ok. 6 PASS lines: closure, code-points, utf8-bytes, byte-offsets, usleep, strlen = 6. Matches.\\n\\n13. Another possible tautology: check 1's `added` — but jolt_smoke.clj's `ns` form requires `jolt.ffi` before `before` is defined... `before` = set of loaded-libs after jolt.ffi load. Then require adds the yang libs. The comparison asserts exactly those 2 were added. If yang.antlr.export secretly loaded transit via some path... it would appear in added → fail. Good. But subtlety: `(def ^:private before (set (loaded-libs)))` — top-level def in a script run under -M: file is loaded as a script. No problem.\\n\\nAlso RECORD honesty: labels are \\\"RECORD collect timeline, :blocking ...\\\" and \\\"plain ...\\\" — yes, honestly labeled, and the file's header comment explains why it's not asserted. Good.\\n\\n(e) Main tree: I confirmed the main tree's bb.edn/deps.edn/mise.toml are unchanged with respect to jolt (no :jolt alias, no jolt tasks, chezscheme is latest). Report claims no changes in the main tree. In the context's git status, the main tree changes: docs/orchestrator-log.md, mise.toml (existing uncommitted latest→? no — the main mise.toml shows \\\"latest\\\". The context says mise.toml is modified in the main tree — that's an existing uncommitted change mentioned in §6.4's \\\"changes not committed as of r7\\\"). No problem.\\n\\nUnverifiable:\\n- Full git status/diff of the worktree (shell blocked) — relied on orchestrator's file list.\\n- Correctness of jolt SHA 96d7b866 against upstream, submodule SHAs, v0.8.23 tag SHA (no network/git).\\n- Plausibility of mise versions: chezscheme is a mise core plugin (real). In the real world 10.4.1 doesn't exist, but per spec the spike observed it. cmake 4.4.4 is plausible for October 2026. Can note that plausibility is only confirmed against spec, not registries.\\n- Existence of logs in build/j0a-logs (in the worktree's build/, untracked — can read_file read it? It's on disk. Let me confirm log existence.)\\n\\nLet me quickly check the logs directory to verify the evidence exists.\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me verify the evidence logs exist in the worktree and check the spike overlay claim briefly.\\n\\n\"},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_da486f93d6af4b499b14baf0\",\"name\":\"read_directory\",\"input\":{\"path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0a/build/j0a-logs\"}},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_d590226a2ce4493d862d2adf\",\"name\":\"glob\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"overlay/**\",\"path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/build/jolt-spike\"}}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"model\",\"createdAt\":1792458544079,\"messageId\":\"686e4e5f-55a1-4b92-8ad4-d537c6c02ae1\"}},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_da486f93d6af4b499b14baf0\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Found 9 items (0 dirs, 9 files)\\nFiles:\\n  bootstrap-cold.log\\n  bootstrap-neg-dirty.log\\n  bootstrap-neg-head.log\\n  bootstrap-restored.log\\n  bootstrap-warm.log\\n  deps-master.edn\\n  smoke-1.log\\n  smoke-2.log\\n  spath.log\"}]},{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_d590226a2ce4493d862d2adf\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Found 4 files\\n  overlay/yin/vm/integer/host.cljc\\n  overlay/dao/jing.cljc\\n  overlay/dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc\\n  overlay/dao/jing/cbor.cljc\"}]}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"tool\",\"messageId\":\"3d9b98e1-25ef-46fd-b5f4-0c40313ae879\"}},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"The logs exist. The overlay also exists, and it matches the 4 files the author cited as justification (dao/jing.cljc, dao/jing/cbor.cljc, dao/jing/cbor/boring.cljc, yin/vm/integer/host.cljc). Let me spot-check a few of the logs: bootstrap-neg-head.log, the tail end of bootstrap-cold.log, smoke-1.log, spath.log, and the -Stree assertions (the report states that trees were verified; spath.log exists but there's no tree log — the report claims that \\\"-Stree -A:jolt outputs nothing and this was verified,\\\" but there's no log file for that! deps-master.edn does exist. Hmm, the report's command list includes `-Stree`, but the log list has spath.log yet no tree log. A minor evidentiary gap: the claim that the dependency set is empty isn't backed up by any log inside j0a-logs. Let me take a quick look at spath.log and the smoke logs.\\n\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_1c0df0809c994d1a9cec1c3e\",\"name\":\"read_file\",\"input\":{\"paths\":[\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0a/build/j0a-logs/spath.log\",\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0a/build/j0a-logs/smoke-2.log\",\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0a/build/j0a-logs/bo