claude\\n4: Session-ID: 8f45af4f-0557-4cfa-a375-78525ee6a40e\\n5: # Findings: J0c round 1 - the ANTLR4 C++ parser built and called from jolt (feasibility ladder)\\n6: Role: Compiler & AST / Stream engineer (native build and FFI probe)\\n7: Implementers:\\n8: - Model: claude-opus-5-5 | Assigned: 2026-10-20 08:17:10 +07 | Status: done (round 1, macOS arm64) | Rationale: owner routing \\\"opus-5.5 for implementation, cmd for review\\\"\\n9: \\n10: ## Verdict\\n11: \\n12: | Rung | Result | Evidence |\\n13: |---|---|---|\\n14: | R1 pinned ANTLR4 C++ runtime 4.13.2, hash-checked, built with mise CMake | **DONE** | `r1-build.log` (exit 0, 39.67 s); negative tamper test `r1-neg-sha.log` (exit 1) |\\n15: | R2 real pinned Python3 grammar generated with `-Dlanguage=Cpp`, helpers identified | **DONE** | `r2-gen.log` (exit 0); deterministic re-run against recorded digests `r2-gen-verify.log`, `r2-gen-verify2.log` (exit 0) |\\n16: | R3 extern \\\"C\\\" wrapper, `:blocking` defcfn call from jolt, bytes decoded in jolt | **DONE** | `r3-build.log` (exit 0); `r3-probe-1.log`, `r3-probe-2.log`, `final-probe.log` (7/7 PASS, exit 0) |\\n17: \\n18: No rung failed. **macOS arm64 only; Linux x86_64 (shiva) NOT RUN.**\\n19: \\n20: - Worktree: `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c`, branch `jolt-j0c` from `jolt-j0a` @ `9f1f9144`. Changes are uncommitted and nothing is staged.\\n21: - Logs: `…/jolt-j0c/build/j0c/logs/`.\\n22: - Baseline: `bb bootstrap:jolt` (cold) exit 0 in 81.71 s (`bootstrap.log`). A final `bb smoke:jolt` exit 0 (`final-smoke.log`).\\n23: \\n24: ## R1: the C++ runtime (DONE)\\n25: \\n26: - **Declaration**, in `jolt-toolchain.edn :native :antlr4-cpp-runtime`:\\n27:   - Version 4.13.2, BSD-3-Clause.\\n28:   - Source `https://www.antlr.org/download/antlr4-cpp-runtime-4.13.2-source.zip`. That is the official C++ runtime distribution; the GitHub 4.13.2 release has no C++ asset.\\n29:   - SHA-256 `0ed13668906e86dbc0dcddf30fdee68c10203dea4e83852b4edb810821bee3c4`.\\n30:   - Upstream: `antlr/antlr4` tag `4.13.2`, tag object `2ac185af855ff39b4b8c41854b756767099c356f`, commit `cc82115a4e7f53d71d9d905caa2c2dfa4da58899`, path `runtime/Cpp`.\\n31: - **Not verified:** that the zip's contents equal `runtime/Cpp` at that commit. I compared no file-level digests. This is a provenance gap for review.\\n32: - **`bb build:jolt-native`** (new; runs `src/dev/jolt_native.clj`):\\n33:   - Fetches into `build/native/src/` only when the archive is absent, and accepts it only at the declared SHA-256. A mismatch deletes the file and fails.\\n34:   - Extracts fresh and builds with the mise CMake into `build/native/darwin-arm64/`.\\n35:   - Writes `build/native/darwin-arm64/build-record.edn` with tool versions, flags and output SHA-256s.\\n36:   - Nothing downloads at namespace load.\\n37: - **Flags:** `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DANTLR_BUILD_CPP_TESTS=OFF -DANTLR_BUILD_SHARED=OFF -DANTLR_BUILD_STATIC=ON -DCMAKE_POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE=ON -DCMAKE_CXX_STANDARD=17`.\\n38:   - **Static linking**, so the runtime is linked into the single wrapper dylib that jolt loads. There is no second library or rpath to resolve at load time (§7.2 asks for this choice to be explicit).\\n39:   - **Tests are OFF** because the runtime's `ANTLR_BUILD_CPP_TESTS=ON` default makes CMake download googletest during the build.\\n40: - **Toolchain:** CMake 4.4.4 (mise, `…/cmake-4.4.4-macos-universal/CMake.app/Contents/bin/cmake`); compiler Apple clang 21.0.0 (clang-2100.1.1.101). The build has 2 harmless upstream warnings.\\n41: - **Output:** `build/native/darwin-arm64/antlr4-runtime/lib/libantlr4-runtime.a`.\\n42:   - **Finding:** its SHA-256 differs on every identical rebuild: `e086b10d…`, `94bcda41…`, `28ce9a67…` across three builds. The archive is not reproducible, presumably because `ar` embeds timestamps (not investigated).\\n43:   - The linked dylib was byte-identical across all three builds (see R3), so gates should digest the dylib, not the archive.\\n44: - **Negative test:** I appended one byte to the cached zip. The task answered `has SHA-256 9bc059e7…, declared 0ed13668…; deleted` and exited 1 (`r1-neg-sha.log`). I restored the original zip and confirmed its SHA-256.\\n45: \\n46: ## R2: generating the C++ parser (DONE)\\n47: \\n48: **Helpers exist at the pinned grammar revision.** grammars-v4 `8af0d4c26c796ea27c15c3d85418f2d0f77c3adb`, `python/python3/Cpp/`, has:\\n49: - `Python3LexerBase.h` (890 bytes)\\n50: - `Python3LexerBase.cpp` (3784 bytes)\\n51: - `Python3ParserBase.h` (233 bytes)\\n52: - `Python3ParserBase.cpp` (182 bytes)\\n53: - `transformGrammar.py`\\n54: \\n55: The four helpers are now in `manifest.edn :files` with their BLAKE3 digests, fetched by the existing generator at the pinned revision:\\n56: \\n57: | file | BLAKE3 |\\n58: |---|---|\\n59: | `Cpp/Python3LexerBase.h` | `393ff86d…817a` |\\n60: | `Cpp/Python3LexerBase.cpp` | `cd598ca5…a5b3` |\\n61: | `Cpp/Python3ParserBase.h` | `59afaba9…3349` |\\n62: | `Cpp/Python3ParserBase.cpp` | `38a2790b…c28b` |\\n63: \\n64: - **License and provenance:** no notice in any file, so they are recorded **UNRESOLVED** exactly like the Java/JS/Dart helpers. `:redistribution :blocked-until` now names C++ too, and generated output stays under `build/` (local build only, never staged).\\n65: - **The C++ target needs more than `-Dlanguage=Cpp`:**\\n66:   1. **A grammar transform.** The pinned `.g4` files carry Java-syntax actions (`this.onNewLine()` and similar) and two `// Insert here @header for C++ …` placeholders. Upstream's own `Cpp/transformGrammar.py` (BLAKE3 `7600aaee…50ec`) rewrites `this.`→`this->` and fills the headers. I recorded the identical edits as 13 exact-text `:patches` on `Python3Lexer.g4`/`Python3Parser.g4` under the `:cpp` target, rather than running the Python script. The two identical `{ this.CannotBePlusMinus() }?` lines are disambiguated by their rule names `literal_pattern`/`literal_expr`.\\n67:   2. **The positioned NEWLINE/DEDENT patch.** The other three targets all carry it, and I ported it to the C++ token factory as 5 exact-text patches on `Cpp/Python3LexerBase.{h,cpp}`:\\n68:      - a new `positionedToken(type, start, stop)`;\\n69:      - NEWLINE placed on its newline characters (`tokenStartCharIndex .. +newLine.size()-1`);\\n70:      - DEDENT and the end-of-input NEWLINE zero-width at `getCharIndex()`.\\n71: \\n72:      **This is a helper patch port I wrote, disclosed here for review against the Java patch.** It is not a hand-written parser. It is not yet checked against the JVM oracle (span parity is later J0c work).\\n73: - **Generator change** (`src/dev/yang_antlr_gen.clj`): a target may now declare `:exts`. C++ keeps and digests both `.h` and `.cpp`, where previously only the first helper's extension was kept, which would have deleted every header. The Java, JavaScript and Dart targets are unchanged: no `:exts` means the old behaviour.\\n74: - **Command:** `clojure -M:antlr-gen --target cpp` (ANTLR tool 4.13.2 from the existing `:antlr-gen` alias).\\n75:   - First run: exit 0, 16.75 s. It generated 8 files in `build/antlr/python3/cpp/`, all ASCII (the generator enforces this).\\n76:   - I recorded their BLAKE3 digests as `:cpp :generated`. Re-runs exited 0 with matching digests, so generation is deterministic and the drift check is live.\\n77:   - Digests: `Python3Lexer.cpp a8be1b51…`, `Python3Lexer.h a1cb6339…`, `Python3LexerBase.cpp 65027edf…`, `Python3LexerBase.h 8548fd2c…`, `Python3Parser.cpp d30cc4df…`, `Python3Parser.h 5e62a9c3…`, `Python3ParserBase.cpp 38a2790b…` (unpatched), `Python3ParserBase.h 59afaba9…` (unpatched).\\n78: - **Defect I introduced and then fixed:** my first manifest edit had one extra `}`. That made the top-level map close early. `clojure.edn/read-string` then silently ignored the rest, dropping `:license` and `:redistribution`, while generation still worked because it doesn't read them. cljstyle exposed it. After the fix, all five top-level keys read back with nothing trailing, matching HEAD. **Worth a guard in the generator:** read with a `PushbackReader` and reject trailing forms.\\n79: - **Divergences in the C++ helper, noted and not fixed:**\\n80:   - Its `commonToken` takes line/column from `lastToken` (Java takes them from the lexer).\\n81:   - Its `getIndentationCount` iterates UTF-8 bytes, not code points.\\n82: \\n83:   Both are candidates for the later JVM-oracle parity comparison.\\n84: \\n85: ## R3: the wrapper and the jolt call (DONE)\\n86: \\n87: - **Location:** `native/jolt-parser/` (`CMakeLists.txt`, `jolt_parser.cpp`). It is a new top-level directory for hand-written native sources:\\n88:   - not `src/`, whose roots are Clojure source roots on jolt's classpath;\\n89:   - not `antlr/`, which holds only pinned grammar inputs and manifests;\\n90:   - not `build/`, which is for generated and untracked output.\\n91: - **ABI** (`extern \\\"C\\\"`, C types only):\\n92:   - `int32_t jolt_parser_parse(const uint8_t* src, size_t len, uint8_t** out, size_t* out_len)`\\n93:   - `void jolt_parser_free(uint8_t*)`\\n94:   - `int32_t jolt_parser_layout_version(void)`\\n95: - **Error handling:** every entry catches `std::exception` and `(...)` and returns −2/−3 with a message buffer. −1 means invalid arguments, −4 means over the 64 MiB output bound.\\n96: - **Buffer layout:** private, fixed-width, little-endian: magic `JPT1`, layout version, status, rule-name table, symbolic-name table, preorder records, collected listener errors. The full layout is documented in the source header.\\n97:   - Each rule record carries its index and child count. Each token record carries type, code-point start/stop and UTF-8 text.\\n98:   - This is raw marshalling only: no Jing encoder, no `toStringTree`, no packet. It is a subset of the §7.3 field list (first-failure evidence and context start are later).\\n99: - **Build:** `bb build:jolt-native` links `libjolt_parser.dylib` against the pinned static runtime and the generated sources (`r3-build.log`, exit 0, 49.95 s).\\n100:   - SHA-256 `fd8ac980a123ac9610743d36c12930bc539de1b40edb2e649f40f9e4b8f9df76`, **identical across three builds**. Mach-O arm64, 1,356,232 bytes, depends only on `libc++` and `libSystem`.\\n101:   - **Finding:** `CXX_VISIBILITY_PRESET hidden` applies only to my own translation units. The three `jolt_parser_*` entries plus about 1,556 `antlr4::` C++ symbols from the static archive are exported (`nm -gU`). A later round should use an exported-symbols list (`-exported_symbols_list` / a version script) so the runtime cannot interpose or be interposed.\\n102: - **jolt side** (`src/dev/jolt_parser_probe.clj`, run with `\\\"$JOLT_HOME\\\"/bin/jolt -Srepro -M:jolt src/dev/jolt_parser_probe.clj`):\\n103:   - **Load and bind:** `ffi/load-library` loads the dylib by absolute path. `(ffi/defcfn c-parse \\\"jolt_parser_parse\\\" [:pointer :size_t :pointer :pointer] :int :blocking)`, plus free and version.\\n104:   - **Call:** `parse-bytes` copies the UTF-8 source into an `ffi/alloc` buffer and calls the parser. It copies the result with `ffi/read-array` into a jolt byte array, frees it through `jolt_parser_free` in a `finally`, and frees its own cells. No pointer leaves the function.\\n105:   - **Decode:** the decoding is plain Clojure over the byte array.\\n106: - **Result for `\\\"1 + 2\\\\n\\\"`:** status 0, a 3196-byte buffer consumed exactly, parse call 2.7–3.6 ms. The decoded tree:\\n107: \\n108: ```\\n109: file_input\\n110:   stmt\\n111:     simple_stmts\\n112:       simple_stmt\\n113:         expr_stmt\\n114:           testlist_star_expr\\n115:             test\\n116:               or_test\\n117:                 and_test\\n118:                   not_test\\n119:                     comparison\\n120:                       expr\\n121:                         expr\\n122:                           atom_expr\\n123:                             atom\\n124:                               NUMBER \\\"1\\\" [0,0]\\n125:                         ADD \\\"+\\\" [2,2]\\n126:                         expr\\n127:                           atom_expr\\n128:                             atom\\n129:                               NUMBER \\\"2\\\" [4,4]\\n130:       NEWLINE \\\"\\\\n\\\" [5,5]\\n131:   EOF \\\"<EOF>\\\" [6,5]\\n132: ```\\n133: \\n134: - **Checks: 7/7 PASS** on three runs (`r3-probe-1.log`, `r3-probe-2.log`, `final-probe.log`):\\n135:   - the layout version is 1;\\n136:   - magic, layout and exact consumption;\\n137:   - no errors;\\n138:   - the root is `file_input`;\\n139:   - the token sequence with code-point spans `NUMBER 1 [0,0]`, `ADD + [2,2]`, `NUMBER 2 [4,4]`, `NEWLINE [5,5]`;\\n140:   - the last token is EOF;\\n141:   - malformed `\\\"1 +\\\\n\\\"` returns status 0 with a syntax-error buffer of two listener errors (`no viable alternative at input '+'` at 1:2, `extraneous input '\\\\n' …` at 2:0), and no exception crosses the boundary.\\n142: - **One iteration fixed:** the first run failed the EOF check because ANTLR's EOF type is −1 and my decoder looked only in the non-negative symbol table. The probe now names −1 `\\\"EOF\\\"`, as the packet contract does. The wrapper was unchanged.\\n143: - **Not compared against the JVM yet.** The NEWLINE span `[5,5]` comes from the ported positioned patch, and the EOF span `[6,5]` (zero-width at index 6) is plausible but unchecked.\\n144: \\n145: ## Collect-safety observations (nothing built on `:blocking` semantics)\\n146: \\n147: - The parse entry is bound `:blocking` as §7.5 prescribes. No collect-safety claim is made: J0a found that a full collection finishes during a 400 ms call whether or not the call is `:blocking`, and I did not re-test that here.\\n148: - The `\\\"1 + 2\\\\n\\\"` parse takes about 3 ms, too short to observe a collection against. A long-parse collection test (§7.5) needs a larger input and a mechanism that can discriminate, and is later J0c work.\\n149: \\n150: ## Not done this round (later J0c rounds; not started)\\n151: - Corpus parity with the 