pair?). C++ passes current line/charPositionInLine. Report already flags the line/col divergence (commonToken takes line/col from lastToken). Author already disclosed the discrepancy. OK.\\n\\n- getCharIndex()-1 for DEDENT when getCharIndex() can be 0? DEDENT at start of file — zero-width stop = start-1; if start=0, stop = SIZE_MAX (size_t underflow!). `positionedToken(Python3Parser::DEDENT, getCharIndex(), getCharIndex() - 1)` — getCharIndex returns size_t; 0-1 wraps to huge value. Then written as u32 idx() saturates to 0xFFFFFFFF = \\\"none\\\". Java version uses int, so -1... CommonToken(type,...,0,-1)? In Java start=0, stop=-1 (int). C++ wraps to SIZE_MAX then saturates to 0xFFFFFFFF — same \\\"none\\\" encoding on the wire. At probe level OK, though token text read-back with start=0 stop=huge value could misbehave if ANTLR reads text from input... but text \\\"\\\" is passed? positionedToken passes `\\\"\\\"` as text to factory create — wait, C++ patch: `_factory->create({this,_input}, type, \\\"\\\", DEFAULT_TOKEN_CHANNEL, start, stop, ...)` — text \\\"\\\" explicitly. Java's CommonToken(pair, type, channel, start, stop) — text null → lazy read from source. Behavior diverges: C++ sets explicit empty text, Java reads back from source. Hmm, in C++ if text is \\\"\\\" does getText() return \\\"\\\"? Actually CommonToken::getText returns _text if non-empty, else extracts from input. \\\"\\\" → empty → read from stream at start..stop. If stop wraps to SIZE_MAX, extraction would be huge/throw? Can DEDENT ever be issued at charIndex 0? DEDENT happens at EOF or dedent... at EOF, getCharIndex() = length. Never 0 unless file starts with a dedent — impossible (no indent at pos 0). Edge case: empty file? No DEDENT. Low risk; but worth INFO: size_t underflow path (getCharIndex()-1) is unguarded, relies on idx() saturation; NEWLINE at input end similar (getCharIndex()-1 at EOF — file with trailing content: getCharIndex() = size, no issue).\\n\\nNext check the probe adversarially (d):\\n- Checks decode exact buffer consumption (`:consumed = :length`) — catches trailing garbage.\\n- Token check compares (butlast tokens) to exact list — includes NEWLINE [5,5]; last is EOF. If wrapper wrote garbage spans, would fail.\\n- Malformed input check: status 0 + tree status 1 + errors non-empty. Doesn't check error content (report says 2 errors — log shows?). Check only asserts (seq errors). Weak but sufficient for feasibility; report quotes actual messages from logs.\\n- Can PASSes pass while broken? \\\"no syntax errors\\\" checks (:status tree) is zero AND errors empty. Root check: first node rule = file_input. Reasonable. One subtle: `check \\\"tokens...\\\"` compares butlast — if tokens list is empty, butlast = empty ≠ expected → fail. OK.\\n- parse-bytes: on error status (-2), buffer is a message string not JPT1 — decode would misparse; probe only asserts status=0 path for both parses (malformed input returns 0). If wrapper returned -2, check would fail (status ≠ 0). Good.\\n- Free path: c-free in finally, only when non-null. in/out-cell/len-cell freed in outer finally. ffi/alloc (max 1 n) — for empty string, n=0, allocates 1, writes nothing, passes len 0 with pointer. Wrapper accepts. No issue.\\n- `ffi/read out-cell :pointer` — if wrapper crashed before writing... wrapper always writes *out=nullptr first. OK.\\n\\n- Probe's platform detection: os is \\\"mac\\\" → darwin, otherwise linux (assumed). No issue.\\n\\njolt_native.clj (R1 enforcement):\\n- fetch!: downloads only if absent; verifies sha256 after download AND if cached; on mismatch deletes + fails. Tamper case: appended byte → sha mismatch → delete, exit 1 (r1-neg-sha.log). But note: fetch! verifies the zip but not contents after unzip. Also doesn't check that the zip's contents match upstream tag (author flagged as unverified provenance gap). Honest.\\n- Uses `unzip -q archive -d src` — assumes unzip available; fine on macOS.\\n- Build records include cmake path/version, cxx version, flags, output sha256. CMake/compiler flags recorded ✓ (jolt-toolchain.edn + build-record.edn's cmake-flags).\\n- Reproducibility claim: archive not reproducible (ar timestamps), dylib identical across 3 builds. Honestly disclosed; gates should digest the dylib. OK.\\n- fetch! URL: file name from last segment of URL — \\\"antlr4-cpp-runtime-4.13.2-source.zip\\\". Build unpacks into \\\"antlr4-cpp-runtime-4.13.2\\\" — assumes zip root directory name; if zip has different root, cmake -S src fails (FATAL, exit non-zero → fail!). Fail-closed. OK.\\n- One issue: `http/get` with :throw false, checks status 200. No TLS pinning beyond hash — hash check suffices.\\n- sh vs run!: cxx version via `sh \\\"c++\\\" \\\"--version\\\"` — uses system c++, but actual build compiler is cmake default (also system c++/Apple clang). build-record captures that. Note: not using mise-pinned compiler; spec says \\\"compiler version and flags\\\" recorded — recorded. OK.\\n\\nbb.edn diff — need to verify only the addition of build:jolt-native. Let me read j0c bb.edn and base (jolt-j0a worktree has bb.edn at commit 9f1f9144; jolt-j0a worktree is at that state + origin fix... actually the jolt-j0a worktree has uncommitted r2 fixes; jolt-j0c branches from committed 9f1f9144, which presumably includes the r2 fixes). Simplest: read j0c bb.edn and diff in head against what I've already seen.\\n\\nAlso spec §7.2/§7.3 quick compliance check: §7.2 new generation targets (lines 696–725), §7.3 wrapper ABI (725–761). Let me read those and the logs. Also verify logs exist: build/j0c/logs/.\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Now the bb.edn delta, spec §7.2-7.3, and the J0c evidence logs.\\n\\n\"},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_777d0ccbf0be40e5b1599b3f\",\"name\":\"read_file\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md\",\"offset\":696,\"limit\":90}},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_e23d6e37852c4f15ace43024\",\"name\":\"read_directory\",\"input\":{\"path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/build/j0c/logs\"}},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_dbbe80c9e456448bb754158e\",\"name\":\"grep\",\"input\":{\"pattern\":\"jolt\",\"path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/bb.edn\",\"output_mode\":\"content\",\"-n\":true,\"-C\":4}}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"model\",\"createdAt\":1792460115391,\"messageId\":\"523d6dbc-2279-4594-bdb0-c3ed1d912df8\"}},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_777d0ccbf0be40e5b1599b3f\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Read 1/1 file, 90 lines\\nNote: showing lines 696-785. More of the file remains. Use offset=786 to continue.\\n696: ### 7.2 A new generation target\\n697: \\n698: R: ANTLR4 supports `-Dlanguage=Cpp`; its official target list has C++ and no C\\n699: target ([4.13.2 C++\\n700: target](https://github.com/antlr/antlr4/blob/4.13.2/doc/cpp-target.md), [4.13.2\\n701: target list](https://github.com/antlr/antlr4/blob/4.13.2/README.md)). The\\n702: runtime is C++17 with CMake builds. This is ANTLR4 C++, not the ANTLR3 C\\n703: runtime.\\n704: \\n705: R: `deps.edn:12-14,74-77` pins the tool and Java runtime to 4.13.2;\\n706: `yang.antlr.md:530-534`, `antlr/python3/manifest.edn` and\\n707: `src/dev/yang_antlr_gen.clj` generate Java, JavaScript and Dart only. C++ is a\\n708: **new generation and export target**, not an existing port to enable. Keep the\\n709: same pinned grammar revision and 4.13.2 tool; pin the C++ runtime source to the\\n710: matching release; record source digests, flags, compiler/CMake versions,\\n711: architecture, generated output hashes, helper patches and linkage. A new target\\n712: never authorizes changing grammar or profile identity to hide a parity failure.\\n713: \\n714: P: build the runtime, the generated Python parser, the target-specific\\n715: lexer/parser helpers and the wrapper for both platforms. Port indentation,\\n716: synthetic NEWLINE/DEDENT positioning and predicate helpers from the pinned\\n717: grammar package; the manifest already patches these for existing targets, and\\n718: selecting Cpp alone does not make Python work. Extend `gen:antlr` and the\\n719: manifest with a `:cpp` target, the positioned NEWLINE/DEDENT patches, helper\\n720: provenance and generated-file digests. Choose static runtime linkage inside the\\n721: wrapper or a separately packaged runtime explicitly; neither yields a single\\n722: self-contained executable. Generation may still need the JVM build tool;\\n723: deployed parsing needs neither JVM nor Node.\\n724: \\n725: ### 7.3 Wrapper ABI and the shared exporter\\n726: \\n727: P: `src/jolt/yang/antlr/cst.jolt` implements the existing host surface:\\n728: `make-worker`, `parse-unit`, `parse-transform`, `utf8-bytes`, `first-failure`. R\\n729: (r7): `src/cljc/yang/antlr/export.cljc:1-14,84-160` owns packet construction;\\n730: `src/clj/yang/antlr/cst.clj:78-154,168-221` supplies host-tree access and\\n731: failure information; `yang.antlr.md:655-661` requires identical packet values.\\n732: **Neither the exporter nor the Jing encoder is ported to C++.** The jolt facade\\n733: calls the shared `export/export-nodes`, `export/result`, `export/failure-kind`\\n734: and error-span helpers.\\n735: \\n736: The `extern \\\"C\\\"` entry takes source UTF-8 pointer and length plus host-only\\n737: mode/profile inputs, and returns a status and one bounded private fixed-layout\\n738: marshalling buffer:\\n739: \\n740: - preorder token and rule records: symbolic token type or rule name/index plus\\n741:   table, code-point start/stop, UTF-8 text, child count, context start;\\n742: - raw listener errors: message, line/column, offending-token start/stop or lexer\\n743:   token-start/input index;\\n744: - first-failure evidence: error kind, offending and last-buffered indices,\\n745:   lexical marker, real non-EOF token index/start/stop.\\n746: \\n747: Fields have explicit widths, lengths and offsets. Arithmetic, bounds and\\n748: topology are checked before an `ops` view is handed to shared export code.\\n749: Spans, synthetic tokens and packets are normalized in shared code, not C++.\\n750: \\n751: This layout is process-local marshalling like a sockaddr, not a value codec; it\\n752: never enters a stream or crosses a trust domain. Only packet values from the\\n753: jolt/shared exporter enter source/CST streams, and canonical encoding stays\\n754: Jing. Records are copied into host-owned values and the native buffer is freed\\n755: through its matching wrapper entry on every path. No pointer enters guest\\n756: values, packets, stores or continuations. Every exported entry catches\\n757: `std::exception` and `catch (...)` and returns an explicit bounded error, with a\\n758: nonallocating failure status where needed. No C++ class, layout or exception\\n759: crosses the C boundary.\\n760: \\n761: ### 7.4 Per-worker caches\\n762: \\n763: P: an opaque native worker handle, owned only by the jolt host facade.\\n764: `make-worker` creates it; its parser stage owns it and disposes it at\\n765: close/reset, including temporary convenience-call workers. Mutable DFA vectors\\n766: and PredictionContextCaches belong to the worker: worker-owned\\n767: LexerATNSimulator/ParserATNSimulator instances replace the generated defaults\\n768: before recognition, with correct cleanup. Per-call parser, token and tree state\\n769: dies after marshalling; caches may survive calls. Immutable generated tables may\\n770: be shared; mutable caches may not. A worker is used by one thread at a time;\\n771: thread-local caches alone do not isolate two workers on one thread.\\n772: \\n773: R: `yang.antlr.md:551-569` requires isolation and JVM `cst.clj:43-71,133-150`\\n774: implements it. The [4.13.2 C++ codegen\\n775: template](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/antlr/antlr4/4.13.2/tool/resources/org/antlr/v4/tool/templates/codegen/Cpp/Cpp.stg)\\n776: (read, not run) wires static DFA/context caches into the generated simulators.\\n777: J0c audits the generated code and helper caches, interleaves two workers on one\\n778: thread, runs independent workers on separate threads, requires packets equal to\\n779: isolated runs, and instruments the generated static caches to show no growth\\n780: during recognition after initialization. Fresh parser construction alone is not\\n781: proof.\\n782: \\n783: ### 7.5 Synchronous surface and collect-safe calls\\n784: \\n785: The jolt `yang.antlr.cst` surface is **synchronous, as on every other host**\"}]},{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_e23d6e37852c4f15ace43024\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Found 13 items (0 dirs, 13 files)\\nFiles:\\n  bootstrap.log\\n  final-build.log\\n  final-probe.log\\n  final-smoke.log\\n  r1-build.log\\n  r1-neg-sha.log\\n  r2-gen-verify.log\\n  r2-gen-verify2.log\\n  r2-gen.log\\n  r3-build.log\\n  r3-probe-1.log\\n  r3-probe-2.log\\n  r3-probe.log\"}]},{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_dbbe80c9e456448bb754158e\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\".claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/bb.edn-2- {gen:antlr\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/bb.edn-3-  {:doc \\\"Generate the pinned grammars-v4 parsers for every target (antlr/*/manifest.edn): Java compiled into build/antlr/<g>/classes, JavaScript in build/antlr/<g>/js, Dart in lib/antlr/<g>; filters after --: --grammar g --target t\\\"\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/bb.edn-4-   :task (apply shell \\\"clojure\\\" \\\"-M:antlr-gen\\\" *command-line-args*)}\\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/bb.edn-5-\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/bb.edn:6:  ;; jolt host lane (experimental, docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md)\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/bb.edn:7:  bootstrap:jolt\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/bb.edn:8:  {:doc \\\"Materialize the jolt-toolchain.edn pins into build/tools/jolt (mise Chez/CMake checked, jolt cloned at the full SHA, never pulled) and wri