1ed2d546e2e7f9cc90f9e1b6f7512d53bd5b44567d7154909c83349\\nfetched Dart/Python3LexerBase.dart 45022cf97972b104313464d80e923ff2fca50b9d5a3d00502067bc569bc218f4\\nfetched Dart/Python3ParserBase.dart e4c4693a640e53b971d37088809c74539cc0f1dda7d9c6a3cdd9beb7ce40a87d\\nfetched Java/Python3LexerBase.java 5212b67286df5ec7f8818876cbb2bb178e80fcdc5b3daae69e225edd5f68d385\\nfetched Java/Python3ParserBase.java f29fa8fc257027885afdd9c778add55a1128c83b0f888035904d952be0e5222d\\nfetched JavaScript/Python3LexerBase.js 25f008c2ee815b3c21af0b037e6dd99aa4b91d54f9f92720f218d542e731262f\\nfetched JavaScript/Python3ParserBase.js e106d38dbd2dff1cc036b70ddc2829b733aa207f1adb2d987e358d3edd316e84\\nvendored Python3Lexer.g4 407a5312b637ebe386eabaceca758a5714f1d3e26b1f058b96e43dca3ec02662\\nvendored Python3Parser.g4 e82099eb8fda51ea8b49c266cba8eb2af47eb400fe657d8d8867b1eaf62fe00d\\ngenerated 8 cpp files in build/antlr/python3/cpp\\nreal 2.24\\nuser 7.53\\nsys 0.30\\nexit 0\\n\\n\"}]}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"tool\",\"messageId\":\"f95c44a2-d76a-48dc-8be9-1c83d54770c7\"}},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"final-probe.log shows 7 PASS lines, SUMMARY ok, exit 0 — the orchestrator brief says \\\"6 PASS lines\\\" — miscounted on the brief side; the log is authoritative: 7 PASS. Minor discrepancy in the brief, not the author's fault. Note it down.\\n\\nr1-neg-sha.log: correct reason, exit 1. ✓\\nr2-gen-verify.log: all digests match the manifest, exit 0, determinism verified via re-run. ✓\\n\\nNow, findings from my adversarial read:\\n\\nC1 (low): jolt_parser.cpp `message()` inside the catch handler does allocation (std::string, vector); if allocation throws (exactly the OOM situation where bad_alloc was caught), a second exception escapes the catch block across the extern \\\"C\\\" boundary → UB/terminate. §7.3 demands \\\"a non-allocating failure status when needed\\\". Fix: use a static const message or a noexcept path for -2/-3 (e.g., return without a buffer, or copy e.what() via a non-throwing path using malloc).\\n\\nC2 (low/info): C++ LexerBase patch: `positionedToken(Python3Parser::DEDENT, getCharIndex(), getCharIndex() - 1)` — getCharIndex() returns size_t; at index 0 it underflows to SIZE_MAX and saturates to 0xFFFFFFFF via idx() (\\\"none\\\"). Also NEWLINE passes text \\\"\\\" to _factory->create — wait, in the C++ patch text \\\"\\\" is passed explicitly, whereas Java passes null text (read back from source). Hmm: the manifest comment says for C++ \\\"text is read back from input\\\": the comment says \\\"text is read back from input\\\" but the patch passes \\\"\\\"? `_factory->create({this,_input}, type, \\\"\\\", DEFAULT_TOKEN_CHANNEL, ...)`. In ANTLR C++'s CommonToken, if _text is set to \\\"\\\" (non-null?), getText() returns _text if _text is non-null… CommonToken::getText(): `if (!_text.empty()) return _text; ... read from source`. Actually in ANTLR C++ CommonToken, if _text is non-empty return it, otherwise extract from the input stream between start..stop. So \\\"\\\" → read from source. OK, the comment is accurate. But DEDENT with start=k, stop=k-1 (underflow to SIZE_MAX): text extraction from start..stop with stop < start → ANTLR C++'s getText from source: `if (_input == nullptr) return \\\"\\\"; ... _input->getText(misc::Interval(start, stop))` — Interval with huge stop → could throw or return a weird value. ANTLR's CharStream::getText with an invalid interval — ANTLRInputStream::getText clamps stop to size-1 and returns \\\"\\\" if start > stop? In the C++ runtime, ANTLRInputStream::getText(interval): if interval.stop >= size then stop = size-1; if start > stop returns \\\"\\\". Probably safe. Java version has the same zero-width pattern (stop=start-1) and it's landed and works. So at most info. Mention briefly as info: size_t wraparound is intentionally mapped to the documented 0xFFFFFFFF \\\"none\\\" encoding; verify against the oracle.\\n\\nActually, wait — for a zero-width DEDENT at index k: start=k, stop=k-1. The wire writes idx(k-1) — if k>0 that's fine (k-1). Only k=0 wraps. DEDENT at charIndex 0 can't happen (DEDENT requires a prior indent). NEWLINE at EOF: at EOF getCharIndex()=size, stop=size-1, fine.\\n\\nC3 (info): fetch coupling — adding Cpp helpers to the manifest's :files means gen:antlr for any target will fetch + digest-check them (see r2-gen-verify.log fetching all files); a network/CDN failure on a Cpp file will also break Java/JS/Dart generation. Outputs and digests for the three existing targets are unchanged (digests byte-identical in manifest; generator changes are opt-in via :exts). Note only.\\n\\nC4 (info): the report/author's own callouts are honest: zip-vs-tag provenance unverified (report line 31), .a is not reproducible, exported symbols (~1556 antlr4:: symbols exported — interposition risk), helper license UNRESOLVED, no parity with JVM, divergence (line/col from lastToken; getIndentationCount byte-wise). All disclosed.\\n\\nC5 (info): brief says orchestrator observed \\\"6 PASS lines\\\"; final-probe.log has 7 PASS + SUMMARY ok. Discrepancy in orchestrator brief, not the change itself.\\n\\nC6? probe's render function — stack logic complexity, cosmetic only. Skip.\\n\\nC7 (info): CMakeLists `file(GLOB GENERATED_SOURCES \\\"${GENERATED_DIR}/*.cpp\\\")` — glob picks up any cpp in the directory; the generation directory contains only pinned 8 files (digest-checked). GLOB without CONFIGURE_DEPENDS is a stale-cache hazard on rebuild, but jolt_native.clj deletes the build tree each time (fs/delete-tree bld). Fine.\\n\\nC8: jolt_native.clj uses system `c++` for the record but cmake picks its own default compiler — the recorded cxx is presumably the same Apple clang. Also `unzip` availability is assumed. Info at most; skip for word budget.\\n\\nConfirming (b) \\\"byte-identical\\\" behavior of shared tools: changes in yang_antlr_gen.clj:\\n1. docstring usage line (comment only),\\n2. `target-exts` added,\\n3. run-antlr!: exts vector + not-any? — equivalent to single-extension filter for old targets,\\n4. generated-digests: some ends-with? against [ext] — equivalent,\\n5. generate-target! passes (target-exts target) — equivalent.\\nOne subtle behavioral difference: old code filtered output files by `(not (str/ends-with? name ext))`; new `(not-any? #(str/ends-with? name %) exts)` with exts=[\\\".java\\\"] — identical predicate. And generated-digests old `(str/ends-with? name ext)` vs new `(some ... exts)` — identical. So existing targets' outputs + digests are byte-identical. ✓ Also java/js/dart manifest :generated digests unchanged from base. ✓ And verify re-run log confirms digests for cpp target; for other targets r2-gen log? r2-gen.log presumably runs --target cpp only. Existing-target regeneration hasn't been re-run by the author? They claim unchanged via code path. Orchestrator can run bb gen:antlr. This is a \\\"couldn't verify\\\" item: I haven't re-run Java/JS/Dart generation to confirm identical outputs; equivalence is by code read (strong).\\n\\n(e) Forbidden files: touched files = manifest, bb.edn, jolt-toolchain.edn, yang_antlr_gen.clj + new native/ and src/dev files. No changes to sources under dao.*/yin.vm; main tree untouched (couldn't run git but confirmed main-tree manifest + gen file are the base versions; bb.edn diff is additive only).\\n\\nAlso confirm nothing else in the worktree changed: deps.edn/mise.toml/src/dev/jolt_bootstrap.clj etc. — the jolt-j0c branch is based on jolt-j0a commit 9f1f9144, which contains the J0a content (committed). Declared changes are 4 modifications + 4 new. jolt_bootstrap.clj inside the j0c worktree — unverified; probably committed as of base. Skip — note reliance on file reads of the declared list.\\n\\nWord budget: <700. Verdict: SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES (fix C1) or SIGNED OFF? C1 is a genuine (rare) escape path that violates §7.3's \\\"non-allocating failure status when needed\\\" and \\\"no exception shall cross the C boundary\\\". In an adversarial review, should flag as low severity and require a fix. This is a feasibility probe, and §7.3 explicitly requires non-allocating failure when needed — I'll go with SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES with C1 as the only required fix.\\n\\nCouldn't verify: SHA-256 of upstream zip / equivalence with tag tree (no network); re-run of generation for existing targets; the dylib being byte-identical across builds (trust the report + build record?); git status of the full worktree; actual jolt/ANTLR runtime behavior (relied on logs); the brief's \\\"6 PASS\\\" vs the log's 7 PASS.\\n\\nWrite it up in under 700 words.\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 01:31:53 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 08:31:53 +07\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\\n# Review: J0c round 1 (ANTLR4 C++ feasibility ladder)\\nRole: Reviewer (adversarial, read-only) | Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max\\n\\n## Verdict: SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES\\n\\nAll three rungs are real and fail-closed; one required correction (F1) and minor notes.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n| id | sev | file:line | evidence | correction |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| F1 | low (required) | jolt_parser.cpp:120-128 (`message`, used by both catch handlers) | The catch blocks call `message()`, which allocates (`std::string`, `std::vector`, then `hand_off`'s malloc). Under the very OOM that triggers `bad_alloc`, the handler can throw again, and an exception escaping a catch block crosses the `extern \\\"C\\\"` boundary - UB/`std::terminate`. §7.3:756-758 requires \\\"a nonallocating failure status where needed\\\" and no exception crossing. | Make the -2/-3 paths nonallocating: return the status with `*out=nullptr` (guest already treats non-JPT1 as failure), or copy `e.what()` through a `malloc`/`memcpy`-only path with a static fallback message. |\\n| F2 | info | antlr/python3/manifest.edn (:cpp patches, `Cpp/Python3LexerBase.cpp`) | `positionedToken(DEDENT, getCharIndex(), getCharIndex()-1)` on `size_t` wraps to SIZE_MAX at index 0; `idx()` saturation maps it to the documented 0xFFFFFFFF \\\"none\\\", so no UB reaches the wire, and DEDENT at index 0 is unreachable in practice. Also C++ sets text `\\\"\\\"` (read back from source) like Java's null; the ported patch matches the Java semantics modulo the disclosed line/col divergence. | None now; keep both under the JVM-oracle span-parity check the report already defers. |\\n| F3 | info | antlr/python3/manifest.edn (:files), src/dev/yang_antlr_gen.clj (fetch-grammar!) | Adding the 4 Cpp helpers to `:files` makes every target's `gen:antlr` run fetch and digest-check them (r2-gen-verify.log:2-13), so a Cpp fetch failure now breaks Java/JS/Dart generation too. Outputs/digests unaffected. | Acceptable coupling (all targets re-pin together); note it in the manifest comment. |\\n| F4 | info | orchestrator brief vs final-probe.log | The brief says \\\"six PASS lines\\\"; the log shows **7** PASS + SUMMARY ok + exit 0 (probe has 7 checks). Report's 7/7 is correct; the brief undercounted. | Orchestrator bookkeeping only. |\\n\\n## What checks out\\n\\n- (a) R1: jolt-toolchain.edn `:native :antlr4-cpp-runtime` pins version, URL, SHA-256, upstream tag/commit, cmake flags; jolt_native.clj `fetch!` downloads only when absent, verifies SHA-256 on both fresh and cached archives, deletes+fails on mismatch (r1-neg-sha.log: correct reason, exit 1). build-record.edn captures cmake path/version, cxx version, flags, output SHA-256s. Non-reproducible `.a` (ar timestamps) and byte-identical dylib are disclosed, with the right conclusion (digest the dylib). Zip-vs-tag content equality is NOT verified - honestly flagged (report:31).\\n- (b) R2 shared-tool safety: yang_antlr_gen.clj's only behavioral change is opt-in `:exts`; for targets without it, `target-exts` returns `[(helper-ext)]` and `not-any?`/`some ends-with?` are predicate-identical to the old single-ext code, so Java/JS/Dart filtering, staging, patches and digests are untouched. Manifest `:generated` digests for all three are byte-identical to base. Exact-text patches fail closed (`apply-patch` throws unless exactly one occurrence; the duplicate `CannotBePlusMinus` lines are disambiguated by rule name). `:license`/`:redistribution` survived the brace mistake: all five top-level keys present, Cpp helpers recorded UNRESOLVED, `blocked-until` names C++.\\n- (c) R3: length-based `std::string(src,len)` preserves embedded NULs; 64 MiB bound (-4); `-1` arg check; `idx()` saturation prevents u32 overflow; only `uint8_t*`/`size_t` cross the boundary; malloc/free matched via `jolt_parser_free`; tree owned by the per-call parser, freed at scope exit; per-call lexer/parser/listener objects, static ATN read-only (two-worker audit correctly deferred, §7.4); walk recursion depth unbounded - inherent to ANTLR recursive descent, §7.6 budget deferred and disclosed. The `message()` escape (F1) is the only containment hole.\\n- (d) Probe: assertions are non-tautological - exact buffer consumption (`:consumed = :length`), exact token/span list via `(butlast tokens)` (empty list fails), root rule name, malformed case requires status 0 + buffer status 1 + nonempty errors, and the log shows the real listener messages; decode handles EOF type -1. Free in `finally` on every path; no pointer escapes `parse-bytes`.\\n- (e) Scope: bb.edn delta is only the `build:jolt-native` task (:18-21); no dao.stream/dao.data/dao.jing/yin.vm source touched; main-tree manifest and generator confirmed at base versions.\\n- (f) Report honesty: extensive NOT-done list matching §7.8 (corpus parity, exporter/cst.jolt, worker isolation, collect-safety, sanitizers, NUL/invalid-UTF-8 policy, exported-symbols list, Linux); helper provenance explicitly UNRESOLVED; helper divergences and the ported-patch authorship disclosed.\\n\\n## Could not verify\\n\\n- Upstream truth of the zip SHA-256, tag/commit IDs, and helper BLAKE3s (no network); zip contents vs `runtime/Cpp` at the tag 