\n11: Worktree: `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c`. Logs are in its `build/j0c/logs/`.\\n12: Changes are uncommitted and nothing was staged.\\n13: \\n14: ## Finding -> change\\n15: \\n16: | finding | change |\\n17: |---|---|\\n18: | **F1 (required)**: the catch handlers allocated | `native/jolt-parser/jolt_parser.cpp` `message()` is now `noexcept`. It sets `*out = NULL` and `*out_len = 0` first, then copies `e.what()` (or `\\\"unknown exception\\\"`) using only `strlen`/`malloc`/`memcpy`. If that `malloc` fails, the buffer stays NULL and only the status (−2/−3) reports the failure. No `std::string`/`std::vector` remain in any failure path, and nothing in a handler can throw. The header comment documents this. |\\n19: | F1 test | New exported **test seam** `int32_t jolt_parser_inject_fault(int32_t mode)`. It is off (0) unless called, and answers the previous mode. Mode 1 throws `std::bad_alloc` inside the parse's `try`; mode 2 throws a non-`std::exception` (`42`); mode 3 throws `bad_alloc` and also makes the handler's own `malloc` fail. The probe adds four checks (below). The seam is always compiled in: it is one atomic int, disclosed here, and gating it behind a build option is a later-round decision. |\\n20: | F2 (info) | No change, as the review asked. DEDENT at index 0 wrapping and text semantics stay under the deferred JVM-oracle span parity. |\\n21: | F3 (info) | `antlr/python3/manifest.edn`, beside the `Cpp/` `:files` entries: \\\"Every target's gen:antlr run fetches and digest-checks ALL of these, so a Cpp helper fetch failure also blocks the Java, JavaScript and Dart generation; all targets re-pin together.\\\" |\\n22: | F4 (orchestrator note) | Acknowledged. The probe had 7 checks and now has **11**. |\\n23: \\n24: ## F1 evidence: `src/dev/jolt_parser_probe.clj`, 11/11 PASS (`fix-probe.log`, `fix-probe-final.log`, both exit 0)\\n25: \\n26: The 7 round-1 checks are unchanged and still pass. The new checks:\\n27: \\n28: | check | result |\\n29: |---|---|\\n30: | `[injected]` `std::bad_alloc` inside the parse | `{:status -2, :message \\\"std::bad_alloc\\\"}` PASS |\\n31: | `[injected]` non-std exception | `{:status -3, :message \\\"unknown exception\\\"}` PASS |\\n32: | `[injected]` `bad_alloc`, and the handler's own malloc fails | `{:status -2, :buffer-bytes 0}` (NULL buffer, no throw) PASS |\\n33: | after the injected failures, the same process parses `\\\"1 + 2\\\\n\\\"` again | `{:status 0, :buffer-bytes 3196}` PASS |\\n34: \\n35: - The jolt process survives every injected failure and reads the failure status. Each injection is reset to 0 in a `finally`.\\n36: - **Rebuild:** `bb build:jolt-native` exit 0, 49.62 s (`fix-build.log`).\\n37: - New dylib SHA-256: `00c27f065ced03717ac378d560b2a781fbd2aabd3232efdf5aa9eadc9c515419`. It changed because the source changed.\\n38: - `nm -gU` now shows four C entries: `jolt_parser_parse`, `jolt_parser_free`, `jolt_parser_layout_version`, `jolt_parser_inject_fault`.\\n39: \\n40: ## Cross-target verification (Java, JavaScript, Dart unchanged vs base `jolt-j0a`)\\n41: \\n42: - **Task name:** `docs/agents/build-n-test.md:120,254,407` names `bb gen:python-antlr`, but `bb.edn` defines only `gen:antlr`. That is a doc/task naming mismatch, pre-existing and not changed here. I ran **`bb gen:antlr`** (every target, foreground, with no other generation running in this worktree).\\n43:   - Result: exit 0, 3.65 s (`fix-gen-all.log`). \\\"generated 8 cpp / 4 dart / 4 java / 4 javascript files\\\".\\n44:   - The generator throws on any difference from a target's recorded `:generated` digests, so a zero exit already proves each output matches its record.\\n45: - **Network:** none was needed. `build/antlr/python3/upstream/` already held all 12 pinned files from round 1. The log's \\\"fetched\\\" label is printed for cache hits as well, and the generator downloads only when a file is absent.\\n46: - **Recorded digests:** the `:java`, `:javascript` and `:dart` target maps,