,\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:22:                   ;; Every target's gen:antlr run fetches and digest-checks ALL of\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:23:                   ;; these, so a Cpp helper fetch failure also blocks the Java,\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn:24:                   ;; JavaScript and Dart generation; all targets re-pin together.\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn-25-                   \\\"Cpp/Python3LexerBase.h\\\" {:blake3 \\\"393ff86dff65763fee957305788c5e63411adae06477ed70c6f1ab7de712817a\\\"},\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn-26-                   \\\"Cpp/Python3LexerBase.cpp\\\" {:blake3 \\\"cd598ca53f0e192605bef42f76991c747d2a2d4599635e034f9b5980b6baa5b3\\\"},\\n.claude/worktrees/jolt-j0c/antlr/python3/manifest.edn-27-                   \\\"Cpp/Python3ParserBase.h\\\" {:blake3 \\\"59afaba901ed2d546e2e7f9cc90f9e1b6f7512d53bd5b44567d7154909c83349\\\"},\"}]}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"tool\",\"messageId\":\"431d9717-4c39-43c7-8d28-d0bbe4fe9a4d\"}},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"All checks complete. Let me verify whether the fault injection logs exist: fix-probe.log, fix-probe-final.log, fix-gen-all.log. A quick directory check of the logs isn't strictly required, but it's preferable to have one. There's enough information; the orchestrator independently re-ran and confirmed 11 PASS.\\n\\nAdversarial evaluation of message(): noexcept, only strlen/malloc/memcpy; g_fault.load() is atomic; no static buffer, no strerror/errno, no thread-safety issues (only touches caller-provided pointers and the atomic variable). e.what() is virtual noexcept. The catch(...) path uses a string literal. The -1/-4 paths don't allocate. F1 CLOSED.\\n\\nFault injection validity: throws at the top of the actual try block → goes through the actual handler; mode 3 executes the *out=NULL branch inside message() (same branch as actual malloc failure). Not a mock of the handler itself. Sound. One caveat: mode 3 simulates malloc failure with a flag rather than actual allocation failure — the simulated branch is identical to the real one.\\n\\nSeam risk: in the production dylib, jolt_parser_inject_fault is exported — any in-process code can deterministically disable parsing or fake failures. Severity is low in a local single-process probe (attacker would need in-process code execution, and at that point they can do worse things); becomes problematic at packaging time (J5 local-build-only deploy, J8). Recommendation: keep it for now, but gate it with a CMake option (default OFF, probe build is ON), or exclude it via the planned export symbol list before J5 packaging; record the decision in the J0c checklist.\\n\\nF3: manifest.edn:22-24 has a note. Cross-target digest claim: according to the report, bb gen:antlr exits 0 (the generator throws on digest mismatch, so exit 0 proves all :generated digests match), and the target maps for java/js/dart match jolt-j0a in EDN. The base manifest digests I compared in round 1 were byte-for-byte identical; the current manifest retains them (in the earlier j0c manifest read I confirmed the same digests; the fix round only added comment lines 22-24 — this is consistent with the fix report which states manifest.edn was modified only for the F3 note). The 12 digests in the report's table match the base values I read (bb61a463..., 855f47de..., a29f3f8c..., e4c4693a...). CLOSED.\\n\\nVerdict: SIGNED OFF. Under 300 words.\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 01:41:22 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 08:41:22 +07\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\\n# Review: J0c round 1 fixes - confirmation\\nRole: Reviewer (read-only) | Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max\\n\\n## Verdict: SIGNED OFF\\n\\n**F1: CLOSED.** jolt_parser.cpp:128-141: `message()` is `noexcept`, sets `*out=nullptr; *out_len=0` first, then only `strlen`/`malloc`/`memcpy` (plus one atomic load); on malloc failure the buffer stays NULL and only the status reports. Adversarial pass: `e.what()` is noexcept by standard; the `catch(...)` path passes a string literal; no `std::string`/`std::vector`, no `strerror`/errno, no static buffers, no per-thread state beyond the `std::atomic<int32_t>` seam; the `-1`/`-4` paths allocate nothing. Nothing throwable remains on any failure path. Header comment (:32-34) documents it.\\n\\n**Fault injection is sound, not a mock.** Modes 1/2 throw at the top of the real `try` in `jolt_parser_parse` (:152-157), so the actual catch handlers run; mode 3 exercises the same NULL-buffer branch inside `message()` a real malloc failure would take. Probe checks (jolt_parser_probe.clj:176-199) assert status -2 with `\\\"std::bad_alloc\\\"`, -3 with `\\\"unknown exception\\\"`, -2 with zero-length buffer, and a clean re-parse afterwards, each resetting the seam in `finally`. Orchestrator's independent 11-PASS run matches.\\n\\n**Seam risk: low now, gate before packaging.** `jolt_parser_inject_fault` is exported in the production dylib, so any in-process code can force fake parse failures. The threat model requires code already running in the process, which can do worse; for a local feasibility probe this is acceptable. Recommendation: before J5 packaging/J8, exclude it via the already-planned exported-symbols list or a CMake option defaulting OFF (probe build ON), and record the decision in the J0c checklist. Not a blocker this round.\\n\\n**F3: CLOSED.** manifest.edn:22-24 carries the coupling note verbatim. Cross-target claim holds: the `:java`/`:javascript`/`:dart` `:generated` digests are byte-identical to base jolt-j0a (verified against my round-1 read; report's 12-row table matches), `bb gen:antlr` exit 0 proves outputs match records (the generator throws on drift), and this round's manifest change is comment-only.\\n\\n**Not verified:** dylib SHA `00c27f06…` and `nm` output (no shell); fix logs beyond the orchestrator's re-run.\"}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"model\",\"createdAt\":1792460620759,\"messageId\":\"b8654f57-69d1-497f-9afc-fdf216d45747\"}},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Created-GMT: 2026-10-20 09:10:22 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 16:10:22 +07\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\\n# Task: review the platform-aware J0b probe and its Linux x86_64 evidence\\nRole: Reviewer (read-only)\\nImplementers:\\n- Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max | Assigned: 2026-10-20 16:10:22 +07 | Status: active | Rationale: owner routing \\\"cmd for review\\\"; author claude-opus-5-5; resume the reviewer\\n\\nReview READ-ONLY: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/build/jolt-spike/j0b/udp_probe.clj and run.sh (platform-aware port; port report /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792485587000-engineer-jolt-j0b-linux-port.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md) against your earlier J0b-era design reading (spec: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md section 8.3). Evidence: macOS arm64 runs build/jolt-spike/j0b/run-7..11.log (36 assertions pass, 3 records) and the REAL Linux x86_64 runs from shiva (Ubuntu 24.04.5, jolt 96d7b866, Chez 10.4.1): build/jolt-spike/j0b/shiva/j0b-shiva-1..3.log (37 assertions pass, 0 fail, 3 records, three identical results) plus j0a-smoke-shiva.log and j0a-bootstrap-shiva.log. The probe files on shiva are byte-identical to the local ones (sha256 verified by the orchestrator).\\nCheck: (a) the Linux constants (EAGAIN 11, EMSGSIZE 90, ENOBUFS 105, EBADF 9, SO_SNDBUF 7, SO_RCVBUF 8, MSG_TRUNC 0x20, SOCK_DGRAM 2) and platform detection via jolt.socket.native/os: sound? any macOS value left hardcoded in a Linux path (or the reverse)? (b) the Linux-only assertion (SO_SNDBUF read-back at least twice the request): correct for Linux semantics (the log shows request 2048 read back 4608 = clamped minimum doubled) and honest, not a tautology; (c) whether the Linux log truly satisfies section 8.3 items 1-5 or only passes because expectations were relaxed per platform (compare the check labels in Linux and macOS logs; list any item the Linux run weakens or skips, e.g. natural would-block was NOT observed on either platform and rests on injection, short send injection-only); (d) does the report/run claim more than the logs show; (e) run.sh portability (POSIX sh, no macOS-only command on Linux) and that the header records enough to reproduce. Under 450 words, final response only, header fields, verdict SIGNED OFF / SIGNED OFF WITH CHANGES / NOT SIGNED OFF, findings table (id, severity, file:line, evidence, correction), and what you could not verify.\"}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"user\",\"createdAt\":1792487428087,\"messageId\":\"cb7c28f3-b45a-4e8a-96e1-43c8eb48ef19\"}},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Let's read spec §8.3, the probe file, run.sh, the port report, and the logs.\\n\\nFiles:\\n- build/jolt-spike/j0b/udp_probe.clj\\n- build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh\\n- collab/1792485587000-engineer-jolt-j0b-linux-port.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md\\n- Spec §8.3 (lines 931–967)\\n- Logs: run-7..11.log, shiva/j0b-shiva-1..3.log, j0a-smoke-shiva.log, j0a-bootstrap-shiva.log\\n\\nThere's a lot to read. I'll start with spec + probe + run.sh + report first.\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"I'll read the spec §8.3, the probe, run.sh, and the port report first.\\n\\n\"},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_b02c9a17ce9b48d0b5f9f32c\",\"name\":\"read_file\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md\",\"offset\":931,\"limit\":37}},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_2c8103f22715478ba15cb773\",\"name\":\"read_file\",\"input\":{\"paths\":[\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh\",\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792485587000-engineer-jolt-j0b-linux-port.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md\"]}}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"model\",\"createdAt\":1792487452609,\"messageId\":\"5ca444d5-0f06-417c-88d2-e17d50accee0\"}},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_b02c9a17ce9b48d0b5f9f32c\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Read 1/1 file, 37 lines\\nNote: showing lines 931-967. More of the file remains. Use offset=968 to continue.\\n931: ### 8.3 J0b probe (owner addition, 2026-10-19)\\n932: \\n933: Separate macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 records are required. It establishes raw\\n934: transport feasibility only, not datagram/DHT conformance.\\n935: \\n936: 1. Bind nonblocking SOCK_DGRAM loopback sockets through FFI and read back the\\n937:    ephemeral port. Send/receive via sendto/recvfrom; compare payload bytes and\\n938:    source address for zero-length, embedded-NUL, distinct consecutive and\\n939:    limit-sized packets. One successful send submits one whole packet; one\\n940:    successful receive consumes at most one packet. A short send is an error,\\n941:    never success and never permission to send the rest as a second packet.\\n942: 2. N = 1200, the landed default (V: `datagram.cljc:55-60`), plus one configured\\n943:    larger N below the observed platform send maximum; receive buffers hold at\\n944:    least N+1 bytes. Record platform limits and sysctls rather than assume them.\\n945:    Verify SOCK_DGRAM and EMSGSIZE from headers (review-reported 2 on both,\\n946:    EMSGSIZE 40 macOS / 90 Linux; R until checked). Above-platform-limit send\\n947:    failure (EMSGSIZE) is tested separately and is an error, not a truncation.\\n948:    Send N, N+1 and greater than the receive buffer; any length over N surfaces\\n949:    an oversize/truncation error, never a successful prefix. If recvmsg/MSG_TRUNC\\n950:    is used instead, bind and verify it on both platforms.\\n951: 3. Send EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK (and macOS ENOBUFS) appear as a distinct raw result,\\n952:    e.g. `:would-block`, with errno and no accepted packet. At the datagram\\n953:    writer surface the outcome is the landed clean transport-error with reason\\n954:    plus the send-failed event (§8.1). Where loopback cannot induce EAGAIN, use\\n955:    deterministic native-seam injection, labelled separately from natural\\n956:    observations, and also exercise real successful syscalls.\\n957: 4. An empty receive waits through the poller, yielding the fiber instead of\\n958:    spinning or blocking the scheduler; another fiber progresses; a packet wakes\\n959:    the waiter. Send-side readiness waits and harness retries are probe\\n960:    observations only, never synchronous `append!` behaviour or an adapter queue.\\n961:    Test cancellation, close and cleanup.\\n962: 5. Record toolchain/runtime SHA, architecture, commands, packet lengths and\\n963:    counts, error/outcome traces, poller wakeup evidence and cleanup results. An\\n964:    unavailable machine is an incomplete gate, not a pass. The Linux x86_64\\n965:    machine is shiva (§10.2).\\n966: \\n967: ### 8.4 J7 integration\"}]},{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_2c8103f22715478ba15cb773\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Read 2/2 files\\n--- File Contents ---\\n\\n// File: build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh (shell)\\n#!/bin/sh\\n# J0b probe runner (foreground), macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64. POSIX sh\\n# (Ubuntu's /bin/sh is dash). Run from the repository root.\\n#\\n#   JOLT       jolt binary. Default: build/tools/jolt/bin/jolt (J0a layout),\\n#              else build/jolt-spike/jolt/bin/jolt (spike layout).\\n#   JOLT_CHEZ  threaded Chez 10.x. Default: build/tools/jolt-env.sh's value when\\n#              that file exists (J0a layout), else the mise 10.4.1 install path\\n#              when present; otherwise left unset and jolt searches PATH.\\ndir=$(cd \\\"$(dirname \\\"$0\\\")\\\" && pwd)\\n\\nif [ -z \\\"$JOLT_CHEZ\\\" ] && [ -f build/tools/jolt-env.sh ]; then\\n  . build/tools/jolt-env.sh\\nfi\\nif [ -z \\\"$JOLT\\\" ]; then\\n  if [ -x build/tools/jolt/bin/jolt ]; then\\n    JOLT=build/tools/jolt/bin/jolt\\n  else\\n    JOLT=build/jolt-spike/jolt/bin/jolt\\n  fi\\nfi\\nif [ -z \\\"$JOLT_CHEZ\\\" ]; then\\n  mise_chez=\\\"$HOME/.local/share/mise/installs/chezscheme/10.4.1/bin/scheme\\\"\\n  [ -x \\\"$mise_chez\\\" ] && JOLT_CHEZ=$mise_chez\\nfi\\n[ -n \\\"$JOLT_CHEZ\\\" ] && export JOLT_CHEZ\\n\\njolt_home=$(cd \\\"$(dirname \\\"$JOLT\\\")/..\\\" 2>/dev/null && pwd)\\necho \\\"jolt: $JOLT\\\"\\necho \\\"jolt-sha: $(git -C \\\"$jolt_home\\\" rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null) dirty=$(git -C \\\"$jolt_home\\\" status --porcelain 2>/dev/null | wc -l | tr -d ' ')\\\"\\nif [ -n \\\"$JOLT_CHEZ\\\" ]; then\\n  echo \\\"chez: $JOLT_CHEZ $(\\\"$JOLT_CHEZ\\\" --version 2>&1)\\\"\\nelse\\n  echo \\\"chez: JOLT_CHEZ unset (jolt searches PATH)\\\"\\nfi\\n\\ncase \\\"$(uname -s)\\\" in\\n  Darwin)\\n    echo \\\"uname: $(uname -sm) macOS $(sw_vers -productVersion 2>/dev/null)\\\"\\n    echo \\\"sysctl: $(sysctl -n net.inet.udp.maxdgram net.inet.udp.recvspace kern.ipc.maxsockbuf 2>/dev/null | tr '\\\\n' ' ')(maxdgram recvspace maxsockbuf)\\\"\\n    echo \\\"lo0 mtu: $(ifconfig lo0 2>/dev/null | sed -n 's/.*mtu \\\\([0-9]*\\\\).*/\\\\1/p' | head -1)\\\"\\n    ;;\\n  Linux)\\n    os=$(sed -n 's/^PRETTY_NAME=\\\"\\\\(.*\\\\)\\\"$/\\\\1/p' /etc/os-release 2>/dev/null)\\n    echo \\\"uname: $(uname -sm) $os\\\"\\n    sys=\\\"\\\"\\n    for k in core/wmem_default core/wmem_max core/rmem_default core/rmem_max ipv4/udp_wmem_min ipv4/udp_rmem_min ipv4/udp_mem; do\\n      sys=\\\"$sys net.$(echo $k | tr / .)=$(tr '\\\\t' ' ' < /proc/sys/net/$k 2>/dev/null)\\\"\\n    done\\n    echo \\\"sysctl:$sys\\\"\\n    echo \\\"lo mtu: $(cat /sys/class/net/lo/mtu 2>/dev/null)\\\"\\n    ;;\\n  *)\\n    echo \\\"uname: $(uname -sm) (unsupported platform for this probe)\\\"\\n    ;;\\nesac\\n\\n\\\"$JOLT\\\" \\\"$dir/udp_probe.clj\\\"\\nrc=$?\\necho \\\"exit: $rc\\\"\\nexit $rc\\n\\n\\n// File: collab/1792485587000-engineer-jolt-j0b-linux-port.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md (markdown)\\nCreated-GMT: 2026-10-20 08:39:47 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 15:39:47 +07\\nCoding-Agent: claude\\nSession-ID: 8f45af4f-0557-4cfa-a375-78525ee6a40e\\n# Findings: J0b UDP probe made platform-aware (macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64)\\nRole: Stream & Network Engineer\\nImplementers:\\n- Model: claude-opus-5-5 | Assigned: 2026-10-20 15:39:47 +07 | Status: done (macOS verified; Linux NOT RUN by me) | Rationale: owner: \\\"push the jolt-j0a branch and run J0b on shiva\\\"\\n\\n**macOS behaviour is unchanged:** `SUMMARY 36 assertions: 36 pass, 0 fail; 3 records`, exit 0, on 5 foreground runs. The PASS/RECORD labels are identical to `run-6.log`.\\n\\n**The Linux expectations below are written but UNTESTED.** I cannot run on shiva.\\n\\nFiles touched: only `build/jolt-spike/j0b/udp_probe.clj`, `build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh`, and new logs `run-7..11.log`. `run-1..6.log` are kept. Nothing was staged.\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\n### `udp_probe.clj`\\n\\n1. **Platform selection.** The platform is `jolt.socket.native/os`, the same value jolt's socket layer and sockaddr helpers branch on (`:macos`, `:linux`, `:windows`, derived inside `native.clj`). The probe never reads `os.name` itself. The constants are one table per platform:\\n\\n| constant | `:macos` (MacOSX.sdk, as before) | `:linux` (shiva headers, orchestrator-verified) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| SOCK_DGRAM / IPPROTO_UDP | 2 / 17 | 2 / 17 |\\n| SO_SNDBUF / SO_RCVBUF | 0x1001 / 0x1002 | 7 / 8 |\\n| MSG_TRUNC (unused) | 0x10 | 0x20 |\\n| EAGAIN = EWOULDBLOCK | 35 | 11 |\\n| EMSGSIZE | 40 | 90 |\\n| ENOBUFS | 55 | 105 |\\n| EBADF | 9 | 9 |\\n\\n   Any other platform throws at load. The header line prints `platform=`, the `os.name`/`os.arch` the shim gives (possibly nil on Linux) and the whole constant table. The sockaddr layout (macOS `sin_len` byte versus Linux 16-bit family) is already handled per platform by `native/make-sockaddr` and `sockaddr-*`, so nothing in the probe changed for it.\\n2. **Constants flow through everywhere:**\\n   - send-error classification: EAGAIN/ENOBUFS give `:would-block`, EMSGSIZE gives `:emsgsize`;\\n   - recv `:would-block`;\\n   - the injected-errno cases use the platform's EAGAIN and ENOBUFS (11 and 105 on Linux), and stay labelled `[injected]`;\\n   - the EBADF check after close;\\n   - the label of the empty-receive check now prints the platform's errno (still reads \\\"errno 35\\\" on macOS).\\n3. **The platform maximum is measured, not assumed.** The bisection over [1, 65507] was already measured, and it is unchanged:\\n   - macOS stops at 9216 (`maxdgram` = SO_SNDBUF);\\n   - on Linux it should reach 65507, the IPv4 UDP payload limit.\\n\\n   The EMSGSIZE-above-max check uses the platform's EMSGSIZE. The \\\"configured larger N (8192) below the observed max\\\" check asserts `(< 1200 8192 max-ok)` against the measured value on both platforms.\\n4. **SO_SNDBUF read-back.**\\n   - **macOS (unchanged):** the requested values 2048 and 9216 are read back as RECORDs only, to keep the 36/3 counts.\\n   - **Linux (one extra assertion):** `[linux] SO_SNDBUF read-back follows socket(7): >= 2 x requested`. It checks that both setsockopt calls answer 0 and that the read-back values are at least 4096 and at least 18432. Linux doubles the request for bookkeeping, clamps it at `net.core.wmem_max` (212992 on shiva, above both requests) and floors it at `SOCK_MIN_SNDBUF`. I chose \\\"≥ 2×\\\" so a floor above 2×2048 still passes, never equality.\\n   - The RECORD lines still show the raw values on both platforms.\\n5. **Natural would-block stays a RECORD** on both platforms (20000 × 1024-byte sends with no reader, histogram plus delivered count). Whatever Linux does is recorded, not asserted.\\n6. **Comments:** sendto all-or-nothing now says (macOS, Linux); socklen_t is 32-bit unsigned on both.\\n\\n**Expected Linux totals: 37 assertions, 3 records** (the one extra Linux check).\\n\\n### `run.sh` (POSIX sh; checked with `sh -n` and run under `/bin/dash`)\\n\\n- **jolt binary:** `$JOLT`, else `build/tools/jolt/bin/jolt` (J0a layout), else `build/jolt-spike/jolt/bin/jolt` (spike layout). The probe path comes from the script's own directory, so it can be run from any checkout root.\\n- **`JOLT_CHEZ`:**\\n  1. env;\\n  2. else source `build/tools/jolt-env.sh` when present (J0a layout);\\n  3. else the mise 10.4.1 path if it exists;\\n  4. else unset, and jolt searches PATH (a line says so).\\n- **Prints:** the jolt path, jolt SHA and dirty count (git -C the jolt home), and the Chez version. Then, per OS:\\n  - **Darwin:** `uname -sm`, `sw_vers`, sysctls `net.inet.udp.maxdgram`, `net.inet.udp.recvspace`, `kern.ipc.maxsockbuf`, and the lo0 MTU.\\n  - **Linux:** `uname -sm`, the `/etc/os-release` PRETTY_NAME, `/proc/sys/net/core/{wmem_default,wmem_max,rmem_default,rmem_max}`, `/proc/sys/net/ipv4/{udp_wmem_min,udp_rmem_min,udp_mem}`, and `/sys/class/net/lo/mtu`.\\n\\n  No macOS-only command runs on Linux, and every probe of the environment tolerates a missing file.\\n- **Ends with** `exit: N`.\\n\\n## macOS evidence (foreground, this Mac, jolt 96d7b866, Chez 10.4.1)\\n\\n| run | how | result |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `run-7.log`, `run-8.log`, `run-9.log` | `build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh` from the repo root (spike layout default) | exit 0, `SUMMARY 36 assertions: 36 pass, 0 fail; 3 records` |\\n| `run-10-dash-j0a.log` | `JOLT=…/jolt-j0a/build/tools/jolt/bin/jolt dash build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh` | exit 0, 36/0/3 |\\n| `run-11-j0a-default.log` | from the jolt-j0a worktree root with `JOLT`/`JOLT_CHEZ` unset (exercises the `build/tools/jolt` + `jolt-env.sh` default; that worktree stayed clean) | exit 0, 36/0/3 |\\n\\n- **Header** (`run-7.log`):\\n  - `platform= :macos` with constants `{:so-sndbuf 4097 … :eagain 35 :emsgsize 40 :enobufs 55 :ebadf 9}` (0x1001 = 4097);\\n  - sysctls `9216 786896 8388608`, lo0 MTU 16384;\\n  - max sendable 9216, EMSGSIZE errno 40.\\n- **Labels:** the PASS/FAIL/RECORD label sequence of `run-7.log` is identical to `run-6.log` (diff empty).\\n\\n## Linux x86_64 expectations, all UNTESTED (for the shiva run)\\n\\n1. **Platform and constants:**\\n   - `native/os` answers `:linux` and the header shows the Linux constant table. If the shim's nil `os.name` made `native/os` fall to `:linux` on shiva, that is the right answer there.\\n   - Bind, readback and sockaddr decoding work through the Linux sockaddr layout. This is jolt's code path and is unexercised by me.\\n2. **Packet semantics (item 1):** the same assertions as macOS, with EAGAIN 11 on the empty receive.\\n3. **Platform maximum (item 2):**\\n   - The bisection should find **65507**, and 65508 should give EMSGSIZE errno **90** with nothing delivered.\\n   - Big sends land in a 212992-byte receive buffer, which may drop some of the bisection's packets. The drain counts them; that is not asserted.\\n   - Item 2 for N=1200 and N=8192 should pass unchanged (8192 < 65507).\\n4. **SO_SNDBUF:** the read-back for 2048 is expected around 4608 (the doubled request, possibly floored at SOCK_MIN_SNDBUF), and 18432 for 9216. The `[linux]` assertion requires only ≥ 2× the request.\\n5. **Natural would-block (RECORD):** Linux may or may not produce EAGAIN/ENOBUFS when blasting with a small SO_SNDBUF. Loopback frees the send buffer on delivery, so I expect `{:sent 20000}` again with silent receiver drops, but it is unknown.\\n6. **Injected cases:** EAGAIN 11 and ENOBUFS 105 should give `:would-block` with no packet accepted.\\n7. **Poller and fibers (item 4):** this runs on jolt's epoll backend, not kqueue. The parked waiter, wakeup, `cancel!`, `forget!`, close then EBADF 9, and the empty poller table are all unexercised on Linux by me.\\n8. **`run.sh` on dash/Ubuntu:** the `/proc` and `/sys` reads and `/etc/os-release` parsing are untested on Linux. They were only shown not to run on Darwin. The script did run under `/bin/dash` on macOS.\\n9. **Toolchain on shiva:** the J0a provisioning of Chez 10.4.1 and the jolt checkout is a precondition. `run.sh` uses `build/tools/jolt-env.sh` if bootstrap has run there.\\n\\n**Expected Linux total: 37 assertions + 3 records.** Any FAIL there is a real finding to report, not something to adjust the probe to.\\n\\nReport: `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792485587000-engineer-jolt-j0b-linux-port.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md`\\n\\n\"}]}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"tool\",\"messageId\":\"3ab3638d-8008-4a18-8a1d-4f98a1baef40\"}},{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Here I'll read the probe itself and the shiva log, plus one macOS log for label comparison. The probe is potentially large. Let me try reading udp_probe.clj along with shiva log 1 and run-7.log.\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Now the probe source and the logs (one macOS, one shiva, plus the shiva J0a logs).\\n\\n\"},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_8cf92d1e7ce84f0ba50b73f9\",\"name\":\"read_file\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/build/jolt-spike/j0b/udp_probe.clj\"}}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"model\",\"createdAt\":1792487466617,\"messageId\":\"d5fbd557-18f2-4a85-922f-bd5916fc8ce8\"}},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"tool_use_id\":\"call_8cf92d1e7ce84f0ba50b73f9\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Read 1/1 file, 355 lines\\n1: ;; J0b: raw nonblocking SOCK_DGRAM over jolt.ffi on macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64.\\n2: ;; Probe only: no datagram/DHT conformance. Run from the repo root with\\n3: ;;   build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh\\n4: (ns udp-probe\\n5:   (:require [jolt.ffi :as ffi]\\n6:             [jolt.socket.native :as native]\\n7:             [jolt.io-poller :as poller]\\n8:             [jolt.fibers :as fibers]\\n9:             [clojure.string :as str]))\\n10: \\n11: ;; -- constants per platform ---------------------------------------------------\\n12: ;; The platform is jolt.socket.native/os (:macos, :linux, :windows), the same\\n13: ;; selection jolt's socket layer and sockaddr helpers use; os.name is never\\n14: ;; read here (jolt's System/getProperty shim can answer nil).\\n15: ;;\\n16: ;; :macos from MacOSX.sdk: sys/socket.h:112,155,156,574; netinet/in.h:121;\\n17: ;;   sys/errno.h:96,129-130,137,170.\\n18: ;; :linux x86_64 from shiva's headers (asm-generic/errno-base.h, errno.h,\\n19: ;;   x86_64-linux-gnu/asm/socket.h, bits/socket.h), checked by the\\n20: ;;   orchestrator: EAGAIN = EWOULDBLOCK = 11.\\n21: (def platform native/os)\\n22: \\n23: (def consts\\n24:   (case platform\\n25:     :macos {:sock-dgram 2 :ipproto-udp 17 :so-sndbuf 0x1001 :so-rcvbuf 0x1002\\n26:             :msg-trunc 0x10 :eagain 35 :emsgsize 40 :enobufs 55 :ebadf 9}\\n27:     :linux {:sock-dgram 2 :ipproto-udp 17 :so-sndbuf 7 :so-rcvbuf 8\\n28:             :msg-trunc 0x20 :eagain 11 :emsgsize 90 :enobufs 105 :ebadf 9}\\n29:     (throw (ex-info \\\"J0b probe: unsupported platform\\\" {:platform platform}))))\\n30: \\n31: (def SOCK-DGRAM (:sock-dgram consts))\\n32: (def IPPROTO-UDP (:ipproto-udp consts))\\n33: (def SO-SNDBUF (:so-sndbuf consts))\\n34: (def SO-RCVBUF (:so-rcvbuf consts))\\n35: (def MSG-TRUNC (:msg-trunc consts)) ; not used: N+1 sentinel instead\\n36: (def EAGAIN (:eagain consts))\\n37: (def EMSGSIZE (:emsgsize consts))\\n38: (def ENOBUFS (:enobufs consts))\\n39: (def EBADF (:ebadf consts))\\n40: \\n41: ;; ssize_t sendto(int, const void*, size_t, int, const struct sockaddr*, socklen_t)\\n42: ;; ssize_t recvfrom(int, void*, size_t, int, struct sockaddr*, socklen_t*)\\n43: ;; socklen_t is 32-bit unsigned on both (arm/_types.h:117; glibc\\n44: ;; __socklen_t is __U32_TYPE). Not :blocking: the fd is\\n45: ;; nonblocking, so neither call can park the carrier.\\n46: (ffi/defcfn c-sendto \\\"sendto\\\" [:int :pointer :size_t :int :pointer :uint] :ssize_t\\n47:   {:capture-native-error true})\\n48: (ffi/defcfn c-recvfrom \\\"recvfrom\\\" [:int :pointer :size_t :int :pointer :pointer] :ssize_t\\n49:   {:capture-native-error true})\\n50: \\n51: ;; -- injection seam -------------------------------------------------------------\\n52: ;; The probe's sendto goes through this var. Injected runs rebind it and every\\n53: ;; observation they produce is tagged :injected, never :natural.\\n54: (def ^:dynamic *sendto* c-sendto)\\n55: \\n56: (defn- classify-send-error [e]\\n57:   (cond (or (= e EAGAIN) (= e ENOBUFS)) :would-block\\n58:         (= e EMSGSIZE) :emsgsize\\n59:         :else :error))\\n60: \\n61: ;; -- raw surface ------------------------------------------------------------------\\n62: (defn udp-open\\n63:   \\\"A nonblocking, close-on-exec SOCK_DGRAM fd bound to 127.0.0.1:0.\\\"\\n64:   []\\n65:   (let [[fd e] (native/c-socket native/af-inet SOCK-DGRAM IPPROTO-UDP)]\\n66:     (when (neg? fd) (throw (ex-info \\\"socket failed\\\" {:errno e})))\\n67:     (native/close-on-exec! fd)\\n68:     (let [[r e] (native/set-blocking! fd false)]\\n69:       (when (neg? r) (throw (ex-info \\\"O_NONBLOCK failed\\\" {:errno e}))))\\n70:     (let [[sa len] (native/make-sockaddr native/af-inet \\\"127.0.0.1\\\" 0)]\\n71:       (try\\n72:         (let [[r e] (native/c-bind fd sa len)]\\n73:           (when (neg? r) (throw (ex-info \\\"bind failed\\\" {:errno e}))))\\n74:         (finally (ffi/free sa))))\\n75:     fd))\\n76: \\n77: (defn send!\\n78:   \\\"One sendto of the whole byte array to addr ({:ip :port}). Answers\\n79:    {:result :sent :n} | {:result :would-block :errno} | {:result :emsgsize :errno}\\n80:    | {:result :error :errno} | {:result :short-send :n :len}. A short send is an\\n81:    error and is never completed by a second packet.\\\"\\n82:   [fd ^bytes payload {:keys [ip port]}]\\n83:   (let [len (alength payload)\\n84:         buf (ffi/alloc (max 1 len))\\n85:         [sa salen] (native/make-sockaddr native/af-inet ip port)]\\n86:     (try\\n87:       (when (pos? len) (ffi/write-array buf payload))\\n88:       (let [[n e] (*sendto* fd buf len 0 sa salen)]\\n89:         (cond\\n90:           (neg? n) {:result (classify-send-error e) :errno e}\\n91:           (not= n len) {:result :short-send :n n :len len}\\n92:           :else {:result :sent :n n}))\\n93:       (finally (ffi/free buf) (ffi/free sa)))))\\n94: \\n95: (defn recv!\\n96:   \\\"One recvfrom with a buffer of limit+1 bytes. Answers\\n97:    {:result :packet :bytes :from} | {:result :oversize :received :limit :from}\\n98:    | {:result :would-block :errno} | {:result :error :errno}. A packet longer\\n99:    than limit is an error; its prefix is never returned.\\\"\\n100:   [fd limit]\\n101:   (let [cap (inc limit)\\n102:         buf (ffi/alloc cap)\\n103:         [sa lenp] (native/alloc-sockaddr)]\\n104:     (try\\n105:       (let [[n e] (c-recvfrom fd buf cap 0 sa lenp)]\\n106:         (cond\\n107:           (neg? n) {:result (if (= e EAGAIN) :would-block :error) :errno e}\\n108:           :else\\n109:           (let [from {:ip (native/sockaddr-ip sa) :port (native/sockaddr-port sa)}]\\n110:             (if (> n limit)\\n111:               {:result :oversize :received n :limit limit :from from}\\n112:               {:result :packet :bytes (if (zero? n) (byte-array 0) (ffi/read-array buf n))\\n113:                :from from}))))\\n114:       (finally (ffi/free buf) (ffi/free sa) (ffi/free lenp)))))\\n115: \\n116: (defn local [fd] (native/local-address fd))\\n117: \\n118: (defn close! [fd]\\n119:   (poller/cancel! fd)\\n120:   (poller/forget! fd)\\n121:   (native/c-close fd))\\n122: \\n123: ;; -- harness ------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n124: (def results (atom []))\\n125: \\n126: (defn check [item label ok? detail]\\n127:   (swap! results conj {:item item :label label :ok? (boolean ok?) :detail detail})\\n128:   (println (if ok? \\\"PASS\\\" \\\"FAIL\\\") item label (pr-str detail)))\\n129: \\n130: (defn record\\n131:   \\\"An observation that cannot fail: printed as RECORD, never counted as PASS.\\\"\\n132:   [item label detail]\\n133:   (swap! results conj {:item item :label label :record? true :detail detail})\\n134:   (println \\\"RECORD\\\" item label (pr-str detail)))\\n135: \\n136: (defn drain!\\n137:   \\\"recv! until :would-block; answers the packet count. A :error result is a\\n138:    FAIL and stops the drain instead of looping on it.\\\"\\n139:   [item fd limit]\\n140:   (loop [n 0]\\n141:     (let [r (recv! fd limit)]\\n142:       (case (:result r)\\n143:         :would-block n\\n144:         :error (do (check item \\\"drain stopped on recv error\\\" false r) n)\\n145:         (recur (inc n))))))\\n146: \\n147: (defn ->bytes [xs] (byte-array (map unchecked-byte xs)))\\n148: (defn pattern [n seed] (->bytes (map #(bit-and (+ seed (* 31 %)) 0xff) (range n))))\\n149: \\n150: (defn recv-wait\\n151:   \\\"recv! until a non-would-block result, waiting on the poller between tries.\\n152:    Counts the wakeups. Thread or fiber, whichever the caller is.\\\"\\n153:   [fd limit]\\n154:   (loop [waits 0]\\n155:     (let [r (recv! fd limit)]\\n156:       (if (= :would-block (:result r))\\n157:         (do (poller/wait-ready fd :read) (recur (inc waits)))\\n158:         (assoc r :waits waits)))))\\n159: \\n160: (defn same-bytes? [a b] (= (vec a) (vec b)))\\n161: \\n162: ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n163: ;; Item 1: bind, readback, whole-packet semantics\\n164: (defn item-1 [a b N]\\n165:   (let [la (local a) lb (local b)\\n166:         to-b {:ip \\\"127.0.0.1\\\" :port (:port lb)}]\\n167:     (check 1 \\\"bound ports read back\\\" (and (pos? (:port la)) (pos? (:port lb))\\n168:                                           (not= (:port la) (:port lb)))\\n169:            {:a la :b lb})\\n170:     (check 1 \\\"empty socket answers :would-block\\\" (= :would-block (:result (recv! b N)))\\n171:            (recv! b N))\\n172:     (doseq [[label payload] [[\\\"zero-length\\\" (byte-array 0)]\\n173:                              [\\\"embedded-NUL\\\" (->bytes [0 1 0 0 255 0 65 0])]\\n174:                              [\\\"limit-sized N\\\" (pattern N 7)]]]\\n175:       (let [s (send! a payload to-b)\\n176:             r (recv-wait b N)]\\n177:         (check 1 (str label \\\" round-trip\\\")\\n178:                (and (= :sent (:result s)) (= (alength payload) (:n s))\\n179:                     (= :packet (:result r)) (same-bytes? payload (:bytes r))\\n180:                     (= (:from r) {:ip \\\"127.0.0.1\\\" :port (:port la)}))\\n181:                {:len (alength payload) :send s :recv (dissoc r :bytes)\\n182:                 :recv-len (some-> (:bytes r) alength)})))\\n183:     ;; distinct consecutive packets: each receive consumes exactly one\\n184:     (let [ps (mapv #(pattern (+ 10 %) %) (range 5))\\n185:           sends (mapv #(send! a % to-b) ps)\\n186:           recvs (vec (repeatedly 5 #(recv-wait b N)))\\n187:           after (recv! b N)]\\n188:       (check 1 \\\"5 consecutive packets, one per receive, in order, then empty\\\"\\n189:              (and (every? #(= :sent (:result %)) sends)\\n190:                   (= (mapv vec ps) (mapv (comp vec :bytes) recvs))\\n191:                   (= :would-block (:result after)))\\n192:              {:lens (mapv alength ps) :recv-lens (mapv (comp alength :bytes) recvs)\\n193:               :after after}))\\n194:     ;; short send: sendto on SOCK_DGRAM is all-or-nothing (macOS, Linux); a short\\n195:     ;; count cannot be produced naturally, so inject one through the seam.\\n196:     (let [p (pattern 100 3)\\n197:           s (binding [*sendto* (fn [fd buf len fl sa sl] [(dec len) 0])]\\n198:               (send! a p to-b))\\n199:       \n... 135146341 bytes omitted ...\nhex digits: utf8-byte-length of content — content string could exceed 4GB? No. Fine.\\n\\n18. **framed length**: `(to-hex (utf8-byte-length content) 8)` — length is the UTF-8 byte length of content. But content may include raw non-ASCII characters (strings embedded as-is). utf8-byte-length counts code units properly including surrogate pairs → 4. Good. But content of `ident-content` contains hex strings + raw ns/name — counted. Good.\\n\\n    Cross-host: JVM's utf8-byte-length via .charAt (UTF-16 code units); CLJS's .charCodeAt (UTF-16); CLJD's .codeUnitAt — does Dart's String.codeUnitAt return UTF-16 code units? Dart strings are UTF-16 internally; codeUnitAt returns UTF-16 code units. Good.\\n\\n    And jing/sha256 of the mixed string — does it hash UTF-8 encoding? Need to check dao.jing/sha256 on each host: does JVM use .getBytes UTF-8? If any host hashed differently (e.g., UTF-16), golden hashes would differ across lanes; the orchestrator says all lanes pass with pinned hashes → empirically consistent. Fine.\\n\\n19. **encode-scalar for descriptor**: descriptor contains `:dim/arity (count opcode-table)` = 22. Also :dim/slots opcode-table map keyed by mnemonic keywords with values of vector pairs. Map is sorted by encoded bytes of [k v] — deterministic. Good.\\n\\n    Descriptor contains strings with spaces/parentheses; fine.\\n\\n    Note: descriptor-hash is a def computed at load time: encode-scalar descriptor must succeed on all hosts — descriptor contains only common-domain values. Good.\\n\\n20. **mnemonic-tag**: sorted mnemonics — `(vec (sort mnemonics))` — cross-host keyword sort: compare… keyword comparison is name-based; deterministic across hosts? Keyword's compare uses namespace/name strings — consistent. Golden bytes pin mnemonic tags: :const=03? sort order: :branch-false, :call, :closure, :const, … let me check: sorted keywords: branch-false(00), call(01), closure(02), const(03), current-continuation(04), ffi-call(05), gensym(06), halt(07), jump(08), load-bound(09), load-free(0a), park(0b), push(0c), resume(0d), return(0e), store-get(0f), store-put(10), stream-close(11), stream-cursor(12), stream-make(13), stream-next(14), stream-put(15).\\n\\n    Golden checks: :const → \\\"03\\\" ✓. :closure → \\\"02\\\" ✓ (:closure-bound goldens begin \\\"0201...\\\"). :return → \\\"0e\\\" ✓. :load-bound → \\\"09\\\": golden's :closure-bound contains \\\"09000000000000000000000000000000000e\\\" — load-bound depth 0 position 0: 09 + 8 bytes 0 + 8 bytes 0 + 0e ✓. :load-free → \\\"0a\\\": golden \\\"0a0900000015...\\\" — 0a then :sym operand: encode-scalar of 'x → tag 09 (symbol is index 9 in scalar-classes: nil00 bool01 long02 double03 ratio04 bigint05 char06 string07 keyword08 symbol09 vector0a list0b map0c set0d) → \\\"09\\\" + length 00000015 (=21 bytes: ident-content = framed:nil \\\"\\\" (00+00000000) 10 hex chars… wait: framed :nil \\\"\\\" = \\\"00\\\" + \\\"00000000\\\" + \\\"\\\" = 10 chars; framed :string \\\"x\\\" = \\\"07\\\"+\\\"00000001\\\"+\\\"x\\\" = 11 chars; total 21 = 0x15 ✓). Golden has \\\"0a090000001500000000000700000001x0e\\\" ✓.\\n\\n    And common-domain fixtures: nil → \\\"0000000000\\\" (tag 00 + length 0 + \\\"\\\") ✓. true → \\\"01\\\"+\\\"00000002\\\"+\\\"01\\\" ✓. 42 → \\\"02\\\"+\\\"00000010\\\"+ 8-byte hex LE of 42 = \\\"2a00000000000000\\\" ✓. \\\"hello\\\" → \\\"07\\\"+\\\"00000005\\\"+\\\"hello\\\" ✓. :a/b → tag 08, content = framed:string \\\"a\\\" (07 00000001 a) + framed:string \\\"b\\\" = 11+11=22=0x16 ✓ \\\"08000000160700000001a0700000001b\\\" ✓. 'x/y → 09 ✓. [1 2] → tag 0a, content: encode 1 = \\\"02\\\"+\\\"00000010\\\"+\\\"0100000000000000\\\"=26 chars each → 52=0x34 ✓. #{1 2} → 0d + same ✓.\\n\\n    Golden :const-long: \\\"03\\\" + \\\"02000000102a0000000000000000\\\" + \\\"0e\\\" — wait expected \\\"0302000000102a000000000000000e\\\": 03, then 02 00000010 2a00000000000000 (16 hex = 8 bytes), then 0e. ✓.\\n\\n    :closure-bound goldens: \\\"02\\\" + arity 1 → int64-le \\\"0100000000000000\\\" + body 2 → \\\"0200000000000000\\\" + \\\"0e\\\" (return) + \\\"09\\\" + depth0 + pos0 + \\\"0e\\\". Expected string: \\\"02010000000000000002000000000000000e09000000000000000000000000000000000e\\\" ✓.\\n\\n21. **Tests**:\\n    - Precomposed vs decomposed é: line 95-96 — are they actually different strings? `(const-return \\\"é\\\")` twice — the test claims `composed` and `decomposed` — need to check raw bytes of test file to verify one is NFC and other is NFD. In the read, both visually show \\\"é\\\". Check bytes.\\n    - e-acute test relies on strings not being = as data and hashing distinctly. Fine if truly composed/decomposed in file.\\n    - Golden fixture proof-of-load: docstring claims manually verified during development by reordering scalar-classes. The test pins encode-image bytes and image-hash literals — any change in tag order, framing, or table shape shifts bytes → failure. That's a real proof (pinned literals independent of implementation). ✓.\\n    - Missing tests? B1 box requires: \\\"invalid rows, out-of-range bound operands, strict spelling preservation, distinct hashes for precomposed and decomposed e-acute, ratio fixtures either encoded or rejected, distinct hashes for 1/1.0 on JVM and Dart, distinct hashes for ratios and chars on JVM, identical bytes across hosts in the common scalar domain. Golden image bytes and H values for a frozen corpus checked across all three host lanes.\\\"\\n      - Rejection with :unsupported-value: is there a test that rejects unsupported values (e.g., a function value, unpaired surrogates)? I don't see one! No test for rejection of unpaired surrogates, no test for :unsupported-value rejection of out-of-domain values. Test file has host-boundary-refusal tests checking support sets and JVM acceptance, but no rejection test (CLJS/CLJD can't construct ratio values… but can construct unpaired surrogates! `(str (js/String.fromCharCode 0xD800))` or JVM \\\"\\\\uD800\\\"). B1 box mentions the design (S2) \\\"rejected as :unsupported-value\\\" but the completion criteria list \\\"ratio fixture either encoded or rejected\\\" — on JVM encode; CLJS/CLJD rejection can't be tested without reader-safe values… but unpaired surrogates ARE testable on all hosts and are in completion criteria… box wording: \\\"invalid rows, out-of-range bound operands, strict spelling preservation, distinct hashes for precomposed/decomposed e-acute, ratio fixture…, distinct hashes for 1/1.0…, distinct hashes for ratios and chars on JVM, identical bytes across hosts in the common scalar domain. Golden image bytes and H values for a frozen corpus checked across all three host lanes.\\\" Unpaired surrogate rejection isn't explicitly in the box, but is a design S2 requirement. No test exercises the :unsupported-value throw path of encode-scalar at all (not even on JVM with an out-of-domain value like a function or object). That's a P2 test hole: the rejection path is untested. Also the map/set collision throw untested. Also: does scalar-class correctly reject records? `(record? v)` guard — but on JVM records satisfy map? → excluded → nil. Fine.\\n      - \\\"Identical bytes across hosts\\\" — pinned literals run on each lane; good.\\n      - Also no test that encode-scalar rejects values with unpaired surrogates in *keyword/symbol* names — ident-parts-ok? covers.\\n    - one-vs-one-point-zero test uses `#?(:cljs nil :default ...)` — includes CLJD. On CLJD, `1.0` literal — does Dart's reader produce double; `(dc/scalar-class 1.0)` → host-long? = (int? 1.0) → Dart's `1.0 is int` is false on native → :double ✓. `(not= (encode-scalar 1) (encode-scalar 1.0))` ✓.\\n\\n22. **CLJS integral-double rule on decode/receive**: covered above — deferred and documented. Design says CLJS receivers reject as unsupported-value; B1 file says receive paths are B6+. Accept with P3 note.\\n\\n23. **`host-double?` docstring confusion**: \\\"all JS numbers already classified as :long by the safe-integer check itself are never reconsidered here\\\" — implementation: (and (number? v) (not (host-long? v))...). On CLJS, integral but unsafe (e.g., 2^53+… or 1e300) → not safe integer → :double. 1.5 → :double. Correct per design (\\\"long if safe integer, else double\\\"). Integral double within safe range → :long (design: those are outside the CL