n\\nThe shared-body arity conflict gap identified earlier: `[[:closure 2 3] [:closure 1 3] [:return] [:load-bound 0 1] [:return]]` — wait, pc2 is [:return] and pc3..4 is the body. Root walk: successors of pc0 = [1], pc1 = [2], pc2 return → []. Nested collected: pc0 → [3 '(2)], pc1 → [3 '(1)]. Queue: [[3 '(2)] [3 '(1)]]. Walk 3 with chain (2): owner[3]=(2), owner[4]=(2). Then [3 '(1)] skipped because owner contains 3. scope-defect at pc3: depth 0, position 1, chain (2) → 1 < 2 OK → image VALID. But closure at pc1 declares arity 1 for same body → when invoked through that closure, position 1 is out of range. Malformed image passes → P1 per the rubric (\\\"validator gap letting malformed image through\\\"). Design S3: \\\"B1's image validator iterates this check by walking each body's declared arity and chain of enclosing bodies.\\\" — \\\"each body's declared arity\\\" singular assumes one arity per body; conflicting declarations should be a defect. Also which chain wins depends on BFS order — nondeterministic w.r.t. image content? Deterministic given an image but arbitrary.\\n\\nAlso cross-body jump variant: body B entered from two different chains via jumps.\\n\\nIs this reachable from B2's lowerer? No — lowerer emits fresh bodies. But the validator's role is precisely to guard against hand-built / foreign images (B6 receives images from the network!). A malicious/buggy producer could send an image with conflicting arity declarations; validator accepts; B3's VM does positional lookup → out-of-range or nil read at runtime. That's exactly the threat model. P1.\\n\\nMinimal fix: in all-body-chains, when `(contains? owner entry)` and the recorded chain ≠ entry's chain, emit a defect (e.g. {:rule :scope-conflict :pc entry}) or return a marker; alternatively validate each :load-bound against every chain that reaches its pc (collect a set of chains per pc; require position/depth valid under all chains). Simplest: track declared chains per entry pc; conflict → defect. Also cross-body jumps into a walked pc from a different chain: walk-body skips already-owned pcs, so a jump into another body doesn't propagate the second chain. Full fix requires chain-set propagation with conflict detection. Minimal fix wording: make owner values a chain-set, propagate on new chains, and check every :load-bound against every chain in the set; or reject images where a body-entry pc is declared with differing chains / where control crosses body boundaries. Hmm — \\\"control crosses body boundaries\\\" is hard to define without body ranges. Chain-set propagation is cleanest.\\n\\nActually wait, let me double-check: does walk-body propagate chains across jumps into already-owned pcs? No: `(contains? owner pc)` → skip. So second chain from a jump is discarded. Example: `[[:closure 1 4] [:return] [:jump 3]?? ...` — construct: root pc0 closure(arity1, body4), pc1 return… let me not over-engineer; one concrete scenario suffices, mention the jump variant as the same defect class.\\n\\n**image-scalar-classes**: scans only :const operands. But scalar values can also appear in :store-get/:store-put key/value (:data kind)! Design says \\\"scalar classes used by an image are derived from tags of its hashed :const operands\\\". Design itself says :const only. Hmm — S2: \\\"The scalar classes used by an image are derived from tags of its hashed `:const` operands.\\\" OK, design says :const only; store keys… :store-get [:key :data] can carry a ratio on JVM. A receiver scanning only :const would miss the ratio class in a store key → not rejected → decode would… but B1 has no decode. Follows design as written ✓. Don't flag (design's own decision; maybe note as P3 that store-put's value :data escapes class scan — but design explicitly says :const; the encoder throws :unsupported-value anyway on JVM for out-of-domain; cross-host: JVM image with ratio in store-put value sent to CLJS — image-scalar-classes misses it, validate-host-support passes, but B6's decode would… B6's problem/design gap, arguably out of scope for B1. Mention as a P3 finding.)\\n\\n**Descriptor encoding check**: descriptor `:dim/encoding` includes strings with spaces — fine.\\n\\n**Now let me verify é composed at line 95.**\\n\\n**Also verify that the test's `#?(:clj ...)` hazard pattern matches the repo precedent** — check other test files for `#?(:clj` with `:default nil` and see whether the Dart lane compiles them (existing precedent). Grep tests.\\n\\n**Also verify no references to yin.vm.debruijn private vars**: requires are only clojure.set, dao.jing, yin.vm, yin.vm.code ✓. All qualified calls: jing/sha256 (public ✓), vm/plain-data? (public ✓), code/mnemonics, code/vector-operand-table, code/terminators (public ✓). No `yin.vm.debruijn/` calls anywhere ✓ (I didn't see any in the files — only comments). Let me grep to confirm.\\n\\n**CLJS `(long v)` in int64-le-hex :default?** No, CLJS has its own branch ✓.\\n\\n**`case` in encode-scalar with keyword keys** — case compares with =; keyword is fine on all hosts ✓. Note: `case (scalar-class v)` when scalar-class returns nil → falls to default clause (throw) ✓. But wait: case's default is the last expression `(throw ...)` ✓.\\n\\n**`(str v)` for :bigint on CLJS/CLJD** — unreachable (host-bigint? false), fine; but case still compiles `(str v)` — resolves everywhere ✓ (no host-specific symbols in bigint branch ✓; ratio branch guarded by reader conditional because numerator/denominator don't resolve on cljd ✓ — good catch by the implementer).\\n\\nHmm wait — on CLJS, numerator/denominator exist in cljs.core (ratio emulation)? cljs.core has numerator/denominator that throw on non-ratio. Anyway, guarded.\\n\\n**On CLJD `(ratio? v)`** — guarded ✓. `(char? v)` on cljd — cljd has char? but not trustworthy; guarded to false ✓.\\n\\n**host-long? :cljd (int? v)** — Dart's int? ✓ matches debruijn's numeric-class :cljd branch `(if (int? v) :int64 ...)` ✓.\\n\\n**Dart's int is 64-bit** ✓ int64-le-hex (long v) — CLJD's `long`? D