y... deftest registers a test; the body runs when the test runs, not at macro-expansion. On Dart, body = nil → skipped ✓. On host-eval (JVM), the deftest macro... whatever. Empirically passes. OK: the nested-conditional pattern works (skipped) for Dart emission, deviating from the documented guard style of the README. P3.\\n\\n4. **BigDecimal/Float folding on JVM** — P2, as analyzed.\\n\\n5. **`framed :string` for :str operand bypasses encode-operand, well-formed-utf16? guard** — P1? Design: \\\"unpaired UTF-16 surrogates are rejected with :unsupported-value, just as the projection's UTF-16 guard does.\\\" An image `[[:gensym \\\"\\\\uD800\\\"] [:return]]` passes image-defect (validator :str check = string?) and gets encoded by image-hash without rejection → host-divergent bytes (JVM's sha256 UTF-8-encodes lone surrogates as '?' (0x3F) via getBytes, CLJS's stringToUtf8ByteArray → replacement char 0xEF 0xBF 0xBD (I think), Dart's utf8.encode → throws or replacement). So the same image → different H per host, silent fork of identity — exactly what this design forbids (\\\"byte identity across hosts is required\\\"; \\\"not silently normalized\\\"). This is a genuine P1: bytes wrong / rejection missing. Location: debruijn_code.cljc:482 (`:str (framed :string (or x \\\"\\\"))`). Fix: `:str (if (nil? x) (framed :string \\\"\\\") (encode-scalar x))` — wait, encode-scalar on a string returns framed :string with the guard ✓; or add an explicit well-formed-utf16? check and throw :unsupported-value. Note: :str nil → \\\"\\\" is fine.\\n\\nHmm wait — double-check: is there any other path where a string bypasses the guard? encode-operand :data → encode-scalar (with guard) ✓. :const operand value → encode-scalar ✓. Only :str. Also :bool is fine. ✓ One site.\\n\\nAlso **validator** doesn't reject unpaired surrogates in :str — the design says the encoder rejects, but the validator lets such an image through image-defect, and image-hash then... with my fix, image-hash would THROW (uncaught ex-info) rather than return a refusal. That's what encode-scalar does for out-of-domain ✓ consistent.\\n\\n6. **Arity collision on shared body in scope validation** — P1 (validator gap letting a malformed image through). Concrete example: `[[:closure 2 3] [:closure 1 3] [:return] [:load-bound 0 1] [:return]]` accepted; at runtime, the arity-1 closure reads position 1 of a 1-slot frame. Also the first-wins chain via `all-body-chains` (around lines 724–730) and the `(contains? owner pc)` skip in `walk-body` (line 703).\\n\\nWait, actually let me re-verify the image validity trace. Vector: pc0 [:closure 2 3], pc1 [:closure 1 3], pc2 [:return], pc3 [:load-bound 0 1], pc4 [:return].\\n\\nwell-formed: mnemonic ✓; arity ✓ (closure 3 elements, load-bound 3, return 1); operand-kind: closure [arity :uint → 2 ✓, body :pc → target-bounds]; load-bound [0 :uint ✓, 1 :uint ✓]; saturation: none; target-bounds: body pc 3 ≤ 4 ✓; terminator: last = :return ✓.\\n\\nscope: all-body-chains: queue [[0 '()]]. walk-body(v 0 '() {}): frontier [0]: pc0 closure → nested [[3 '(2)]]; successors [:closure not terminator] → [1]; owner {0 '()}. pc1: closure → nested [[3 '(2)] [3 '(1)]]; successors [2]; owner{0,1}. pc2: return terminator → []; owner{0,1,2}. frontier empty → {:owner {0 '() 1 '() 2 '()}, :nested [[3 '(2)] [3 '(1)]]}. Queue becomes [[3 '(2)] [3 '(1)]]. walk-body(3 '(2)): pc3 load-bound → successors [4]; owner 3→'(2); pc4 return → owner 4→'(2). Nested: none. Queue [[3 '(1)]] → contains? owner 3 → skip. owner = {0 '(), 1 '(), 2 '(), 3 '(2), 4 '(2)}.\\n\\nscope-defect: pc3 load-bound depth 0 position 1: chain '(2): depth 0 < 1 ✓; position 1 < (nth '(2) 0)=2 ✓ → nil. Image accepted. Gap confirmed.\\n\\nIs such an image \\\"malformed\\\"? Two closures declare the same body with different arities — the design S3 says \\\"walk each body's declared arity and the enclosing body chain\\\" — each body has one declared arity; this image contradicts that and should be rejected. The B2 lowe