t.\\n\\nAlso, let me check the design-document sentence about the descriptor: \\\"That descriptor declares the lowering-contract version, arity, ordered slots, canonical encoding, and one lift morphism from :yin.debruijn.code/* to :yin.code/*\\\" ✓ all five present (lines 122-138).\\n\\nAlso: \\\"Slots with strict spelling are typed as raw Bytes, so no-NFC behavior is a slot rule\\\" — hmm, the descriptor declares :dim/encoding {:nfc false ...}. \\\"Typed as raw Bytes\\\" — this dimension has no Bytes-typed slots; string content is embedded raw. The encoding map declares :nfc false ✓ sufficient.\\n\\nLet me also recheck scalar-class ordering issues on JVM: `(host-char? v)` comes before `(host-ratio? v)` etc. ✓. But the ordering of `(boolean? v)` and `(nil? v)` ✓.\\n\\nCLJS potential issue: does `(host-long? 1)` also need to handle cljs longs via the :long tag? ✓.\\n\\n**Another potential P2: the JVM branch of `int64-le-hex` uses `(long v)` — what if v is an Integer etc. ✓. If v is a huge positive BigInteger that passes the nonneg-int? for a :uint operand — the validator allows it, but encoding throws an unqualified exception. P3.\\n\\n**`(<= 0 x (dec length))`** — x is an integer of any size ✓.\\n\\nAlright, let me consolidate the findings.\\n\\nFindings:\\n\\n**P1-1: `:str` operand bypasses the UTF-16 surrogate guard** — debruijn_code.cljc:482 (`:str (framed :string (or x \\\"\\\"))` inside encode-operand). Design S2: \\\"Unpaired UTF-16 surrogates are rejected with :unsupported-value, just as the projection's UTF-16 guard does.\\\" Scenario: `[[:gensym \\\"\\\\uD800\\\"] [:return]]` passes `image-defect` (the :str check is `(or (nil? x) (string? x))`, line 607), and `image-hash` blithely hashes it; `jing/sha256` UTF-8-encodes unpaired surrogates differently per host (JVM's `.getBytes \\\"UTF-8\\\"` → 0x3F; CLJS's `stringToUtf8ByteArray` → EF BF BD; Dart's `utf8.encode` → throws or replaces) → identical image produces different H per host: a silent identity fork, exactly what S2/B1 forbids. Also, utf8-byte-length counts it as 3 (per the copied docstring, \\\"will be a 3-byte replacement character\\\"), so the length prefix doesn't even match the JVM's actual byte count. Minimal fix: route :str through encode-scalar (`:str (if (nil? x) (framed :string \\\"\\\") (encode-scalar x))`) or add a well-formed-utf16? guard that throws :unsupported-value. Note: the :const path correctly rejects via scalar-class → nil → throw ✓ (lines 355, 442-443).\\n\\n**P1-2: Scope validator accepts one body declared with conflicting arities** — debruijn_code.cljc:724-730 (all-body-chains skips `(contains? owner entry)`) and :703 (walk-body skips owned pcs). Concrete image: `[[:closure 2 3] [:closure 1 3] [:return] [:load-bound 0 1] [:return]]` → `image-defect` = nil (first-walk-wins chain '(2) validates position 1), but the arity-1 closure at pc1 declares the same body pc3 with arity 1; when invoked through it, the frame has 1 slot, and `[:load-bound 0 1]` reads position 1 out of range at runtime. Design S3: \\\"The B1 image validator walks each body's declared arity and its enclosing-body chain, repeating the check\\\" — a body with two conflicting declared arities defeats the single-chain model, and a malformed hand-built image slips through the sole gatekeeper (S3: \\\"Only the B1 image validator lets executable images through\\\"). Same family: walk-body drops a second reaching chain across bodies via `[:jump]`, so a :load-bound validated under chain A executes under chain B. Minimal fix: if a body's entry pc gets re-dequeued with a chain different from its recorded one, record a defect (extend all-body-chains to compare chains and return a conflict defect); a full fix keeps the set of chains per pc and validates every :load-bound under all chains that reach it.\\n\\n**P2-1: JVM host-double? silently folds BigDecimal/Float to the nearest double** — debruijn_code.cljc:328-336 (`(and (number? v) (not long/ratio/bigint))`) + :413 double-le-hex `(double v)` at :234. Scenario: `(encode-scalar 0.1000000000000000000001M)` and `(encode-scalar 0.1M)` produce identical bytes — two distinct JVM values, silently normalized; likewise for `(float 1.5)`. Design S2: \\\"Values outside the declared executable domain are rejected with a qualified unsupported-value diagnostic and never silently normalized\\\"; the 14-class domain (lines 163-164) has no BigDecimal/Float class, and vm/plain-data? (vm.cljc:726) accepts any `(number? x)` into :const/:data slots, so such values pass `image-defect` and reach the encoder. Minimal fix: make JVM host-double? `(instance? Double v)` (CLJS/CLJD keep the complement rule) → BigDecimal/Float fall through to nil → :unsupported-value. (The projection's numeric-class has the same fold via float?, but that's the merged dormant table's own domain decision, and this dimension's contract explicitly disclaims silent normalization.)\\n\\nHmm — wait, does the design require BigDecimal rejection? \\\"Values outside the declared executable domain\\\" — the domain is 14 classes; BigDecimal isn't a member of any class; so yes, reject. And the P1 criterion is \\\"wrong bytes / wrong hash\\\" — folding two distinct values to the same bytes is a wrong-hash scenario, but only for out-of-domain inputs that should be rejected in the first place. P2 (serious gap) seems appropriate. It requires JVM-only exotic numerics, and there's parity precedent in the projection. Keep P2.\\n\\n**P2-2: No test exercises any :unsupported-value rejection path** — the test file never once triggers encode-scalar's throw: no unpaired surrogate fixture (buildable on all 3 hosts: JVM \\\"\\\\uD800\\\", CLJS (js/String.fromCharCode 0xD800), CLJD via String.fromCodePoint… hmm on CLJD? (str (String.fromCodePoint 0xD800))? On Dart, lone surrogates within a String are possible via the String.fromCharCodes equivalent), no out-of-domain value fixtures, no map/set collision fixtures. The rejection behavior is an S2 requirement (\\\"Unpaired UTF-16 surrogates are rejected with :unsupported-value\\\"); the P1-1 :str bypass survives precisely because no test pins the rejection. The B1 box lists \\\"a ratio fixture that is either encoded or rejected\\\" — the JVM-encoding side is tested, but the CLJS/CLJD rejection side is untested (understandable — values can't be built there; the host-boundary test inspects the support set instead — acceptable), but surrogate rejection is testable on every lane and untested. Minimal fix: one cross-host deftest asserting `(thrown-with-msg? ... :unsupported-value ...)` on (dc/encode-scalar <lone-surrogate-string>) and on image-hash of `[[:const lone-surrogate]]`… note that encode-scalar throws ex-info; use thrown? with ex-data check.\\n\\nActually is this a \\\"serious gap\\\" P2 or P3? The completion criteria for B1 don't literally enumerate surrogate tests; S2 requires the behavior; the behavior in the :const path is implemented. Missing tests → P2 borderline. Given the adversarial role, and given that the untested path harbored a real bug (P1-1), I'll rate it P2.\\n\\n**P3-1: saturated-operands hand-copied, drops [:ffi-call 2]** — debruijn_code.cljc:630 vs code.cljc:242. No hole today: this dimension's operand-kind-checks gives :uint `nonneg-int?` (line 610), so a nil ffi-argc fails as :operand-kind rather than :saturation; but the set is described as mirroring the source's saturation rules while silently omitting one entry — if a future :str/:bool/:buffer saturated slot were added to ffi-call, or the :uint check were relaxed, the omission becomes a hole. Minimal fix: add [:ffi-call 2] (harmless duplication) or derive the set from the source table's saturated operands minus redefined mnemonics. Also note the rule-name drift: a nil uint is reported as :operand-kind here, but :saturation/:argc in yin.vm.code — fine as its own vocabulary, worth a docstring note.\\n\\n**P3-2: ratio components can exceed long range → unqualified exception** — debruijn_code.cljc:423-424 `(int64-le-hex (numerator v))`: a JVM Ratio's numerator/denominator are BigIntegers; `(/ 10000000000000000000N 3)` produces a ratio whose numerator's `(long …)` throws IllegalArgumentException \\\"Value out of range for long\\\" rather than a qualified :unsupported-value ex-info (rejected, but the diagnostic contract \\\"qualified unsupported-value diagnostic\\\" is broken). Same for a huge BigInteger :uint operand via int64-le-hex (encode-operand line 483) — though target-bounds limits :pc, nothing but nonneg-int? limits :uint. Minimal fix: guard ratio encoding with `(<= Long/MIN_VALUE n Long/MAX_VALUE)` → :unsupported-value; or encode components as :bigint text. For :uint, either bound the operand or catch → :unsupported-value.\\n\\nHmm, :uint on CLJS: a non-safe integer like 1e30 passes nonneg-int? (integer? 1e30 — CLJS's integer? = Number.isInteger → true!) → int64-le-hex cljs branch: mod/floor computation on 1e30 → precision loss → silently wrong bytes! Let me verify: CLJS `(integer? 1e30)` → true (Number.isInteger(1e30) is true). nonneg-int? ✓ passes. encode-operand :uint → int64-le-hex cljs branch: lo = mod 1e30 by 2^32 — floating-point mod on a value beyond 2^53 → inexact → wrong bytes, silently. But could a 1e30 :uint operand exist? argc of 1e30 in a hand-built image. JVM: nonneg-int? (integer? 1e30) → JVM's integer? on a Double → false ✓ rejected. CLJS accepts and encodes garbage. Cross-host: an image with `[:call 1e30 true]` — wait, call's argc is :uint; validator on CLJS accepts 1e30 (integer? true); encodes to wrong bytes vs JVM rejects the same image. Divergence. But is 1e30 a legal argc? No sane image has one; but a validator divergence across hosts means the same image is valid on one host and invalid on another — violates \\\"only the gatekeeper\\\" consistency. Severity: P3 (edge case, hand-built adversarial images only; B6 receives external images → could matter for consensus across hosts on image validity). Mention within P3-2 or as standalone P3. Minimal fix: require safe-integer range for :uint/:pc operands (on all hosts `(<= 0 x < 2^53)` — JVM's target-bounds already limits :pc; :uint needs its own bound).\\n\\nActually wait, also verify: CLJS `(integer? 1e30)` — cljs.core/integer? = `(and (number? n) (js/Number.isInteger n))`? Actually cljs's integer?: \\\"Returns true if n is an integer.\\\" Implementation: `(and ^boolean (goog/isNumber n) ^boolean (js/Number.isInteger n))`? Hmm, I think modern cljs's integer? is `(and (number? n) (not (js/Number.isNaN n)) (js/isFinite n) (== n (Math.floor n)))` or uses Number.isInteger. Either way 1e30 → true. ✓. And nonneg-int? → true. OK.\\n\\nAnd :pc target-bounds `(<= 0 x (dec length))` rejects 1e30 for pc ✓ on all hosts. So only :uint (call/ffi-call argc, closure arity, load-bound depth/position!). load-bound depth 1e30: passes nonneg-int? → scope-defect: `(>= depth (count chain))` → 1e30 ≥ chain length → defect ✓ caught. closure arity 1e30: passes validator; on CLJS, encode → wrong bytes vs JVM: JVM's (long 1e30)? JVM operand 1e30 is a Double → integer? false → operand-kind defect ✓ JVM rejects, CLJS accepts+encodes garbage. Divergence remains. P3.\\n\\n**P3-3: Descriptor hashes prose** — debruijn_code.cljc:128, 138: the :dim/encoding :image-hash formula string and :dim/lift-to description sentence live inside `encode-scalar descriptor` → descriptor-hash → H. Copy-editing either sentence forks H for every image. Design S2: the descriptor \\\"declares the lowering-contract version, arity, ordered slots, canonical encoding, and one lift morphism\\\" — a prose declaration satisfies the wording, but hashing free text makes H fragile; lowering-contract-version already guards shape changes. Minimal fix: reduce the hashed value to data (keywords), move prose to docstrings. Borderline P3.\\n\\n**P3-4: image-scalar-classes only scans :const, missing :data operands of :store-get/:store-put** — debruijn_code.cljc:537. The code follows the design's own sentence (\\\"derived from tags within the hashed :const operands\\\", design line 198), so this is a design-level under-approximation that the code inherits: a JVM-produced `[[:store-put \\\"k\\\" 1/2] [:return]]` has a :ratio tag inside the bytes that get hashed, but `validate-host-support` on CLJS/CLJD reports no missing classes, so the S2 host-boundary rejection (\\\"receiving hosts lacking the class reject the image before execution with a qualified unsupported-value outcome\\\") can't fire from this scan. Minimal fix (code side, cheap): scan every :data operand (const/store-get/store-put) — strictly more conservative, still \\\"derived from tags within hashed operands\\\"; or record a design-level decision.\\n\\n**Reader conditional audit (clean, with notes):**\\n- Every #?() in source files: code-unit-at (:clj/:cljs/:cljd all explicit), int64-le-hex (:cljs explicit + :default — no :clj branch, cljd falls to :default which is the correct portable bit-op code, matching the yin.vm.debruijn:844-853 original), double-le-hex (all 3 explicit), host-char?/host-ratio?/host-bigint? (:cljd first and false — matches the \\\"put :cljd first\\\" guidance in handoff.md:212-215 and the amended design wording), host-long? (:clj first but :cljd explicit — the emit reader matches :cljd regardless of position; same order as the production precedent yin.vm.debruijn:220-225), the remove-set in host-supported-scalar-classes (:cljs/:cljd/:clj all explicit), the :ratio branch in encode-scalar (:cljs/:cljd explicit before :default — correctly prevents unresolved numerator/denominator on Dart), the :cljd-only import in the ns form is first-positioned. No dependence on :default where :clj is present. ✓\\n- Test files: three JVM-only deftests use \\\"unconditional deftest with conditional body\\\" — the very spelling orchestrator-log.md:1636-1638 recommends for CLJD — with :clj branch + :default nil inside the deftest body; the deftest itself is emitted on all lanes and the body compiles to nil on Dart, so the `1/2`, `\\\\a`, `10000000000000000000N` literals never reach the Dart emitter. test/README.md:10's stricter `#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)` guard is for excluding whole top-level forms; not needed here. Verified against precedent (test/dao/gui/event/fixture_test.cljc:56,74,87 same shape). Clean — with the caveat that if a future refactor lifts these conditionals to top level, they'll need the :cljd-first spelling.\\n\\n**Confirmed clean list:**\\n1. Opcode table is genuinely derived: carried-mnemonics = (disj code/mnemonics :var :closure) (line 65), each entry = renamespace-slots over (get code/vector-operand-table m) (line 80) — kinds and order are read off the source table; the new table has all 21 source mnemonics accounted for (19 carried + :closure redefined + :var split into :load-bound/:load-free); test 232-249 pins the derivation against the live source table, so changes in code/vector-operand-table propagate and the shape test fails on drift. :load-bound [depth :uint, position :uint], :load-free [name :sym], :closure [arity :uint, body :pc] (lines 82-84) matches design S2 exactly (\\\"…:var becomes :load-bound or :load-free, and :closure carries arity and body references\\\"). The :syms kind correctly disappears along with the old :closure's params slot. The mnemonic-tag ordering (sort, lines 450-457) is deterministic and pinned by golden bytes (const=03, closure=02, load-bound=09, load-free=0a, return=0e — I verified the indices by hand against the sorted 22-mnemonic vocabulary).\\n2. No access to yin.vm.debruijn private vars: requires = clojure.set, dao.jing, yin.vm, yin.vm.code only (lines 41-44); every qualified call is public (jing/sha256, vm/plain-data?, code/mnemonics, code/vector-operand-table, code/terminators); every mention of yin.vm.debruijn is in prose. The copied helpers (to-hex 154-165, code-unit-at 168-172, utf8-byte-length 175-193, framed 200-205, int64-le-hex 208-223, double-le-hex 226-240, well-formed-utf16? 247-264) are faithful to yin.vm.debru