the descriptor declares all five S2 elements ✓ (lines 122-138).\\n\\nWait — one more re: P1-1 severity: is jing/sha256 on JVM's lone surrogate → 0x3F actually true? Java's String.getBytes(\\\"UTF-8\\\") replaces unpaired surrogates with '?' (0x3F), yes. CLJS goog crypt stringToUtf8ByteArray: treats charCode > 0x7FF → 3-byte encoding of the surrogate value itself (0xED 0xA0 0x80 for 0xD800) — actually goog's implementation encodes the code unit as-is into 3 bytes (doesn't replace). Dart's utf8.encode throws on unpaired surrogates (FormatException) by default, allowMalformed → replacement char. So: JVM '?', CLJS 3-byte CESU-like, Dart throws → three different behaviors, at least one host diverges or crashes. Solid P1.\\n\\nAlso for CLJS the utf8-byte-length on \\\"\\\\uD800\\\" counts 3 (>= 0x800 → 3), length prefix 3; JVM's actual byte is 1 (0x3F) → length prefix mismatches content even on JVM. Wrong bytes on JVM too. P1 confirmed.\\n\\nShould I double-check that `framed` is used anywhere else with unguarded raw content? encode-scalar :string — guarded via scalar-class ✓. ident-content — guarded via ident-parts-ok? ✓. encode-operand :str — not guarded ✗. That's the only hole.\\n\\nAlso, is there any path where a keyword/symbol name reaches `framed` unguarded? :sym/:kw operands go via encode-scalar → scalar-class guard ✓.\\n\\nAlright, verdict: NOT READY or READY WITH CHANGES? Two P1s (one produces wrong bytes/identity fork for a class of images the validator accepts; one lets a malformed image through the sole gatekeeper). Both edge-case hand-built images, lowerer output would never trigger either… but the whole point of B1's validator+encoder is adversarial/foreign images (B6). Criteria: P1 = \\\"wrong bytes, wrong hash, validator hole letting malformed images through.\\\" Two P1s → NOT READY. Hmm. \\\"READY WITH CHANGES\\\" vs \\\"NOT READY\\\": given the phase is exactly \\\"validator + encoder as sole gatekeeper\\\" and both P1s defeat precisely the guarantee the