ljc:242 vs debruijn_code.cljc:630.\\n\\nP3-2: debruijn_code.cljc:423-424, 483, 595-597.\\n\\nP3-3: debruijn_code.cljc:127-138, 502-508.\\n\\nP3-4: debruijn_code.cljc:531-539; design lines 198-202.\\n\\nAlso the CLJS :uint precision divergence folded into P3-2? Or as standalone P3-5. Fold: P3-2 covers int64-le-hex input-range issues on both ends (JVM ratio components throw unqualified; CLJS accepts unsafe-integer :uint and silently mis-encodes while JVM rejects the same image → validator divergence across hosts). Actually these are two different things; let P3-2 be ratio overflow, and P3-3 be CLJS :uint unsafe integer. Then prose→P3-4, scan→P3-5. OK.\\n\\nOne more re-verification on P1-2 — is the conflict image actually accepted? One more check on walk-body's nested-closure collection: at pc0 `nested'` appends [(nth t 2) (cons (nth t 1) chain)] = [3 '(2)] ✓; at pc1 → [3 '(1 (nothing))]… chain at pc1 is '() (root body), so nested is [3 '(1)] ✓. all-body-chains processes [3 '(2)] first (queue order = BFS collection order) → owner[3] = '(2). Then [3 '(1)] is skipped ✓. scope-defect pc3: chain '(2), depth 0 OK, position 1 < 2 OK → nil defect ✓. image-defect → nil. Accepted. And note the nondeterminism flavor: swap the two closure instructions (`[[:closure 1 3] [:closure 2 3] …]`) and the same :load-bound 0 1 gets rejected — validation depends on which declaration is walked first. Good detail to cite.\\n\\nLet me also verify the claim that position 1 is out of range on a frame with arity-1 closure invocation: design §4: calls use `(vec (take arity (concat args (repeat nil))))` → frame length = arity = 1 → position 1 → nth out-of-range → VM error or nil. ✓ Malformed at runtime.\\n\\nAlso re-verify trace for P1-1: `[[:gensym \\\"\\\\uD800\\\"] [:return]]` — well-formed: mnemonic ✓ arity ✓ (gensym 1 operand) operand-kind :str → string? ✓ saturation: [:gensym 1] is non-nil ✓ terminator ✓. scope: no load-bound ✓. image-defect nil. image-hash → encode-tuple → encode-operand :str → framed :string \\\"\\\\uD800\\\" — no throw. ✓.\\n\\nOne more: does `encode-scalar` throw or return for a const string with a lone surrogate? scalar-class → well-formed-utf16? false → nil → case default → throw ex-info :unsupported-value ✓. Good — the asymmetry proves intent.\\n\\nNow to write the final answer. Format: starts with the four header lines exactly, then verdict, findings with file:line, design quote, minimal fix, then the confirmed-clean section. Findings only; no edits. This is the final response — no plan file needed (the task explicitly says produce the full review as final response, not a plan). Plan mode says I should write a plan file… but the user's task is a review with an explicit deliverable format, read-only, \\\"produce the full review now as your final response.\\\" No plan file needed since there's no implementation work to plan; the deliverable is the review itself. Just answer directly.\\n\\nLength: needs to be thorough but concise.\",\"signature\":\"\"},{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-22 08:20:00 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-22 15:20:00 Indochina Time\\nCoding-Agent: cmd\\nSession-ID: pending\\n\\n## Verdict: NOT READY\\n\\nTwo P1s, both defeating guarantees this exact phase exists to provide (the sole admitter of images, and host-identical hashed bytes). Both fixes are small and local; a re-review after them should be fast.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## P1 findings\\n\\n**P1-1. `:str` operands bypass the UTF-16 surrogate guard — unrefused, host-divergent hashed bytes.**\\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:482` (`:str (framed :string (or x \\\"\\\"))` in `encode-operand`).\\nDesign violated (yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md:168-171): \\\"Unpaired UTF-16 surrogates are refused with `:unsupported-value`, as they are by the projection's UTF-16 guard. Values outside the declared executable domain are refused with a qualified `unsupported-value` diagnostic, not silently canonicalized.\\\"\\nConcrete scenario: `[[:gensym \\\"\\\\uD800\\\"] [:return]]` passes `image-defect` (the `:str` kind check is only `string?`, line 607), then `image-hash` hashes it without refusal. `jing/sha256` UTF-8-encodes the lone surrogate three different ways — JVM `.getBytes \\\"UTF-8\\\"` emits `0x3F` (and the `utf8-byte-length` prefix says 3, so even the JVM's own framing is internally wrong), CLJS `stringToUtf8ByteArray` emits a 3-byte CESU-like encoding, Dart `utf8.encode` throws. Same image → different H (or a crash) per host: a silent identity fork, exactly what S2 forbids. The `:const` path refuses correctly (`scalar-class` line 355 → nil → qualified throw lines 442-443), which proves the asymmetry is an oversight, not a decision.\\nSmallest fix: at line 482, route non-nil `:str` through the guarded encoder — `:str (if (nil? x) (framed :string \\\"\\\") (encode-scalar x))` — or add an explicit `well-formed-utf16?` check throwing the same `:unsupported-value` ex-info.\\n\\n**P1-2. The scope validator accepts a body declared by two closures with conflicting arities — a malformed image gets through the sole admitter.**\\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc:724-730` (`all-body-chains` skips an entry when `(contains? owner entry)`, discarding the second declared chain) and `:703` (`walk-body` likewise drops re-reach under a different chain, e.g. via a cross-body `:jump`).\\nDesign violated (yin.vm.debruijn-vm.md:250-252): \\\"B1's image validator repeats the check by walking each body's declared arity and enclosing-body chain\\\"; (254-255) \\\"only the B1 image validator admits executable images.\\\"\\nConcrete scenario: `[[:closure 2 3] [:closure 1 3] [:return] [:load-bound 0 1] [:return]]` → `image-defect` returns nil (I traced it: the BFS records chain `'(2)` at pc 3 first; the second declaration `'(1)` is silently dropped; position 1 < 2 validates). But the arity-1 closure at pc 1 declares the same body; called through it, §4's `(vec (take arity ...))` frame has 1 slot and `[:load-bound 0 1]` reads out of range at