/debruijn_code_test.cljc` (whole file): the qualified throw at `debruijn_code.cljc:442-443`, the map/set collision throws (`:432-441`), and the surrogate refusal are never triggered. The lone-surrogate fixture is constructible on all three lanes (JVM `\\\"\\\\uD800\\\"`, CLJS `(js/String.fromCharCode 0xD800)`, CLJD equivalent), so S2's surrogate rule (doc:168-169) is testable cross-host and untested. P1-1 survived precisely because no refusal test pins the behavior at the second entry point. (The ratio-refusal side on CLJS/CLJD is legitimately untestable — the value can't be constructed there — and the host-boundary test at `:272-291` checking the declared support set is a reasonable substitute for that one.)\\nSmallest fix: one cross-host deftest asserting the ex-info `{:rule :unsupported-value}` for `(dc/encode-scalar <lone-surrogate string>)` and for `dc/image-hash` of `[[:const <lone-surrogate>] [:return]]`, plus (after the P1-1 fix) `[[:gensym <lone-surrogate>] [:return]]`.\\n\\n## P3 findings\\n\\n**P3-1. `saturated-operands` is hand-copied and drops `[:ffi-call 2]`.**\\n`debruijn_code.cljc:630` vs the source `code.cljc:242` (`#{[:gensym 1] [:stream-make 1] [:call 2] [:ffi-call 2]}`). No live gap: this dimension's stricter `:uint nonneg-int?` kind check (`:610`) rejects a nil ffi-argc as `:operand-kind` instead. But the opcode table is derived-as-data while this set is copied, so a future shape change to `:ffi-call` (or relaxing the `:uint` check) turns the omission into a gap. Fix: add `[:ffi-call 2]`, or derive the set from the source table's saturated operands for carried mnemonics.\\n\\n**P3-2. Ratio components outside long range throw unqualified.**\\n`debruijn_code.cljc:423-424`: JVM Ratio numerator/denominator are BigIntegers; for e.g. `(/ 10000000000000000000N 3)`, `(long …)` inside `int64-le-hex` throws `IllegalArgumentException` (\\\"Value out of range for long\\\") — a refusal, but not the \\\"qualified `unsupported-value` diagnostic\\\" the design (doc:170-171) requires. Fix: range-check the components and throw the qualified ex-info, or encode them through the `:bigint` text rule.\\n\\n**P3-3. Cross-host validator divergence on unsafe-integer `:uint` operands (CLJS).**\\n`debruijn_code.cljc:595-597` + `:483`: on CLJS `(integer? 1e30)` is true, so `[:call 1e30 true]` passes `image-defect` and `int64-le-hex`'s CLJS floor/mod decomposition (`:214-220`) produces silently inexact bytes beyond 2^53; on JVM the same image is rejected (`integer?` false for a Double). One image, valid on one host, invalid on another — against the \\\"only admitter\\\" consistency the sharing path needs. Fix: bound `:uint` (and `:pc`) operands to the safe-integer range in `nonneg-int?`.\\n\\n**P3-4. Hashed descriptor contains prose.**\\n`debruijn_code.cljc:128` (the `:image-hash` formula string) and `:138` (the lift-morphism sentence) flow through `encode-scalar descriptor` → `descriptor-hash` → every H. A copy-edit of either sentence forks the identity of every image, even though `lowering-contract-version` already guards shape changes. The design (doc:153-155) requires the descriptor to *declare* these things, not to hash their prose. Fix: keep the hashed values as data (keywords/versions), move the sentences into docstrings.\\n\\n**P3-5. `image-scalar-classes` scans only `:const`, missing the `:data` operands of `:store-get`/`:store-put`.**\\n`debruijn_code.cljc:537`. This follows the design's own sentence (doc:198, \\\"derived from tags in its hashed `:const` operands\\\"), so it is a design-level under-approximation the code faithfully inherits — but the consequence is real: a JVM-produced `[[:store-put \\\"k\\\" 1/2] [:return]]` has `:ratio` bytes in its hash, yet `validate-host-support` on CLJS/CLJD reports nothing missing, so S2's receiver refusal (doc:200-202) cannot fire from this scan. Fix (cheap, strictly more conservative): scan every `:data` operand, or record the `:const`-only limitation as an explicit design decision for B6.\\n\\n## Checked and clean\\n\\n- **Opcode table derivation (genuine, not hand-copied).** `carried-mnemonics = (disj code/mnemonics :var :closure)` (`:61-65`); each carried entry is `renamespace-slots` over `(get code/vector-operand-table m)` (`:78-81`) — kinds and order read straight off the source table, attributes moved to `:yin.debruijn.code/*`. All 21 source mnemonics accounted for: 19 carried, `:closure` redefined, `:var` split. New shapes match the design (doc:148-152) exactly: `:load-bound [depth :uint, position :uint]`, `:load-free [name :sym]`, `:closure [arity :uint, body :pc]` (`:82-84`); the `:syms` kind correctly vanishes with the old params slot. Test `:232-249` pins the derivation against the *live* source table, so drift in `yin.vm.code` fails the suite. I hand-recomputed the sorted mnemonic tag indices (const=03, closure=02, load-bound=09, load-free=0a, return=0e over the 22-mnemonic sorted vocabulary) and the full byte strings of all four golden fixtures (tags, 8-hex length prefixes, LE int64s, ident framing) — they match the pinned literals.\\n- **No private-var reach.** Requires are `clojure.set`, `dao.jing`, `yin.vm`, `yin.vm.code` only (`:41-45`); every qualified call is public (`jing/sha256`, `vm/plain-data?`, `code/mnemonics`, `code/vector-operand-table`, `code/terminators`); all `yin.vm.debruijn/` mentions are prose. The copied helpers (`to-hex :154`, `code-unit-at :168`, `utf8-byte-length :175`, `framed :200`, `int64-le-hex :208`, `double-le-hex :226`, `well-formed-utf16? :247`) are faithful to `yin.vm.debruijn:775-872, 228-253`, differing only where intended (own `class-tag`).\\n- **Scalar encoder core rules.** No NFC anywhere; `ident-content` frames ns/name exactly with `:nil` for an absent namespace (`:390-396`, so `sym` ≠ `ns/sym`); no integral-double folding (JVM `instance?` longs; CLJS safe-integer per doc:179-181; CLJD `int?` matching `yin.vm.debruijn/numeric-class`'s Dart rule); map/set sorted by encoded bytes with collision guards; cross-host double bytes verified consistent (LE bits, one canonical quiet NaN `000000000000f87f`, CLJD BE-ByteData-read-in-reverse matches the original); CLJS negative-long decomposition through floor/mod traced correct (`-1` → `ff`×8).\\n- **Host classification, corrected parts.** `host-char?`, `host-ratio?`, `host-bigint?` are `false` on CLJD and CLJS with `:cljd` listed FIRST (`:284, :296, :313-315`) — matching both the corrected design wording (doc:184-196: char JVM-only because CLJD `char?` can't be trusted; ratio/bigint JVM-only, refused elsewhere) and handoff.md:212-215's trap guidance. `host-long?`/`host-double?`/`code-unit-at`/`double-le-hex` put `:clj` first but carry explicit `:cljd` branches — the same order as production precedent (`yin.vm.debruijn:220-232, 866-872`), which the passing Dart lane proves the emitter honors. I audited every `#?()` in both files: none relies on a `:default` fallthrough where a `:clj` branch is present; `int64-le-hex`'s `:cljs`/`:default` has no `:clj` branch and its default is the portable bit-op code; `encode-scalar`'s `:ratio` branch guards `numerator`/`denominator` behind explicit `:cljs`/`:cljd` branches so Dart never resolves them; the ns-form import is `:cljd`-only. The test file's three JVM-only deftests use \\\"unconditional deftest with conditional body\\\" — the exact spelling orchestrator-log.md:1636-1638 prescribes for CLJD — so the `1/2`, `\\\\a`, and `…N` literals never reach the Dart emitter (precedent: `test/dao/gui/event/fixture_test.cljc:56,74,87`).\\n- **CLJS integral-double rule not conflated with encode.** The encode side implements \\\"long iff safe integer, else double\\\"; the receive-side refusal is explicitly documented as a later decode path (docstring `:34-40`) — correct scoping, since B1 has no wire decoder (B6's obligation; see P3-5 for the scan gap it inherits).\\n- **`image-hash` = H, alone.** `sha256(descriptor-hash ‖ encode-image v)` (`:511-524`), descriptor-hash folding in `lowering-contract-version` (`:106-110, :122`) — matches D9/D14. Exactly two sha256 call sites; no projection fingerprint, no second identity. Nothing diagnostic in the hashed bytes: no side table exists in this file; hashed content is descriptor data plus positional tuples only. `jing/sha256` hashes UTF-8 bytes of the string on all three hosts (`dao/jing.cljc:271-288`), so the mixed hex+raw preimage is host-identical for well-formed UTF-16 content (the one bypass is P1-1).\\n- **Validator structural rules.** Mirror `yin.vm.code`'s §7.5 rules 1-7 with the argc rule folded into a stricter `:uint` kind check; rule ordering is safe (mnemonic precedes arity, so a nil table lookup can't reach it); root-body `:load-bound` rejected (depth 0 ≥ count of `'()`); dangling jump/closure-body caught by `target-bounds`; `:load-bound` at an unreached pc rejected via nil chain (`:748-749`); nested-closure chains innermost-first, consistent with §4's frame order conversion.\\n- **Tests vs the B1 box.** Composed (line 95, U+00E9) vs decomposed (line 96, U+0065 U+0301) é verified in the raw file bytes — a real no-NFC proof; `1`/`1.0` tested on JVM+Dart and excluded on CLJS exactly as the corrected design requires (doc:193-195); char and ratio distinct-hash tests are JVM-only, matching both the design and the code's actual predicates (neither stricter nor looser); the golden fixtures pin implementation-independent byte and hash literals covering `:const`, `:closure`/`:load-bound`, and `:load-free`/`:sym` — any tag-order, framing, mnemonic-order, or descriptor perturbation shifts the pinned strings and fails on every lane, so the load-bearing claim holds by construction, not by assertion.\"}],\"meta\":{\"source\":\"model\",\"createdAt\":1790061269779,\"messageId\":\"f3bd11be-2910-4eb4-93f0-5c23b84210ce\"}}],\"interrupted\":false,\"modState\":{\"workspace\":{\"cwd\":\"/Users/sto/workspace/worktree-debruijn-b1\",\"platform\":\"darwin\",\"branch\":\"debruijn-b1\",\"gitStatus\":\"A  src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc\\nA  test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc\\n?? collab/1790055879109-yinvm-engineer-debruijn-b1.claude-sonnet-5.stdout.log\\n?? collab/1790055879109-yinvm-engineer-debruijn-b1.prompt.md\\n?? collab/1790060000000-reviewer-debruijn-b1.prompt.md\\n?? node_modules\",\"recentCommits\":[\"562dd46d docs(design): correct char and ratio host support for ClojureDart\",\"6f082654 docs(design): accept the returned-result-set merge as a known limit\",\"f54a9888 docs(design): correct = to bind content=, not host =\",\"55f435ec docs: log the rebuild-readiness gate clearance and the no-backward-compat ruling\",\"cab33f84 docs(design): clear the CBOR rebuild-readiness gate\"],\"additionalDirectories\":[]},\"compaction\":{\"tokens\":106722,\"basis\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Can't execute shell commands due to permission issues. But that's fine — the git snapshot in context showed \\\"A\\\" (staged), but the task says it's already committed at HEAD 03eeffef, so I'll trust the task description. Reading from the working tree is possible either way, and I've already done that. The snapshot was explicitly noted as potentially stale.\\n\\nPrecedent: test/dao/gui/event/fixture_test.cljc uses `#?(:clj ... :default nil)` — wait, let me check the pattern at the head of those conditionals. Line 55-56 shows `:default nil))` — I need the head portion. Probably `#?(:clj (do ...) :default nil)`. Whether that file passes the Dart lane... GUI event fixture — maybe it's a JVM-only namespace that gets excluded from Dart at the ns level? Not sure. Good enough: this pattern exists in the repo. Combined with the fact that the orchestrator's CLJD lane passed, I'll treat the test file's conditional expressions as acceptable while noting the stricter guard documented in the README as P3.\\n\\nNo, wait — I should double-check the fixture_test.cljc pattern to see whether :cljd comes first. Let me quickly read lines 50-60. Also, I should check the test/README statement \\\"JVM-only namespaces are excluded at the reader level by #?(:cljd nil :clj ...)\\\" — the debruijn test file isn't a JVM-only namespace, it's portable with JVM-only branches inside. Regarding nested conditional expressions inside a deftest body, the emit reader (feature :cljd) picks :default nil → correctly skipped on Dart. The \\\"host pass also matches :clj\\\" danger, per the orchestrator-log, applies to top-level forms/requires, where #?(:clj defn) gets host-evaluated and isn't emitted. Here the deftest is unconditional and gets emitted on all lanes. The body is conditional. On Dart the body is nil. That's the correct behavior — it matches the log's recommendation of \\\"unconditional deftest with conditional body for JVM tests\\\"! orchestrator-log:1637 says exactly this: \\\"This plan owes cljd two spellings: `#?@(:cljd [] :clj [[…]])` for JVM glue requires, and unconditional deftest with conditional body for JVM tests.\\\" So the test file follows the recommended spelling (unconditional deftest + conditional body). ✓ Not a danger. However, on the body-conditional thing: `#?(:clj X :default nil)` or `#?(:cljd nil :clj X)`? The log says \\\"conditional body\\\" but doesn't specify the spelling. If the host-eval pass matches :clj, would `#?(:clj X :default nil)` nested inside a deftest body cause problems at build time? The deftest body isn't evaluated at compile time. ✓ No problem. I'll downgrade this to \\\"confirmed clean\\\" with a note.\\n\\nHmm, though one subtle point remains: does the CLJD *emit* reader include the :clj feature? Evidence from debruijn.cljc / dao.jing.cljc production code: `#?(:clj A :cljs B :cljd C)` works on Dart (C is selected). So the emit reader matches :cljd even when :clj comes first → the emit features either don't include :clj, or :cljd takes precedence. Either way, the source files are safe. ✓\\n\\nNext let me check lines 50-56 of fixture_test.cljc — no, skip it. Not needed.\\n\\nRemaining verifications:\\n1. Is the rest of `vm/plain-data?` (the metadata portion) public? — confirmed public (defn plain-data?).\\n2. `code/mnemonics` public ✓, `code/terminators` public ✓, `code/vector-operand-table` public ✓.\\n3. `jing/sha256` public ✓ UTF-8 on all hosts ✓.\\n\\nOne more thing to check — the descriptor at line 122 uses the keyword `:yin.debruijn.code/dimension`, and `:dim/lowering-contract-version` etc. Are `:dim/*` keywords an existing convention? The yin.vm.debruijn descriptor probably uses the same. Not important.\\n\\nAlso, let me check the design-document sentence about the descriptor: \\\"That descriptor declares the lowering-contract version, arity, ordered slots, canonical encoding, and one lift morphism from :yin.debruijn.code/* to :yin.code/*\\\" ✓ all five present (lines 122-138).\\n\\nAlso: \\\"Slots with strict spelling are typed as raw Bytes, so no-NFC behavior is a slot rule\\\" — hmm, the descriptor declares :dim/encoding {:nfc false ...}. \\\"Typed as raw Bytes\\\" — this dimension has no Bytes-typed slots; string content is embedded raw. The encoding map declares :nfc false ✓ sufficient.\\n\\nLet me also recheck scalar-class ordering issues on JVM: `(host-char? v)` comes before `(host-ratio? v)` etc. ✓. But the ordering of `(boolean? v)` and `(nil? v)` ✓.\\n\\nCLJS potential issue: does `(host-long? 1)` also need to handle cljs longs via the :long tag? ✓.\\n\\n**Another potential P2: the JVM branch of `int64-le-hex` uses `(long v)` — what if v is an Integer etc. ✓. If v is a huge positive BigInteger that passes the nonneg-int? for a :uint operand — the validator allows it, but encoding throws an unqualified exception. P3.\\n\\n**`(<= 0 x (dec length))`** — x is an integer of any size ✓.\\n\\nAlright, let me consolidate the findings.\\n\\nFindings:\\n\\n**P1-1: `:str` operand bypasses the UTF-16 surrogate guard** — debruijn_code.cljc:482 (`:str (framed :string (or x \\\"\\\"))` inside encode-operand). Design S2: \\\"Unpaired UTF-16 surrogates are rejected with :unsupported-value, just as the projection's UTF-16 guard does.\\\" Scenario: `[[:gensym \\\"\\\\uD800\\\"] [:return]]` passes `image-defect` (the :str check is `(or (nil? x) (string? x))`, line 607), and `image-hash` blithely hashes it; `jing/sha256` UTF-8-encodes unpaired surrogates differently per host (JVM's `.getBytes \\\"UTF-8\\\"` → 0x3F; CLJS's `stringToUtf8ByteArray` → EF BF BD; Dart's `utf8.encode` → throws or replaces) → identical image produces different H per host: a silent identity fork, exactly what S2/B1 forbids. Also, utf8-byte-length counts it as 3 (per the copied docstring, \\\"will be a 3-byte replacement character\\\"), so the length prefix doesn't even match the JVM's actual byte count. Minimal fix: route :str through encode-scalar (`:str (if (nil? x) (framed :string \\\"\\\") (encode-scalar x))`) or add a well-formed-utf16? guard that throws :unsupported-value. Note: the :const path correctly rejects via scalar-class → nil → throw ✓ (lines 355, 442-443).\\n\\n**P1-2: Scope validator accepts one body declared with conflicting arities** — debruijn_code.cljc:724-730 (all-body-chains skips `(contains? owner entry)`) and :703 (walk-body skips owned pcs). Concrete image: `[[:closure 2 3] [:closure 1 3] [:return] [:load-bound 0 1] [:return]]` → `image-defect` = nil (first-walk-wins chain '(2) validates position 1), but the arity-1 closure at pc1 declares the same body pc3 with arity 1; when invoked through it, the frame has 1 slot, and `[:load-bound 0 1]` reads position 1 out of range at runtime. Design S3: \\\"The B1 image validator walks each body's declared arity and its enclosing-body chain, repeating the check\\\" — a body with two conflicting declared arities defeats the single-chain model, and a malformed hand-built image slips through the sole gatekeeper (S3: \\\"Only the B1 image validator lets executable images through\\\"). Same family: walk-body drops a second reaching chain across bodies via `[:jump]`, so a :load-bound validated under chain A executes under chain B. Minimal fix: if a body's entry pc gets re-dequeued with a chain different from its recorded one, record a defect (extend all-body-chains to compare chains and return a conflict defect); a full fix keeps the set of chains per pc and validates every :load-bound under all chains that reach it.\\n\\n**P2-1: JVM host-double? silently folds BigDecimal/Float to the nearest double** — debruijn_code.cljc:328-336 (`(and (number? v) (not long/ratio/bigint))`) + :413 double-le-hex `(double v)` at :234. Scenario: `(encode-scalar 0.1000000000000000000001M)` and `(encode-scalar 0.1M)` produce identical bytes — two distinct JVM values, silently normalized; likewise for `(float 1.5)`. Design S2: \\\"Values outside the declared executable domain are rejected with a qualified unsupported-value diagnostic and never silently normalized\\\"; the 14-class domain (lines 163-164) has no BigDecimal/Float class, and vm/plain-data? (vm.cljc:726) accepts any `(number? x)` into :const/:data slots, so such values pass `image-defect` and reach the encoder. Minimal fix: make JVM host-double? `(instance? Double v)` (CLJS/CLJD keep the complement rule) → BigDecimal/Float fall through to nil → :unsupported-value. (The projection's numeric-class has the same fold via float?, but that's the merged dormant table's own domain decision, and this dimension's contract explicitly disclaims silent normalization.)\\n\\nHmm — wait, does the design require BigDecimal rejection? \\\"Values outside the declared executable domain\\\" — the domain is 14 classes; BigDecimal isn't a member of any class; so yes, reject. And the P1 criterion is \\\"wrong bytes / wrong hash\\\" — folding two distinct values to the same bytes is a wrong-hash scenario, but only for out-of-domain inputs that should be rejected in the first place. P2 (serious gap) seems appropriate. It requires JVM-only exotic numerics, and there's parity precedent in the projection. Keep P2.\\n\\n**P2-2: No test exercises any :unsupported-value rejection path** — the test file never once triggers encode-scalar's throw: no unpaired surrogate fixture (buildable on all 3 hosts: JVM \\\"\\\\uD800\\\", CLJS (js/String.fromCharCode 0xD800), CLJD via String.fromCodePoint… hmm on CLJD? (str (String.fromCodePoint 0xD800))? On Dart, lone surrogates within a String are possible via the String.fromCharCodes equivalent), no out-of-domain value fixtures, no map/set collision fixtures. The rejection behavior is an S2 requirement (\\\"Unpaired UTF-16 surrogates are rejected with :unsupported-value\\\"); the P1-1 :str bypass survives precisely because no test pins the rejection. The B1 box lists \\\"a ratio fixture that is either encoded or rejected\\\" — the JVM-encoding side is tested, but the CLJS/CLJD rejection side is untested (understandable — values can't be built there; the host-boundary test inspects the support set instead — acceptable), but surrogate rejection is testable on every lane and untested. Minimal fix: one cross-host deftest asserting `(thrown-with-msg? ... :unsupported-value ...)` on (dc/encode-scalar <lone-surrogate-string>) and on image-hash of `[[:const lone-surrogate]]`… note that encode-scalar throws ex-info; use thrown? with ex-data check.\\n\\nActually is this a \\\"serious gap\\\" P2 or P3? The completion criteria for B1 don't literally enumerate surrogate tests; S2 requires the behavior; the behavior in the :const path is implemented. Missing tests → P2 borderline. Given the adversarial role, and given that the untested path harbored a real bug (P1-1), I'll rate it P2.\\n\\n**P3-1: saturated-operands hand-copied, drops [:ffi-call 2]** — debruijn_code.cljc:630 vs code.cljc:242. No hole today: this dimension's operand-kind-checks gives :uint `nonneg-int?` (line 610), so a nil ffi-argc fails as :operand-kind rather than :saturation; but the set is described as mirroring the source's saturation rules while silently omitting one entry — if a future :str/:bool/:buffer