s :uint; validator on CLJS accepts 1e30 (integer? true); encodes to wrong bytes vs JVM rejects the same image. Divergence. But is 1e30 a legal argc? No sane image has one; but a validator divergence across hosts means the same image is valid on one host and invalid on another — violates \\\"only the gatekeeper\\\" consistency. Severity: P3 (edge case, hand-built adversarial images only; B6 receives external images → could matter for consensus across hosts on image validity). Mention within P3-2 or as standalone P3. Minimal fix: require safe-integer range for :uint/:pc operands (on all hosts `(<= 0 x < 2^53)` — JVM's target-bounds already limits :pc; :uint needs its own bound).\\n\\nActually wait, also verify: CLJS `(integer? 1e30)` — cljs.core/integer? = `(and (number? n) (js/Number.isInteger n))`? Actually cljs's integer?: \\\"Returns true if n is an integer.\\\" Implementation: `(and ^boolean (goog/isNumber n) ^boolean (js/Number.isInteger n))`? Hmm, I think modern cljs's integer? is `(and (number? n) (not (js/Number.isNaN n)) (js/isFinite n) (== n (Math.floor n)))` or uses Number.isInteger. Either way 1e30 → true. ✓. And nonneg-int? → true. OK.\\n\\nAnd :pc target-bounds `(<= 0 x (dec length))` rejects 1e30 for pc ✓ on all hosts. So only :uint (call/ffi-call argc, closure arity, load-bound depth/position!). load-bound depth 1e30: passes nonneg-int? → scope-defect: `(>= depth (count chain))` → 1e30 ≥ chain length → defect ✓ caught. closure arity 1e30: passes validator; on CLJS, encode → wrong bytes vs JVM: JVM's (long 1e30)? JVM operand 1e30 is a Double → integer? false → operand-kind defect ✓ JVM rejects, CLJS accepts+encodes garbage. Divergence remains. P3.\\n\\n**P3-3: Descriptor hashes prose** — debruijn_code.cljc:128, 138: the :dim/encoding :image-hash formula string and :dim/lift-to description sentence live inside `encode-scalar descriptor` → descriptor-hash → H. Copy-editing either sentence forks H for every image. Design S2: the descriptor \\\"declares the lowering-contract version, arity, ordered slots, canonical encoding, and one lift morphism\\\" — a prose declaration satisfies the wording, but hashing free text makes H fragile; lowering-contract-version already guards shape changes. Minimal fix: reduce the hashed value to data (keywords), move prose to docstrings. Borderline P3.\\n\\n**P3-4: image-scalar-classes only scans :const, missing :data operands of :store-get/:store-put** — debruijn_code.cljc:537. The code follows the design's own sentence (\\\"derived from tags within the hashed :const operands\\\", design line 198), so this is a design-level under-approximation that the code inherits: a JVM-produced `[[:store-put \\\"k\\\" 1/2] [:return]]` has a :ratio tag inside the bytes that get hashed, but `validate-host-support` on CLJS/CLJD reports no missing classes, so the S2 host-boundary rejection (\\\"receiving hosts lacking the class reject the image before execution with a qualified unsupported-value outcome\\\") can't fire from this scan. Minimal fix (code side, cheap): scan every :data operand (const/store-get/store-put) — strictly more conservative, still \\\"derived from tags within hashed operands\\\"; or record a design-level decision.\\n\\n**Reader conditional audit (clean, with notes):**\\n- Every #?() in source files: code-unit-at (:clj/:cljs/:cljd all explicit), int64-le-hex (:cljs explicit + :default — no :clj branch, cljd falls to :default which is the correct portable bit-op code, matching the yin.vm.debruijn:844-853 original), double-le-hex (all 3 explicit), host-char?/host-ratio?/host-bigint? (:cljd first and false — matches the \\\"put :cljd first\\\" guidance in handoff.md:212-215 and the amended design wording), host-long? (:clj first but :cljd explicit — the emit reader matches :cljd regardless of position; same order as the production precedent yin.vm.debruijn:220-225), the remove-set in host-supported-scalar-classes (:cljs/:cljd/:clj all explicit), the :ratio branch in encode-scalar (:cljs/:cljd explicit before :default — correctly prevents unresolved numerator/denominator on Dart), the :cljd-only import in the ns form is first-positioned. No dependence on :default where :clj is present. ✓\\n- Test files: three JVM-only deftests use \\\"unconditional deftest with conditional body\\\" — the very spelling orchestrator-log.md:1636-1638 recommends for CLJD — with :clj branch + :default nil inside the deftest body; the deftest itself is emitted on all lanes and the body compiles to nil on Dart, so the `1/2`, `\\\\a`, `10000000000000000000N` literals never reach the Dart emitter. test/README.md:10's stricter `#?(:cljd nil :clj ...)` guard is for excluding whole top-level forms; not needed here. Verified against precedent (test/dao/gui/event/fixture_test.cljc:56,74,87 same shape). Clean — with the caveat that if a future refactor lifts these conditionals to top level, they'll need the :cljd-first spelling.\\n\\n**Confirmed clean list:**\\n1. Opcode table is genuinely derived: carried-mnemonics = (disj code/mnemonics :var :closure) (line 65), each entry = renamespace-slots over (get code/vector-operand-table m) (line 80) — kinds and order are read off the source table; the new table has all 21 source mnemonics accounted for (19 carried + :closure redefined + :var split into :load-bound/:load-free); test 232-249 pins the derivation against the live source table, so changes in code/vector-operand-table propagate and the shape test fails on drift. :load-bound [depth :uint, position :uint], :load-free [name :sym], :closure [arity :uint, body :pc] (lines 82-84) matches design S2 exactly (\\\"…:var becomes :load-bound or :load-free, and :closure carries arity and body references\\\"). The :syms kind correctly disappears along with the old :closure's params slot. The mnemonic-tag ordering (sort, lines 450-457) is deterministic and pinned by golden bytes (const=03, closure=02, load-bound=09, load-free=0a, return=0e — I verified the indices by hand against the sorted 22-mnemonic vocabulary).\\n2. No access to yin.vm.debruijn private vars: requires = clojure.set, dao.jing, yin.vm, yin.vm.code only (lines 41-44); every qualified call is public (jing/sha256, vm/plain-data?, code/mnemonics, code/vector-operand-table, code/terminators); every mention of yin.vm.debruijn is in prose. The copied helpers (to-hex 154-165, code-unit-at 168-172, utf8-byte-length 175-193, framed 200-205, int64-le-hex 208-223, double-le-hex 226-240, well-formed-utf16? 247-264) are faithful to yin.vm.debruijn:775-872, 228-253 — diffs only where this dimension intentionally differs (class-tag vs slot-tag).\\n3. Scalar encoder: no NFC anywhere (strings are embedded as-is; ident-content frames ns/name exactly, an absent ns frames as :nil so sym vs ns/sym never collide, lines 390-396); no double folding of integer values (host-long? JVM = instance? Long/Integer/Short/Byte; CLJS = Number.isSafeInteger per design \\\"long iff safe integer, double otherwise\\\"; CLJD = int?); map/set sorted by encoded bytes (lines 432-441), with collision guard; unpaired surrogates rejected at scalar-class (line 355 well-formed-utf16?) → qualified :unsupported-value throw (lines 442-443); out-of-domain → nil class → same qualified throw. Double byte-rule agreement across hosts (LE bits; a single canonical quiet NaN \\\"000000000000f87f\\\"; CLJD ByteData BE read in reverse matches the original). int64 LE two's complement matches for negatives across hosts (CLJS floor/mod decomposition verified: v=-1 → lo=2^32-1, hi=-1 → ff×8).\\n4. image-hash = sha256(descriptor-hash ‖ encode-image v) (lines 511-524), descriptor-hash = sha256(encode-scalar descriptor) with descriptor carrying lowering-contract-version 1 (lines 106-110, 122) — matches design D9. Nothing diagnostic in the hashed bytes: no side table exists in this file; only descriptor data + positional tuples. Only two sha256 call sites (descriptor-hash, image-hash); H is the sole identity; no projection fingerprint. jing/sha256 hashes the UTF-8 bytes of a string on all three hosts (dao/jing.cljc:271-288) — cross-host byte identity for the mixed hex+raw preimage holds for well-formed UTF-16 content (see P1-1 for the one bypass).\\n5. Validator structural rules mirror yin.vm.code's §7.5 rules 1-7 (nonempty/mnemonic/arity/operand-kind/saturation/target-bounds/terminator), with the argc rule folded into a stricter :uint/:pc operand-kind check — I traced each; rule ordering is safe (mnemonic before arity, so nil table lookups don't reach arity). Root-body :load-bound rejected (depth 0 ≥ count of '() = 0); negative depth/position rejected by operand-kind; dangling jump/closure-body by target-bounds; a :load-bound on an unreachable pc rejected via chain nil (lines 748-749). Design S3's own `[5 0]` case tested (test:48-53). Nested-closure chain order is innermost-first, matching the runtime frame-vector transformation S2/B2 describes.\\n6. Tests: é composed (line 95, U+00E9) vs decomposed (line 96, U+0065 U+0301) verified in the raw file bytes — a real proof of no NFC; 1 vs 1.0 tested under #?(:cljs nil :default …) = JVM+Dart only, exactly matching the amended design (\\\"The B1 test for 1 versus 1.0 is a JVM/Dart test\\\"); char and ratio distinct-hash tests are :clj-only, matching \\\"tests for ratio and char are JVM-only\\\" and the amended char/ratio host story (host-char? is false on CLJD because char? can't be trusted; host-ratio? is false on CLJS+CLJD; host-bigint? is false on CLJS+CLJD) — the code's behavior matches the amended design wording exactly, no looser or stricter. Golden fixtures pin the encode-image bytes and image-hash literals independently of the implementation, covering :const scalar, :closure/:load-bound, :load-free/:sym — any perturbation of tag ordering, framing, mnemonic ordering, or the descriptor shifts bytes and fails on all lanes; the byte strings I recomputed by hand for all four goldens (mnemonic tags, scalar tags, length prefixes, LE integers, ident framing) match