xplicit stream correspondence.\\n   Missing marker coverage fails; neither traversal order nor original\\n   cursor-cell numbering chooses a logical identity. Accepts a carrier.\\\"\\n  [wire correspondence]\\n  (let [task (decode* wire (assoc default-budget :check-numbering false))]\\n    (letfn [(rewrite-slot\\n              [d x]\\n              (cond (ref? d) (rewrite x) (refs? d) (mapv rewrite x)\\n                    (and (contains? #{:V :optional-V} d) (= :row (first x))) [:row (rewrite (second x))]\\n                    :else x))\\n            (rewrite\\n134:(deftest window-sorted-and-unique\\n144:(deftest canonical-under-is-renaming-invariant\\n160:(deftest alias-topology-and-census\\n172:(deftest symmetric-cursor-cells-are-interchangeable\\n183:(deftest gates-and-graph-before-hashes\\n208:(deftest numbering-precedes-frame-defect\\n211:        abstract (rows/node :task (assoc slots 5 (rows/node :bindings ['yin/def [:lit 1]])))\\n221:        abstract (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots cycle) 5 (rows/node :bindings ['yin/def [:lit 1]])))\\n229:(deftest schema-and-literal-canonicality\\n258:(deftest child-scope-and-shared-child-dag\\n270:(deftest pending-envelope-and-nil-literal-are-preserved\\n282:(deftest task-root-checks-carried-code-and-frame\\n298:           (:yin.k/kind (error #(rows/decode (rows/emit (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots bad-task) 5 (rows/node :bindings ['yin/def [:lit 1]])))))))))\\n303:(defn- reversed\\n315:(deftest unordered-containers-are-order-free\\n328:(defn- descriptors\\n338:(defn- rename-and-reorder\\n370:(defn- census-cells\\n387:(defn- renamed-reversed\\n394:(defn- verdict\\n399:(defn- root-of\\n404:(defn- c\\n409:(defn- v\\n414:(defn- s\\n419:(defn- equal-bodies\\n424:(defn- review-counterexample\\n431:(defn- path-abc\\n436:(defn- two-sets-shared\\n441:(defn- two-cycle\\n446:(defn- loop-and-two-cycle\\n451:(defn- with-child\\n458:(defn- frame-task\\n484:(defn- frame-fixtures\\n492:(deftest canonical-roots-are-pinned\\n521:(deftest census-is-numeric-from-ten-cursor-cells\\n531:(deftest marker-shaped-guest-maps-are-not-portable\\n544:(deftest well-formed-guest-markers-are-refused\\n552:(deftest every-literal-context-refuses-markers\\n574:(deftest cursor-cell-and-identity-shaped-literal-are-distinct\\n582:(defn seven-cursor-cell-triangle-square\\n590:(deftest seven-cursor-cells-are-refused\\n600:(deftest large-literals-in-ordered-positions-are-not-spelled\\n642:(deftest discovery-order-numbering\\n665:(deftest occurrence-count-separates-members\\n674:(deftest interchangeable-ties\\n688:(deftest alias-pattern-is-part-of-the-key\\n711:(defn- refused-states\\n737:(deftest unresolved-cursor-cells-refuse\\n762:(deftest numbering-is-total\\n771:(deftest gates-and-work\\n804:(defn- rng\\n809:(defn- next-int!\\n815:(defn- shuffler\\n824:(defn- decoded\\n838:(defn- distinct-by\\n845:(defn- gen-value\\n870:(defn- reached\\n878:(defn- gen-task\\n895:(defn- gen-frame-task\\n909:(defn- gen-child-task\\n918:(defn- gen-incidence-task\\n946:(defn- outcome\\n960:(defn- search-states\\n989:(deftest counterexample-search-quick\\n999:(deftest ^:slow counterexample-search\\n---\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\n---\\n\\n# BUILD & TEST\\n\\n## Commands\\n\\n```sh\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\"name\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below for\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\n#\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below).\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\n\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\n```\\n\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\n\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\\n\\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\\n\\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\\n\\n```sh\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\\n```\\n\\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\\n\\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\\n\\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\\n\\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\\n\\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \\\"Test tiers: quick and slow\\\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\\n\\n\\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\\nslice L5) is two tests:\\n\\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\\n  REPL.\\n\\n```sh\\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\\n\\n# ClojureDart\\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n\\n# Linting\\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\n```\\n\\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\\n\\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\\n\\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\\n\\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\\n\\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\\\"When to tag a test slow\\\"): a deftest that\\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\\n\\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \\\"Remote test\\nservers\\\" for how to run them):\\n\\n| Run | Time |\\n|---|---|\\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\\n\\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\\n\\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\\n\\n```sh\\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\\\"test\\\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\\n```\\n\\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-8}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/shards\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" | tee \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$OUT/npm.log\\\" 2>&1                   && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$OUT/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1     && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:python-antlr >\\\"$OUT/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1             && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nSUBS=\\\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\\\"\\n: > \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\nfor s in $SUBS; do\\n  bb test:sub:list \\\"$s\\\" 2>/dev/null | awk \\\"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\");print}\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"\\n  cat \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\n  echo \\\"$s $(wc -l < \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\")\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/plan.txt\\\"\\ndone\\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\\nfind test -type f \\\\( -name \\\"*_test.clj\\\" -o -name \\\"*_test.cljc\\\" \\\\) | sed -e \\\"s#^test/##\\\" -e \\\"s#\\\\.cljc\\\\?\\\\$##\\\" -e \\\"s#/#.#g\\\" -e \\\"s#_#-#g\\\" | sort -u > \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\"\\nsort -u \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\" > \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\"\\ncomm -23 \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\" \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nrun_shard() {\\n  s=$1; f=\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"; [ -s \\\"$f\\\" ] || return 0\\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \\\"$n\\\"); done < \\\"$f\\\"\\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \\\"${args[@]}\\\" >\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\\nprintf \\\"%s\\\\n\\\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_shard {}\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-6}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nrun_ns() {\\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \\\"$n\\\" > \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\\necho \\\"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" > \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nxargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_ns {}\\\" < \\\"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\\n\\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\\nand `git`.\\n\\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\\n\\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\\n\\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\\n\\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\\n\\n```sh\\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \\\"$URL\\\";\\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\\n```\\n\\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\\n\\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\\n\\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\\ninto the clone:\\n\\n```sh\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\\n```\\n\\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \\\"Can't\\nfind flutter on PATH\\\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\\n\\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\\n\\n\\\"One lane set at a time\\\" and \\\"one Dart lane at a time\\\" are per-machine rules about\\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\\\"on one machine where\\navoidable\\\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\\n\\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\\n  by the design's own reasoning (\\\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\\\"), not yet\\n  verified by a measured run.\\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\\n\\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\\nseparate Dart clone.\\n\\n### The full run\\n\\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\nDART=\\\"${2:-}\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nLOG=\\\"$1/target-ci\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$LOG\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\\\" | tee \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$LOG/npm.log\\\" 2>&1               && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$LOG/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:python-antlr >\\\"$LOG/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1         && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"dart peer built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"  # shiva only\\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \\\"$@\\\" >\\\"$LOG/$name.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n         echo \\\"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"; }\\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\\nif [ -n \\\"$DART\\\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\\n  ( cd \\\"$DART\\\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\\\" 2>&1\\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\\nfi\\nwait\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n```sh\\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \\\"bash -lc \\\\\\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\\\\\"\\\"'\\n```\\n\\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\\n(measured 2026-10-11).\\n\\n### Known differences from the laptop\\n\\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\\n  The assertion \\\"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\\\" gets no `bind-failed`\\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\\n\\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\\n\\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\\n\\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\\n\\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\\n\\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\\n\\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\\n\\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\\n\\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\\n\\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\\n\\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\\n   ```sh\\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\\n\\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\\n   Common patterns:\\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\\n\\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./archive/yin-vm/1790348400000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-impl-regate.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:11:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,330p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790347900000-vm-engineer-yin-def-rule-r-commit-one-fix1.claude-opus-5-5.report.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-25 16:59:32 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-25 23:59:32 +07\\nCoding-Agent: claude\\nSession-ID: resume-of-078d0a96-daf0-4cef-b994-076601d800d6\\n\\n# Report: Rule R commit one, fix round 1\\n\\nRole: VM Runtime Engineer. Worktree: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-ucf-rule-r\\n(branch ucf-rule-r). Nothing is staged or committed. The whole uncommitted\\ndiff, commit one plus this round, is 62 tracked files (+1713/-520) and two\\nnew test files.\\n\\n## Lanes\\n\\nEach lane ran solo under mise. Dart ran with `rm -rf test/cljd-out` first.\\nAll three are green. The rule-r tests ran on every host.\\n\\n| Lane | Last round | This round |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| JVM | 2045 tests, 180913 assertions | 2049 tests, 180956 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors |\\n| Node | 1960 tests, 47956 assertions | 1963 tests, 47972 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors |\\n| Dart | 1922 passed | 1925 passed |\\n\\nNode and Dart each gained three tests: the adapter test, the canonicalize\\ntest, and the definition-key requirement test. The JVM also gained the\\nnegative-fixture audit test.\\n\\n- **kondo** on every changed file: 0 errors. All 8 warnings exist at HEAD;\\n  I checked this against HEAD copies of `vm.cljc`, `ast_walker.cljc`,\\n  `linearize_test`, `macro_test`, and `telemetry_test`.\\n- **cljstyle check:** clean.\\n- **ASCII and 80 columns:** every added line in the whole uncommitted diff\\n  (code, tests, docs) is ASCII and at most 80 columns. The one exception is\\n  the grid-table rows in the docs, which must be full table width.\\n\\n## P1: adapters stamped code on the caller's behalf (fixed)\\n\\n- `linearize/ast-loader` and `linearize/rows-loader` now return\\n  `(fn [vm code contract])`. Each checks the incoming code's own AST stamp\\n  (`check-contract!` against `vm/ast-contract`) before it lowers anything.\\n  Only the linearizer's own lowered output, built from verified input, is\\n  loaded under `vm/semantic-contract`.\\n- The only path that stamps code automatically is now explicit:\\n  `vm/fresh-code-loader [load contract]`. Its docstring restricts it to a\\n  medium whose only producer is trusted fresh code. It is used in these\\n  places:\\n  - `test_utils/run-session`, where `queue-ast!` is the only producer.\\n  - `test_utils/run-encoder-session`, where the encoder observer is the only\\n    producer.\\n  - The test suites that lower their own ASTs (`load-ast` and `load-rows`\\n    helpers).\\n- The REPL's `:semantic` loader passes `vm/ast-contract` explicitly, with a\\n  comment that the expander is the only producer.\\n- **Other places that stamped on the caller's behalf.** `ucf/canonicalize`\\n  stamped any batch it was given with `contract-stamp`. That is the same\\n  defect: an old or unstamped batch came out labelled \\\"v3\\\" and could be\\n  loaded under that label. It is now `(canonicalize datoms contract)`. It\\n  checks the batch's stamp first, and the stamp on its output is the one it\\n  checked. I updated its 10 test call sites.\\n- **Tests that go through the adapters.** In `rule_r_test`, for both\\n  `ast-loader` and `rows-loader`:\\n  - an unstamped input is refused with `:contract-missing`;\\n  - an input stamped \\\"v2\\\" is refused with `:contract-mismatch` and reports\\n    `:actual \\\"v2\\\"`;\\n  - an input stamped \\\"v3\\\" lowers, loads, and runs to 3;\\n  - the `fresh-code-loader` path runs.\\n\\n  `canonicalize` gets the same missing and old-stamp tests, plus a check\\n  that the stamp it returns is the one it verified. `linearize_test` asserts\\n  that the adapter refuses an unstamped input before the loader sees it.\\n\\n## P2: the store-write audit was a one-line regex (fixed)\\n\\n`store_write_audit_test.clj` now reads every file under `src` as forms,\\nusing tools.reader with reader conditionals preserved so that every host's\\nbranch is read.\\n\\n- **What counts as a store mutation:** a call whose head is `assoc`,\\n  `assoc-in`, `update`, `update-in`, `merge`, `merge-with`, `into`,\\n  `dissoc`, `select-keys`, `conj`, `swap!`, `reset!`, `vswap!`, or\\n  `vreset!`, when either:\\n  - one of its arguments is `:store`; or\\n  - one of its arguments is a key path containing `:store`; or\\n  - its target is a store. That means a `store`, `new-store`, or `store0`\\n    style symbol, `(:store x)`, or `(get x :store)`.\\n\\n  Also flagged: every `store-put` call, and every map literal with a\\n  `:store` key (a map rebuilt around the store).\\n- **How sites are keyed:** each site is keyed by file, top-level form name,\\n  and call head, with a count. So the allowlist does not shift when line\\n  numbers change.\\n- **How the gate fails:** on any unlisted site, on any listed site that no\\n  longer exists, and on any file it cannot read. Every source file reads\\n  cleanly today.\\n- **Negative fixtures.** 13 must be detected, and are:\\n  - `(assoc-in state [:store 'yin/def] v)`, codex's example;\\n  - `update :store`;\\n  - `update-in [:store ...]`;\\n  - `assoc :store (conj ...)`;\\n  - `dissoc (:store vm)`;\\n  - `select-keys (get vm :store)`;\\n  - `into store`;\\n  - a `merge` of `new-store` written across several lines;\\n  - `swap! a assoc-in [:store ..]`;\\n  - `reset! a {:store v}`;\\n  - `merge-with ... {:store m}`;\\n  - an unlisted `engine/store-put`;\\n  - a write hidden inside `#?(:clj ...)`.\\n\\n  The old one-line regex missed at least codex's example and the multi-line\\n  `merge`.\\n- **New sites on the allowlist.** Parsing found sites the regex could not\\n  see. Each is listed with its reason:\\n  - VM construction: `vm/empty-state`'s FFI-pair map, and the de Bruijn\\n    `create-vm` maps.\\n  - `engine/check-wait-set` and the `assoc :store` calls in `handle-make`\\n    and `handle-cursor`. These carry engine-minted keys.\\n  - `dao.stream.waitset/check`: its result map, which holds the store its\\n    `:advance` wrote.\\n  - `dao.space.query/open-published!`: an index handle, not a VM store.\\n  - `repl/make-expander`: the expander's macro store.\\n  - Display projections in two cljs demos.\\n  - `macro/make-ctx` and `macro/expand-batch`: the checked macro store.\\n- `yin.vm.engine.md` section 1.1 now describes the parsed audit.\\n\\n## P2: definition keys are store-slice requirements (fixed, as designed)\\n\\n- **Test first.** `query_test/definition-keys-are-store-slice-requirements`\\n  failed on both sides before the fix. The requirements corpus now also\\n  includes a definition tree, so tree/segment equality is checked for it.\\n- **AST side.** The `$ast` `:store-keys` query is now an `or-join`. Its new\\n  branch reads the literal key of an `:application` whose operator is a\\n  `:variable` named by a reserved name.\\n- **Segment side.** The `$code` query adds `[$code _ _ :define ?key]`.\\n- A definition still contributes no effect identifier, the same as\\n  `:store-put`. This does not conflict with the completion change from last\\n  round. Fable's \\\"contributes :vm/store-put syntactically\\\" matches how a\\n  `:vm/store-put` row is treated: its key enters the store slice, and its\\n  footprint has no effects.\\n- **Docs.** code-as-tuples 7.7.1: the table row now says a `:define` key\\n  goes into the store-slice requirement, like a `:store-put` key. The 7.7\\n  query listings in the doc match the code. The UCF text at lines 825-829\\n  already agreed.\\n- **Precision consequence (please note).** Completion now pulls every\\n  definition key of every reachable segment into the slice. Previously it\\n  pulled none, which undercounted. So definitions that share a segment with\\n  a reachable closure body now come along with it.\\n\\n  The `completion_test` maker fixture defined `a` and `b` in the same\\n  segment as `mk`. With this fix, reading `b` would also pull `a` and its\\n  stream. I split the fixture into two loads: `mk` and `fact` in one, `a`\\n  and `b` in the other. Every original per-key assertion (`a` pulls the\\n  stream, `b` does not) still holds unchanged. This is the conservative\\n  `:store-put` rule applied as designed. I found no correctness conflict.\\n- **Query-engine finding (not changed).** A plain symbol constant in a\\n  pattern (`[$ast ?op :variable yin/def]`) did not constrain the match; it\\n  appears to be treated as a variable. I used a `reserved-name?` predicate\\n  clause instead. Anyone writing `dao.space.query` patterns with symbol\\n  constants should know this.\\n\\n## Also\\n\\nThe UCF header banner (line 4) said \\\"This revision (r3)\\\". It now says r4\\n(Rule R's \\\"v3\\\" contract), wrapped to 80 columns in ASCII. The UCF 7.3.3 and\\nyin-repl-design texts now describe the verified adapters and\\n`vm/fresh-code-loader` instead of \\\"linearizers supply the constant\\\".\\n\\n## Remaining places that supply a contract implicitly\\n\\nNone of these relabels external input. Each is either a trusted fresh\\nproducer or reports a contract rather than admitting code under it:\\n\\n- `vm/eval`, walker (`ast_walker.cljc`). It takes an AST map from the\\n  caller, stamps it `ast-contract`, and validates the whole tree. The design\\n  names it as a fresh producer.\\n- `macro/bounded-row-evaluator`, the transformer runner. It loads a stored\\n  macro body under `ast-contract`. Those bodies came from expander input\\n  batches, which carry no stamp of their own. The design names it as\\n  trusted. If expander input ever needs to be stamped, this is where that\\n  would apply.\\n- `repl/program-loaders`: expander output. All four VMs load it under the\\n  current constants.\\n- The handoff demo sender stamps its registers with `vm/semantic-contract`,\\n  not with the stamp its code was admitted under. They are the same today,\\n  because loaders admit only the current contract. The receiver verifies\\n  the shipped stamp.\\n- `completion/complete`: `:contract` defaults to `vm/semantic-contract`. It\\n  describes the quiescent VM, and code fetched through `:fetch` is analysed\\n  under v3 rules without a stamp. This is analysis only, never admission.\\n- `ledger/lowering-profile`: records that `lower` (a fresh producer) ran\\n  under v3. `verify-derivation` compares profiles; it does not assign one.\\n- The demos: `compilation_pipeline.cljs`, `continuation_stream.cljs`, and\\n  `register_bench_cljd.cljd` lower fresh yang output.\\n- `vm/fresh-code-loader` and its callers listed under P1: the one named\\n  trusted path.\\n\\n## Files changed this round\\n\\n- **Source:**\\n  - `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`: `fresh-code-loader`, the extraction queries\\n    and fns.\\n  - `src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc`: the adapters.\\n  - `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc`: `canonicalize`.\\n  - `src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc`: the `:semantic` loader.\\n- **Tests:**\\n  - `test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`: rewritten.\\n  - `test/yin/vm/rule_r_test.cljc`: two new tests.\\n  - `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc`: new test and corpus entry.\\n  - `test/yin/vm/completion_test.cljc`: fixture split.\\n  - `test/yin/vm/ucf_test.cljc`: stamps on `canonicalize` calls.\\n  - `test/yin/vm/linearize_test.cljc`: adapter arity and refusal.\\n  - `test/yin/vm/telemetry_test.cljc`: adapter arity.\\n  - `test_utils`, `semantic_stream_observer`, `semantic_engine`,\\n    `semantic_ffi`, `stack_parity`, `stack_effects`, `register_test`: the\\n    `fresh-code-loader` wrappers.\\n- **Docs:** `yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md` (7.7 queries, 7.7.1 row),\\n  `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` (header, 7.3.3),\\n  `yin.vm.engine.md` (1.1), `yin-repl-design.md`.\\n\\n## Unrun checks\\n\\nNone of the requested checks were skipped. The three lanes, kondo, and\\ncljstyle all ran. As before, there is no separate parity-harness command;\\nthe parity suites ran inside the three lanes.\\n\\nStatus: COMPLETE\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./archive/yin-vm/1790348400000-architect-yin-def-rule-r-impl-regate.gpt-6-sol.stdout.log:54:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_27\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-25 17:12:26 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-26 00:12:26 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\\n\\nThe three prior findings are **RESOLVED, PARTLY RESOLVED, RESOLVED**, respectively:\\n\\n1. **Adapter stamp laundering — RESOLVED.** Both adapters check the incoming AST contract before lowering (src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc:468-475,497-503); `ucf/canonicalize` now checks its input too (src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc:210-228). The direct negative tests cover missing and old stamps (test/yin/vm/rule_r_test.cljc:496-531).\\n2. **Store-write audit — PARTLY RESOLVED.** It parses forms and host branches and catches the previously missed `assoc-in` form (test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj:68-83,227-249). It is not an exact audit of all writes; see the finding below.\\n3. **Definition-key requirement — RESOLVED.** Both extraction queries include definition keys (src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1715-1728,1743-1753), matching the corrected documentation (docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md:1356-1362) and the tree/segment test (test/dao/space/query_test.cljc:1537-1544). The completion fixture split retains its original per-key assertions (test/yin/vm/completion_test.cljc:222-240); the wider same-segment slice is conservative, not a hidden regression.\\n\\nP1 | src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:729-733 | `make-ctx` accepts a composition-supplied store of macro packets while checking only its reserved-name keys (line 1233). A packet taken from that store is passed to the transformer runner (lines 982-984,793-797), which loads its rows under `vm/ast-contract` without verifying any contract carried by the packet. An old or unstamped canonical macro packet can therefore be relabelled as `v3`. Direct `invoke` also accepts a packet (lines 810-815). | Carry and verify an AST contract with seeded macro packets before execution; stamp only packets freshly produced by the current expander. Test old and unstamped seeded packets.\\n\\nP2 | test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj:37-57 | The audit recognizes store-like symbol names and direct `:store` arguments, but not an alias such as `(let [heap (:store vm)] (assoc heap 'yin/def v))`. That write produces no audit site. The test itself acknowledges the alias residual (lines 21-24), whereas the design document calls the allowlist exact (docs/design/yin.vm.engine.md:65-75). | Track local store aliases and add this negative fixture, or narrow the documented guarantee and retain explicit review of alias-bearing writes.\\n\\nThe remaining automatic contract sources are either fresh-source paths or descriptive stamps as reported, **except the seeded macro-packet path above**. The query workaround is appropriate: the query engine binds bare symbols in patterns (src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:970-980); documenting that syntax separately is deferred, not a defect in this change.\\n\\nI did not rerun suites. The supplied independent JVM, Node, Dart, kondo, and cljstyle results are green, but they do not cover the unstamped seeded-macro route.\\n\\nVerdict: REQUEST CHANGES\\nSign-off: DENIED\"}}\n./archive/yin-vm/1791720000000-vm-engineer-srvm-phase4b-stage1-r2.report.md:53:- `cljstyle fix test/yin/vm/semantic_register/effects_test.cljc` reformatted the renamed call sites; subsequent `cljstyle check test/yin/vm/semantic_register/effects_test.cljc` — PASS, exit 0.\n./archive/yin-vm/1790074232000-yinvm-engineer-b2-polish.prompt.md:97:no em dashes, cljstyle-style Clojure.\n./archive/yin-vm/1790112269000-yinvm-engineer-cross-body-validator.claude-sonnet-5.stdout.log:23:- `cljstyle`: no such alias/task exists in this repo (`bb tasks`/`deps.edn` have no cljstyle entry); not run.\n./archive/yin-vm/1790112269000-yinvm-engineer-cross-body-validator.claude-sonnet-5.stdout.log:27:- Could not run `clj-kondo`/`cljstyle` as the completion criteria ask (see above) — flagged rather than silently skipped.\n./archive/yin-vm/1790794837027-vm-engineer-cell-slice1.claude-opus-5-5.stdout.log:61:- **cljstyle: blocked.** The command needed an approval this session couldn't grant, so it did not run. I matched the surrounding style by hand. Please run `cljstyle check` on the 8 files.\n./archive/yin-vm/1790794837027-vm-engineer-cell-slice1.claude-opus-5-5.stdout.log:75:- cljstyle did not run (blocked, as above).\n./archive/yin-vm/1792600000000-engineer-p7m-fix1.codex.stdout.log:8:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"git status --short\\ncat collab/1792590000000-review-p7m-lint-scan.findings.md\\ncat docs/agents/build-n-test.md\\nrg -n 'row-algorithm|profile-mismatch|hashes|image|numbering' src/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md\\nrg --files docs/design | rg 'continuation'\\nrg --files -g AGENTS.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"?? collab/1792286000000-orchestrator-note-linker-relocation.md\\n?? collab/1792570000000-engineer-p7m-lint-scan.codex.stdout.log\\n?? collab/1792570000000-engineer-p7m-lint-scan.prompt.md\\n?? collab/1792570000000-engineer-p7m-lint-scan.report.md\\n?? collab/1792570000000-engineer-p7m-scan-golden.clj\\n?? collab/1792570000000-review-p7m-lint-scan.packet.md\\n?? collab/1792590000000-review-p7m-lint-scan.fable.stdout.log\\n?? collab/1792590000000-review-p7m-lint-scan.findings.md\\n?? collab/1792590000000-review-p7m-lint-scan.prompt.md\\n?? collab/1792600000000-engineer-p7m-fix1.codex.stdout.log\\n?? collab/1792600000000-engineer-p7m-fix1.prompt.md\\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-14 +07\\nReviewer: Fable (non-author; author codex)\\nBranch: srvm-phase7m, commit 6fc65722 (`git diff e6337294..HEAD`)\\nScope: `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc`, `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc`, `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc`, `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/scanner_goldens.cljc`\\n\\n# Review: lint and scanner repair on the merged phase-7 branch\\n\\n## 1. The four address-validation sites\\n\\n**Site 1, `decode*` row hash (`rows.cljc:909-914`).** The hashes step now\\nparses the row id first; a registered non-BLAKE3 algorithm is refused\\n`:yin.k/profile-mismatch :row-algorithm`, then the digest is verified with\\n`jing/segment-matches?` and any mismatch (including a malformed id, which\\n`parse-segment-address` answers nil for and the `when-let` skips) stays\\n`:yin.k/hash-mismatch :row`. Order: the check sits at the head of step 4\\n(hashes), after gate, structure and graph (continuation §7.2; transport\\ntuples §8 steps 1-3); the parse is cheaper than the digest, so the\\nrefusal precedes the hash as it should. For a carrier whose profile fixes\\nBLAKE3 this is the right policy: `emit` mints under the default and\\n`rows_test.cljc:110` pins `:blake3`, so no legitimate version-3 pack can\\ncarry a SHA-256 row id; and a forged pack gains nothing, because the\\ndigest is still checked under the algorithm the address names. The\\nrefusal is also not the only guard: with it removed (mutant 5) the\\nSHA-256 root is still refused by canonical numbering\\n(`:yin.k/undecodable :noncanonical-cursor-cells`), so the new check\\nnames the defect earlier and more precisely rather than closing a hole.\\n\\n**But the status word diverges from the published UCF text (F1, below).**\\n`docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:128-130` says a reader\\n\\\"refuses an id whose algorithm is not the one version 3 mints under\\n(`:yin.k/undecodable`)\\\", and step 4 (`:756-761`) folds the same case into\\n`:yin.k/hash-mismatch`. Before this commit the code answered\\n`:yin.k/hash-mismatch :row` (step 4's reading). The commit introduces a\\nthird status, `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, that neither passage names, and\\nthe engineer report presents it as a design choice without citing the\\nspec. `:yin.k/status` is a dispatch contract for readers (profile-mismatch\\nis the gate's \\\"unsupported version/profile\\\" family, undecodable the\\n\\\"this pack is not well formed\\\" family). One of code or spec must move;\\nthe spec's dedicated paragraph already decided `:yin.k/undecodable`.\\n\\n**Site 2, `decode*` slot 11 carried code (`rows.cljc:917`).**\\n`segment-matches? address v` verifies a store address under its own\\nalgorithm; `:yin.k/hash-mismatch :code` and the well-formedness `bad!`\\nfollow unchanged. Correct for a STORE address (A is the identity; the\\nalgorithm is self-described). Mutant 6 (BLAKE3-only) killed.\\n\\n**Sites 3 and 4, `check-install!` (`rows.cljc:1850-1852`).** The manifest's\\n`:yin.semantic/code` address verifies the record under the algorithm that\\naddress names; the record's `:yin.ledger/output` verifies the delivered\\nimage under the algorithm that address names. The two are independent\\nconjuncts of one `and` together with the tree-equality conjunct, so a\\nrecord minted under one algorithm and an image under another both pass\\n(tested, three combinations) and dropping either conjunct is caught\\n(mutants 8a, 8b). No forged install passes on one conjunct alone.\\n\\n**Lint.** `test/dao/jing/hash_registry_contract_test.cljc` is not in the\\ndiff; `flags-equality-validation?`, `validation-target-call?` and the\\nsweep are unchanged. The four sites are satisfied by replacing the\\nequality with the registry's total predicate, not by hiding a\\n`segment-key` call behind an alias or a let (the lint walks nested forms\\nbut not let-bound aliases, so that dodge was available and was not\\ntaken). For every existing BLAKE3-addressed pack `segment-matches?`\\ncomputes the same digest the old `(= id (segment-key body))` did, so\\ndefault-algorithm equality semantics are preserved; the full rows suite\\nwith its root pins is green (§2).\\n\\n## 2. Verification (all foreground, JVM)\\n\\n| Run | Result |\\n|---|---|\\n| `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n dao.jing.hash-registry-contract-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-custody-test -n yin.vm.ucf.rows-checkpoint-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.scan-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.layout-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test` | 121 tests, 10056 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors (`target/p7m-review/lane.log`) |\\n| `clojure -M:test -v yin.vm.semantic-register.scan-test/goldens-match` (slow, every group) | 1 test, 67 assertions, 0 failures |\\n| `clj -M:kondo --lint` on the four changed files | 0 errors, 0 warnings |\\n| `git diff --quiet` after all mutants | clean; worktree carries only untracked `collab/` files |\\n\\nNo existing root id or pin moved: the rows suites that check the pins\\nare in the green lane above and the diff adds tests only.\\n\\n## 3. Scanner golden regeneration\\n\\nIndependently recomputed through a throwaway test namespace (the direct\\n`clojure -Sdeps` form of the reproduction script needs approval in this\\nsession; the probe ran the same `scan/old-answer` over\\n`(:ast (prelude/module-spec registry))`, rebuilt the fixture text exactly\\nas `collab/1792570000000-engineer-p7m-scan-golden.clj` does, and compared\\nwith `=` against the committed file): the regenerated text is **byte for\\nbyte identical** to `scanner_goldens.cljc`; the answer equals\\n`new-answer`; `clojure.data/diff` between the fixture's `[:c4 :py]` and\\nthe recomputation is `[nil nil ...]`; every other group's recomputation\\nstill equals the fixture (71 assertions, 0 failures). Probe deleted.\\n\\nAttribution: `git log 58dc0964..e6337294 -- src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc`\\nshows exactly `c63ba6d6` (C4 I2) and `544e99e1` (C4 I3), both ancestors of\\nthe merge base. The diff's definition heads are: `getattr` split into\\n`lookup-attr` + `getattr` (explains every `-[attr-error true]` line and\\nthe `getattr`/`lookup-attr` free-name additions), and twelve new\\ndefinitions `no-module import-sub dict-ref import-from star-name\\nstar-item star-copy-all star-copy-public import-star import-fail\\nrun-session lookup-attr`, which is the report's `:definitions-added` set\\nexactly. No definition was removed. Only `[:c4 :py]` changed in the\\nfixture (the header note plus that map; the slow goldens-match over all\\ngroups is green). Not a scanner bug; not caused by the rows/derive work.\\n\\n## 4. Regression tests are real\\n\\n- `carrier-row-algorithm-is-part-of-the-profile`: honest SHA-256 root\\n  (digest verified with `segment-matches?`), refused with the named\\n  status and kind; killed mutant 5.\\n- `carried-code-verifies-the-address-algorithm`: honest SHA-256 carried\\n  code decodes to the same slot; corrupted content refuses\\n  `:yin.k/hash-mismatch`; killed mutant 6.\\n- `install-store-addresses-verify-under-their-own-algorithms`: three\\n  algorithm combinations lower and attach; killed mutant 7. Its negative\\n  case is weaker than it reads (F2): it corrupts `:yin.ledger/output`\\n  after the record address was minted, so the record-address conjunct,\\n  not the image conjunct, is what refuses. The image conjunct is\\n  nevertheless covered by the pre-existing test (`rows_custody_test.cljc:299,306`),\\n  which is what killed mutant 8b.\\n\\n## 5. Mutants (cp backup; restore verified by `git diff --quiet -- src`)\\n\\n| # | Mutant | Witness run | Result |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | row hash reverted to `(= id (segment-key body))` | lint sweep | killed (1 failure) |\\n| 2 | carried code reverted to `(= address (segment-key v))` | lint sweep | killed (1 failure) |\\n| 3 | manifest record reverted to `(= (segment-key record) addr)` | lint sweep | killed (1 failure) |\\n| 4 | install image reverted to `(= output (segment-key image))` | lint sweep | killed (1 failure) |\\n| 5 | row-algorithm refusal removed | rows-test | killed (2 failures; the SHA root then refuses as `:yin.k/undecodable :noncanonical-cursor-cells`, i.e. numbering, not acceptance) |\\n| 6 | carried code verified only under BLAKE3 | rows-test | killed (1 error: the SHA-256 address refused) |\\n| 7 | install image verified under the record address's algorithm | rows-custody-test | killed (4 failures, 2 errors; the two mixed combinations) |\\n| 8a | manifest-record conjunct dropped | rows-custody-test | killed (2 failures, by the existing \\\"changed record with honest input and output\\\" case) |\\n| 8b | image-output conjunct dropped | rows-custody-test | killed (4 failures, by the existing cases at :299 and :306; the new test's negative did NOT fire, see F2) |\\n\\nNo mutant survived.\\n\\n## Findings\\n\\n| id | severity | file:line | finding | fix |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| F1 | medium | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:912`; `docs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:128-130, 756-761` | A non-BLAKE3 carrier row id is refused `:yin.k/profile-mismatch :row-algorithm`, but the UCF transport-tuples text says such an id is `:yin.k/undecodable` (its row-id paragraph) or `:yin.k/hash-mismatch` (step 4, which is what the code answered before this commit). A third status is introduced without amending the spec; readers dispatch on `:yin.k/status`. | Owner's call, one line either way. Default: code follows the spec's dedicated paragraph, `(bad! :row-algorithm [id])` and the test's expected status becomes `:yin.k/undecodable`; or keep `profile-mismatch` and amend both spec passages (and the continuation §7.2 hashes row) in the same commit. |\\n| F2 | low | `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:324-327` | The new test's \\\"wrong image\\\" negative is refused by the record-address conjunct (the record changed after its address was minted), not by the image conjunct it means to exercise; mutant 8b passed this test. | Re-mint the record address after replacing `:yin.ledger/output` (and put it back in the manifest) so only the image conjunct can refuse; then the test kills 8b on its own. |\\n| F3 | info | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc:909-912` | The explicit algorithm refusal is redundant for safety (numbering refuses the same pack) and exists to name the defect early; the comment says only \\\"fixed by the profile\\\". | Optional: extend the comment with the numbering fact so a later reader does not remove the check as dead or, conversely, believe numbering could be relaxed. |\\n\\nEverything else is sound: the four sites are algorithm-aware in the way\\nthe lint and `dao.jing.call-site-classification.md` intend, the lint was\\nsatisfied without weakening the test, the golden regeneration is\\nmechanical and reproduces byte for byte with a fully attributed diff, the\\nlane is green and all nine mutants are killed.\\n\\nVERDICT: CHANGES_REQUESTED\\n---\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\n---\\n\\n# BUILD & TEST\\n\\n## Commands\\n\\n```sh\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\"name\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below for\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\n#\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below).\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\n\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\n```\\n\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\n\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\\n\\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\\n\\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\\n\\n```sh\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\\n```\\n\\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\\n\\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\\n\\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\\n\\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\\n\\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \\\"Test tiers: quick and slow\\\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\\n\\n\\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\\nslice L5) is two tests:\\n\\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\\n  REPL.\\n\\n```sh\\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\\n\\n# ClojureDart\\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n\\n# Linting\\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\n```\\n\\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\\n\\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\\n\\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\\n\\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\\n\\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\\\"When to tag a test slow\\\"): a deftest that\\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\\n\\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \\\"Remote test\\nservers\\\" for how to run them):\\n\\n| Run | Time |\\n|---|---|\\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\\n\\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\\n\\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\\n\\n```sh\\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\\\"test\\\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\\n```\\n\\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-8}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/shards\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" | tee \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$OUT/npm.log\\\" 2>&1                   && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$OUT/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1     && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:python-antlr >\\\"$OUT/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1             && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nSUBS=\\\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\\\"\\n: > \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\nfor s in $SUBS; do\\n  bb test:sub:list \\\"$s\\\" 2>/dev/null | awk \\\"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\");print}\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"\\n  cat \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\n  echo \\\"$s $(wc -l < \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\")\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/plan.txt\\\"\\ndone\\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\\nfind test -type f \\\\( -name \\\"*_test.clj\\\" -o -name \\\"*_test.cljc\\\" \\\\) | sed -e \\\"s#^test/##\\\" -e \\\"s#\\\\.cljc\\\\?\\\\$##\\\" -e \\\"s#/#.#g\\\" -e \\\"s#_#-#g\\\" | sort -u > \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\"\\nsort -u \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\" > \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\"\\ncomm -23 \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\" \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nrun_shard() {\\n  s=$1; f=\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"; [ -s \\\"$f\\\" ] || return 0\\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \\\"$n\\\"); done < \\\"$f\\\"\\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \\\"${args[@]}\\\" >\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\\nprintf \\\"%s\\\\n\\\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_shard {}\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-6}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nrun_ns() {\\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \\\"$n\\\" > \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\\necho \\\"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" > \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nxargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_ns {}\\\" < \\\"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\\n\\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\\nand `git`.\\n\\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\\n\\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\\n\\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\\n\\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\\n\\n```sh\\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \\\"$URL\\\";\\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\\n```\\n\\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\\n\\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\\n\\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\\ninto the clone:\\n\\n```sh\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\\n```\\n\\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \\\"Can't\\nfind flutter on PATH\\\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\\n\\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\\n\\n\\\"One lane set at a time\\\" and \\\"one Dart lane at a time\\\" are per-machine rules about\\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\\\"on one machine where\\navoidable\\\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\\n\\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\\n  by the design's own reasoning (\\\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\\\"), not yet\\n  verified by a measured run.\\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\\n\\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\\nseparate Dart clone.\\n\\n### The full run\\n\\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\nDART=\\\"${2:-}\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nLOG=\\\"$1/target-ci\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$LOG\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\\\" | tee \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$LOG/npm.log\\\" 2>&1               && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$LOG/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:python-antlr >\\\"$LOG/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1         && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"dart peer built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"  # shiva only\\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \\\"$@\\\" >\\\"$LOG/$name.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n         echo \\\"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"; }\\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\\nif [ -n \\\"$DART\\\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\\n  ( cd \\\"$DART\\\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\\\" 2>&1\\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\\nfi\\nwait\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n```sh\\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \\\"bash -lc \\\\\\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\\\\\"\\\"'\\n```\\n\\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\\n(measured 2026-10-11).\\n\\n### Known differences from the laptop\\n\\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\\n  The assertion \\\"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\\\" gets no `bind-failed`\\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\\n\\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\\n\\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\\n\\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\\n\\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\\n\\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\\n\\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\\n\\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\\n\\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\\n\\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\\n\\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\\n   ```sh\\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\\n\\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\\n   Common patterns:\\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\\n\\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\\nrg: src/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:113:(deftest carrier-row-algorithm-is-part-of-the-profile\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:124:    (is (= :yin.k/profile-mismatch (:yin.k/status refusal)))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:125:    (is (= :row-algorithm (:yin.k/kind refusal)))))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:129:  (let [image [[:const 0 1] [:halt 0]]\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:130:        address (jing/segment-key image {:algorithm :sha256})\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:132:        wire (rows/emit (rows/node :task (assoc (:slots task) 11 {address image})))]\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:133:    (is (= {address image} (nth (:slots (rows/decode wire)) 11)))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:196:      (is (= 2 (count (rows/canonical-numbering a)))))))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:204:    (is (= 2 (count (rows/canonical-numbering (make-task :x :y)))))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:210:(deftest gates-and-graph-before-hashes\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:213:      (is (= :yin.k/profile-mismatch (:yin.k/status (error #(rows/decode (assoc wire :yin.k/version version)))))))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:235:(deftest numbering-precedes-frame-defect\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:243:  ;; An unresolved numbering is also reported before the frame defect.\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:260:    ;; carriers are built under a supplied numbering.\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:352:;; Discovery-order numbering (design note\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:353:;; yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md §5, items 12 to 21).\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:639:                        d (error #(rows/canonical-numbering in-set budget counter))]\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:669:(deftest discovery-order-numbering\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:671:    (is (= (n :x 1 :y 2) (rows/canonical-numbering (fixture :s1 :s2 :x :y))))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:672:    (is (= (n :y 1 :x 2) (rows/canonical-numbering (review-counterexample))))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:673:    (is (= (n :x 1 :y 2) (rows/canonical-numbering (equal-bodies [:x :y] (v [:x :x :y])))))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:674:    (is (= (n :x 1 :y 2) (rows/canonical-numbering (equal-bodies [:x :y] (v [:x :y :y])))))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:675:    (is (= (n :y 1 :x 2) (rows/canonical-numbering (equal-bodies [:x :y] [:row (rows/node :vec [(s [(c :x) (c :y)]) (c :x)])]))))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:676:    (is (= (n :b 1 :a 2) (rows/canonical-numbering\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:678:    (is (= (n :x 1 :y 2 :z 3) (rows/canonical-numbering (two-sets-shared))))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:679:    (is (= (n :a 1 :b 2 :c 3) (rows/canonical-numbering (path-abc))))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:685:      (is (= (n :y 1 :x 2 :test/cursor 3) (rows/canonical-numbering task)))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:733:    (is (= {:b :yin.k/cc-1 :a :yin.k/cc-2} (rows/canonical-numbering witness)))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:767:          numbering (zipmap (census-cells task) (map #(keyword \\\"yin.k\\\" (str \\\"cc-\\\" %)) (range 1 100)))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:768:          wire (rows/emit-numbered task numbering)\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:789:(deftest numbering-is-total\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:820:      (is (= 4 (count (rows/canonical-numbering task)))))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc:977:      (rows/canonical-numbering task rows/default-budget counter)\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:250:    (is (= {:yin.k/status :yin.k/profile-mismatch :yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive}\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:270:  ;; link id is the row's parent, and its image is the one the manifest\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:290:      (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-image 'host.mod]\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:293:      (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-image 'host.mod]\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:296:      (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-image 'host.mod]\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:298:    (testing \\\"a manifest and record of another image than the one delivered\\\"\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:299:      (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-image 'host.mod]\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:302:      (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-image 'host.mod]\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:305:    (testing \\\"the image the record names, delivered under another vector\\\"\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:306:      (is (= [:yin.k/undecodable :install-image 'host.mod]\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:307:             (kind #(assoc-in % [:response :image] (:image other))))))\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:312:  (doseq [[image-algorithm record-algorithm] [[:sha256 :blake3] [:blake3 :sha256] [:sha256 :sha256]]]\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:315:          image-address (jing/segment-key (get-in response [:image :value]) {:algorithm image-algorithm})\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:316:          record (assoc (:derivation response) :yin.ledger/output image-address)\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:328:                                            (jing/segment-key [[:lit :wrong]] {:algorithm image-algorithm}))) nil)]\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc:329:        (is (= :install-image (:yin.k/kind (refused bad {}))))))))\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:31:> free environment, never a native image, and its frames are `:act` and\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:73:or 2 is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` at the gate and is never normalised or\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:79:§2.1, §4). Any other profile map is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`.\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:126:the numbering procedure's leaf keys depend on the digest and are\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:141:numbering (the n-th cursor cell met in §6's canonical walk), never content-addressed, and scoped to the task whose root\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:214:hashed as Jing hashes it. Collection rows have no metadata slot, so\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:262:included, under the final numbering of §6. The spellings (keys) of §6\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:263:are used only inside the numbering procedure and never decide the order\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:391:id equals `parent`, its image is well formed, hashes to the address it\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:392:is delivered under, is the image the manifest names, and its format,\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:395:**Amended after 7 (2026-10-14)** — Reason: 7b enforces install link and manifest/image agreement at lower.\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:400:is a collection of symbols, and an `:image` map whose `:value` the\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:410:hash-identify the image's `:value` vector (`:install-image`). These are\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:414:No new resolver or independent claimed-image-address check is added.\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:441:`requires` gains `:yin.k/trees`.) No `layout` slot and no native image:\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:465:`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`. The slot admits only `:yin.k/policy`,\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:477:## 6. Canonical cursor-cell numbering\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:485:can). The numbering is therefore defined over the **whole alias\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:489:**What is defined, and what is not claimed.** The canonical numbering is\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:493:`yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.cursor-cell-numbering.md` §2). It is not the\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:499:numbering assigned today.\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:506:the carrier *transforms* into ordered vectors before numbering: each\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:518:procedure below over one item from a numbering; the **root walk** is over\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:519:the `:task` row from the empty numbering and its numbers are the output;\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:521:the enclosing walk's numbering, run only to produce the member's **key**.\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:534:member produced by a key walk under the numbering at that moment (every\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:535:member of one container is keyed from that one numbering):\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:577:  order: each group's members are re-keyed under the current numbering\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:591:the order the host lists them (the numbering note's §2 shows the rows do\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:599:interchangeable (numbering note §3 says what), but nothing it accepts can\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:617:validation (step 7), so a wrong numbering is reported as\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:665:emission the implementation asserts that the numbering is a bijection\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:673:numberings. It decodes the rows to the abstract task, runs the\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:679:map and census (numbering note §2); the procedure's one use of host\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:703:equal root ids express semantic equality under §6's canonical numbering.\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:725:   integer 3, else `:yin.k/profile-mismatch` (version found, supported\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:737:   `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; the two are never conflated.\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:758:   algorithm; every code payload hashes to its key `A` and is well formed\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:772:6. **Canonical numbering.** Recompute §6 under the work bound, for the\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:791:   (continuation §3.3). This step runs after canonical numbering (step 6)\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:822::install-response :install-link-id :install-image :install-child`); (c) dependencies: `requires`'\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:855:`:yin.k/profile-mismatch`, `:yin.k/undecodable` with `:yin.k/path` as\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:874:the assembled task, then canonical numbering and emit; the slice is the\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.ucf-transport-tuples.md:912:  lift's §6 numbering undoes resource remapping. Outside that context\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.v2-amendment.md\\nAGENTS.md\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n./archive/yin-vm/1793310000000-engineer-frame-nan.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log:10:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"git status --short; rg --files -g AGENTS.md -g '*rows*.cljc' src test; cat collab/1792057828000-architect-8f3-nan-ruling.report.md; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"?? collab/1792057828000-architect-8f3-nan-ruling.report.md\\n?? collab/1793310000000-engineer-frame-nan.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\\n?? collab/1793310000000-engineer-frame-nan.prompt.md\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/fencing_rows_test.cljc\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/stage_e_rows_test.cljc\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_test.cljc\\ntest/yin/vm/malformed_rows.cljc\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_test.cljc\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc\\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/compose_rows_test.cljc\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc\\nCreated-GMT: 2026-10-15 09:50:28 GMT\\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-15 16:50:28 +07\\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-15 09:55:00 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-15 16:55:00 +07\\nCoding-Agent: claude (claude-fable-5-1)\\nSession-ID: pending\\nBrief: ./collab/1793100000000-architect-8f3-nan-ruling.prompt.md\\nBranch: srvm-phase8f3 @ 7b8d2a93 (no source edits; probe test ns created and deleted; tree clean apart from collab/)\\n\\n# Architect ruling: 8f-3 Node+Dart `noncanonical-registers` on NaN programs\\n\\n## 1. Verdict\\n\\nRoot cause confirmed by reading the code and by a JVM probe. It is the\\nhypothesised bug class: §3.4 item 13 (`canonical-projection` in\\n`src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/code.cljc`, lines 815 and 820) compares a\\nre-projected tuple against the loaded tuple with host `=`, whole tuple,\\nliteral operands included. The re-projection copies every literal operand by\\nreference (`with-registers`, line 703, `:else (nth t (inc i))`), so the two\\ntuples hold the *same* NaN. The JVM's `=` short-circuits on reference identity\\n(`Util.equiv`: `k1 == k2`) and passes. On JS a NaN is a primitive: `identical?`\\nis `===`, false for NaN, and the number `-equiv` is also false. On Dart `=` on\\ndoubles is IEEE `==`, false for NaN. So the check reports the first tuple that\\ncarries a NaN literal as `:noncanonical-registers` at its pc. The two failing\\ntests put `##NaN` straight into the test form (`(py/float (data/float-value\\n##NaN))`, int_ops_test.cljc 402-412 and 533-536), which lowers to a\\n`[:const rd ##NaN]` tuple; pc 1600/1601 is the first such tuple after the\\nprelude. ±inf, -0.0 and large floats are NOT part of this defect: they are\\nreflexive under `=` on all three hosts.\\n\\nThis passed in 8e only because the `:semantic` runner then ran the old kernel,\\nwhose loader had no item 13. Nothing about the Python prelude changed.\\n\\n## 2. Evidence (JVM probe, throwaway ns `yin.vm.nan-probe-test`, deleted)\\n\\n```\\nA  well-formed? [[:const 0 ##NaN] [:halt 0]]                 -> nil   (shared box; JVM identity)\\nA2 well-formed? NaN in a body and inside a data literal      -> nil   (same reason)\\nB  (= [:const 0 nanA] [:const 0 nanB]), distinct boxes        -> false (JVM = is not NaN-reflexive either; only identity saves it)\\nC  (cbor/content= [:const 0 nanA] [:const 0 nanB])            -> true\\nD  (cbor/content= t (copy t)) for every mnemonic in §2.3,\\n   incl. symbols, keyword ops, [regs], bool, nil buffer, str,\\n   bigint literal                                             -> true for all 22 shapes (no refusal, no throw)\\nE  nested distinct NaNs in {:k} and [...] with ±inf, -0.0,\\n   #{} , \\\"s\\\", 1/2, 1.5M: content= true, host = false\\nF  content= keeps -0.0 apart from 0.0 (host = merges them);  int vs float stays apart\\nG  semantic/load-vector + vm/run of [[:const 0 ##NaN] [:halt 0]] -> value ##NaN   (kernel executes NaN fine)\\nH  reload of the identical vector into the same machine      -> :ok\\nI  reload of a content-equal vector with a FRESH NaN box      -> throws \\\"Cannot load segment: the id already holds different code\\\"\\n```\\n\\nProbe I exposes a second site of the same class (see §3, F2).\\n\\n## 3. The fix (minimal, two sites, one new helper)\\n\\nF1 — `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/code.cljc`\\n\\nAdd `[dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]` to the ns requires and one private helper next\\nto `with-registers`:\\n\\n```clojure\\n(defn- tuple=\\n  \\\"Item 13's tuple equality. Host `=` is not NaN-reflexive on JS and Dart\\n   (only the JVM short-circuits on the shared box), so a NaN literal would\\n   refuse its own re-projection. Slot by slot: the re-projection copies\\n   literal operands by reference, so `identical?` settles them without a\\n   traversal; everything else goes through the encoder's own kind-strict\\n   equality, under which canonical NaNs are equal and 0.0 and -0.0 are not.\\\"\\n  [a b]\\n  (and (= (count a) (count b))\\n       (every? true? (map cbor/content= a b))))\\n```\\n\\n(`cbor/content=` already begins with `identical?`, so the slotwise form gives\\nthe reference shortcut for free.) Then replace the two comparisons in\\n`canonical-projection`:\\n\\n- line 815: `(when-not (= t (nth v pc))` → `(when-not (tuple= t (nth v pc))`\\n- line 820: `(when-not (= (get canon pc) (nth v pc))` → `(when-not (tuple= (get canon pc) (nth v pc))`\\n\\nWhy slotwise rather than `(cbor/content= t (nth v pc))` on the whole tuple:\\nthe whole-tuple form builds a `content-key` over every data literal four\\ntimes per load (two sides, register pass and layout pass); the slotwise form\\nhits `identical?` on the literal slot and never walks it. Either form is\\ncorrect; the slotwise one is the one to ship. Do NOT compare only register\\nslots in the layout pass: there the canon tuple at pc may come from another\\npc of `v`, so literals must be compared, and content= does that.\\n\\nBehaviour notes for the rule: item 13 keeps refusing real register and layout\\ndefects in NaN-carrying vectors (content= is a strict equality, F below pins\\nit). A `-0.0` literal versus a `0.0` literal is now a difference (host `=`\\nmerged them), which is the encoder's own notion of canonical content and is\\nthe right answer for a content-addressed vector. On JS, `content-key` refuses\\nan unsafe raw integral Number; such a literal could not reach `segment-key`\\neither (same refusal), so no vector that loads today stops loading.\\n\\nF2 — `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc` line 281 (`store-image`)\\n\\n`(= (val existing) image)` compares two whole images (vector, bodies,\\nanalysis) with host `=`. With a NaN literal this throws \\\"the id already holds\\ndifferent code\\\" for a content-equal reload: on the JVM already when the box\\ndiffers (probe I), on JS and Dart even for the identical vector (probe H would\\nfail there). Both images under one segment id are deterministic functions of\\ntheir vector, and `:address` is `jing/segment-key` (a keyword\\n`:segment/blake3-<digest>` over the canonical encoding), so address equality\\nis content equality. Replace with:\\n\\n```clojure\\n(if (= (:address (val existing)) (:address image)) images ...)\\n```\\n\\nNo helper, no traversal, host-neutral. (Alternative, if the Architect of the\\ncarrier prefers vector comparison: `(cbor/content= (:vector (val existing))\\n(:vector image))`. Address comparison is the one to ship.)\\n\\nNothing else on the load/derive/allocation path compares literal values:\\nevery other `=`/`not=`/`set`/`distinct` in `code.cljc`, `derive.cljc`,\\n`linearize.cljc`, `layout.cljc`, `analysis.cljc`, `scan.cljc` is over\\nmnemonics, pcs, register ids, counts, symbols or kinds (surveyed by grep; the\\nlist is in the orchestrator's transcript).\\n\\n## 4. Tests to add (portable `.cljc`, all three lanes)\\n\\nT1 — `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/code_test.cljc`, new deftest\\n`item-13-compares-literal-operands-by-content` (keep the `refusals` table\\nand `every-item-has-a-refusal-row` untouched). Accepted (`nil` from\\n`code/well-formed?`):\\n\\n```clojure\\n'[[:const 0 ##NaN] [:halt 0]]\\n;; NaN in a body and in main: exercises the per-body register pass and the\\n;; whole-segment layout pass\\n'[[:closure 1 [x] 4] [:const 2 ##NaN] [:call 0 1 [2] false] [:halt 0]\\n  [:const 0 ##NaN] [:return 0]]\\n;; NaN and signed zero nested inside data operands\\n'[[:const 0 [##Inf ##-Inf -0.0 0.0 ##NaN {:k ##NaN}]] [:halt 0]]\\n'[[:store-put 0 {:k ##NaN} [##NaN]] [:halt 0]]\\n```\\n\\nRefused, pinning that NaN does not mask a defect and that content= is strict:\\n\\n```clojure\\n;; rd must be 0: the NaN literal is beside the point\\n[{:rule :noncanonical-registers :pc 0} '[[:const 1 ##NaN] [:halt 1]]]\\n;; (f (fn [x] NaN) (fn [y] Inf)) with its bodies swapped; the layout pass\\n;; now compares [:const 0 ##NaN] against [:const 0 ##Inf] and must differ\\n[{:rule :noncanonical-layout :pc 1}\\n '[[:var 1 f] [:closure 2 [x] 7] [:closure 3 [y] 5] [:call 0 1 [2 3] false]\\n   [:halt 0] [:const 0 ##Inf] [:return 0] [:const 0 ##NaN] [:return 0]]]\\n```\\n\\n(Codex: verify the pc of the second refusal by running it; the existing\\nswapped-bodies row reports pc 1, where the first `:closure` sits, because the\\nlayout pass reports the least differing pc; the first differing tuple here\\nis also at the closure site. Pin whatever `well-formed?` returns on the JVM\\nafter the fix, then confirm Node and Dart agree.)\\n\\nThese rows fail today on Node and Dart (every accepted row above returns\\n`{:rule :noncanonical-registers :pc 0}` there) and pass on the JVM, which is\\nexactly the gap the lanes left open.\\n\\nT2 — a new deftest in `test/yin/vm/semantic_engine_test.cljc` (it already\\ndrives the register kernel) `a-nan-literal-loads-runs-and-reloads`:\\n\\n```clojure\\n(let [nan   (* 1.0 ##NaN)             ; a fresh box on the JVM, a plain NaN elsewhere\\n      v1    [[:const 0 ##NaN] [:halt 0]]\\n      v2    [[:const 0 nan] [:halt 0]]  ; content-equal, not identical on the JVM\\n      m     (semantic/load-vector (semantic/create-vm {}) v1 code/contract)\\n      value (vm/value (vm/run m))]\\n  (is (not= value value) \\\"the kernel yields NaN\\\")        ; portable NaN test\\n  (is (cbor/content= ##NaN value))\\n  (let [m2 (semantic/load-vector m v1 code/contract)     ; identical reload (fails today on JS/Dart)\\n        m3 (semantic/load-vector m v2 code/contract)]    ; content-equal reload (fails today on the JVM too)\\n    (is (= (:program m) (:program m2) (:program m3)) \\\"one address, one segment id\\\")))\\n```\\n\\nT3 — no new Python test: `yang.python.antlr.int-ops-test/nan-comparisons-test`\\nand `float-division-edge-test` are the regression and go green on Node and\\nDart with F1. Keep them.\\n\\n## 5. Other kernels and paths\\n\\n- `derive.cljc` (`stack-image`, `register-image`, the `:stack` and `:register`\\n  runners) calls `code/parse`, not `well-formed?`; item 13 never runs there,\\n  which is why those runners stayed green in 8e and 8f-3. No change.\\n- `debruijn_register_code.cljc` rules (`live-exact-rule` 641, 368, 518, 550)\\n  compare live sets, arities and owners: integers only. No change.\\n- `debruijn_code.cljc` 233 `(not= v v)` is a deliberate NaN detector. No change.\\n- `yin.vm/same-content?` (vm.cljc 1072) already uses `cbor/content=` after\\n  the 8e ruling. `yin.vm.data/data-content=` likewise.\\n- Known gap, OUT of this ruling's scope, for the ledger:\\n  `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register/frame.cljc` 141 compares a captured\\n  frame's free-environment bindings with the task's free-env by host `=`. A\\n  NaN-valued free binding in a captured, parked or waiting frame would be\\n  refused as `:free-env-mismatch` on JS and Dart. Same class, different layer\\n  (carrier validation, not load). Fix shape when taken: slotwise content\\n  equality guarded for callables and refs the way `data-content=` does.\\n\\n## 6. Ordered steps for codex\\n\\n1. In `code.cljc`: add the `dao.jing.cbor` require, add `tuple=` after\\n   `with-registers`, switch lines 815 and 820 to `tuple=`. Update the\\n   `canonical-projection` docstring with one sentence: tuples compare by the\\n   encoder's content equality, not host `=`.\\n2. In `semantic_register.cljc` `store-image`: compare `:address`, not the\\n   image.\\n3. Add T1 to `code_test.cljc` and T2 to `semantic_engine_test.cljc`\\n   (`cbor` and `code` requires as needed).\\n4. JVM: `clojure -M:test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.code-test -n\\n   yin.vm.semantic-engine-test -n yang.python.antlr.int-ops-test`. Expect T2's\\n   content-equal reload to have FAILED before step 2 and pass after.\\n5. Node and Dart lanes for the same three namespaces (foreground, one lane at\\n   a time per docs/agents/build-n-test.md); confirm \\\"Testing\\n   yin.vm.semantic-register.code-test\\\" appears in the node output and that\\n   `nan-comparisons-test` / `float-division-edge-test` report the Python\\n   vectors, not `[:thrown ...]`.\\n6. Quick tier (G1) before handing back; the design doc\\n   `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` §3.4 item 13: add one clause\\n   that tuple equality is content equality (kind-strict, canonical NaN equal,\\n   signed zero distinct). No other doc changes.\\n7. Report path `./collab/<ms>-engineer-8f3d.report.md`, ending with the three\\n   lanes' counts for the three namespaces.\\n\\nRULING-READY\\n---\\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\\n---\\n\\n# BUILD & TEST\\n\\n## Commands\\n\\n```sh\\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \\\"name\\\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below for\\n# which lanes may overlap.\\n#\\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\\n# overlap lanes, see \\\"Remote test servers\\\" below).\\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\\n\\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\\n```\\n\\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\\n\\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\\n\\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\\n\\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\\n\\n```sh\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\\n```\\n\\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\\n\\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\\n\\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\\n\\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\\n\\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \\\"Test tiers: quick and slow\\\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\\n\\n\\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\\nslice L5) is two tests:\\n\\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\\n  REPL.\\n\\n```sh\\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\\n\\n# ClojureDart\\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n\\n# Linting\\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\\n```\\n\\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\\n\\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\\n\\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\\n\\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\\n\\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\\\"When to tag a test slow\\\"): a deftest that\\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\\n\\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \\\"Remote test\\nservers\\\" for how to run them):\\n\\n| Run | Time |\\n|---|---|\\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\\n\\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\\n\\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\\n\\n```sh\\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\\\"test\\\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\\n```\\n\\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-8}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/shards\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" | tee \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$OUT/npm.log\\\" 2>&1                   && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$OUT/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1     && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:python-antlr >\\\"$OUT/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1             && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nSUBS=\\\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\\\"\\n: > \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\nfor s in $SUBS; do\\n  bb test:sub:list \\\"$s\\\" 2>/dev/null | awk \\\"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\\\\\"\\\\\\\");print}\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"\\n  cat \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\"\\n  echo \\\"$s $(wc -l < \\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\")\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/plan.txt\\\"\\ndone\\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\\nfind test -type f \\\\( -name \\\"*_test.clj\\\" -o -name \\\"*_test.cljc\\\" \\\\) | sed -e \\\"s#^test/##\\\" -e \\\"s#\\\\.cljc\\\\?\\\\$##\\\" -e \\\"s#/#.#g\\\" -e \\\"s#_#-#g\\\" | sort -u > \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\"\\nsort -u \\\"$OUT/covered.txt\\\" > \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\"\\ncomm -23 \\\"$OUT/all.txt\\\" \\\"$OUT/covered.sorted\\\" > \\\"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nrun_shard() {\\n  s=$1; f=\\\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\\\"; [ -s \\\"$f\\\" ] || return 0\\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \\\"$n\\\"); done < \\\"$f\\\"\\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \\\"${args[@]}\\\" >\\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$s.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\\nprintf \\\"%s\\\\n\\\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_shard {}\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nPAR=\\\"${2:-6}\\\"\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nOUT=\\\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\\\"; rm -rf \\\"$OUT\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$OUT\\\"\\nrun_ns() {\\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \\\"$n\\\" > \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n  echo \\\"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \\\"^Ran \\\" \\\"$OUT/$n.log\\\" | tail -1)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n}\\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\\necho \\\"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\\\" > \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\nxargs -P \\\"$PAR\\\" -I{} bash -c \\\"run_ns {}\\\" < \\\"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\\\"\\necho \\\"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\\\" >> \\\"$OUT/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\\n\\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\\nand `git`.\\n\\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\\n\\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\\n\\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\\n\\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\\n\\n```sh\\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \\\"$URL\\\";\\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\\n```\\n\\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\\n\\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\\n\\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\\ninto the clone:\\n\\n```sh\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\\n```\\n\\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \\\"Can't\\nfind flutter on PATH\\\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\\n\\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\\n\\n\\\"One lane set at a time\\\" and \\\"one Dart lane at a time\\\" are per-machine rules about\\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\\\"on one machine where\\navoidable\\\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\\n\\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\\n  by the design's own reasoning (\\\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\\\"), not yet\\n  verified by a measured run.\\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\\n\\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\\nseparate Dart clone.\\n\\n### The full run\\n\\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\\n\\n```sh\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\\nset -u\\nexport PATH=\\\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\\\"\\nDART=\\\"${2:-}\\\"\\ncd \\\"$1\\\" || exit 2\\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\\neval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\nLOG=\\\"$1/target-ci\\\"; mkdir -p \\\"$LOG\\\"\\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\\necho \\\"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\\\" | tee \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nnpm ci --silent >\\\"$LOG/npm.log\\\" 2>&1               && echo \\\"npm ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\\\"$LOG/build-node.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"node repl built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb gen:python-antlr >\\\"$LOG/antlr.log\\\" 2>&1         && echo \\\"antlr ok $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer.log\\\" 2>&1 && echo \\\"dart peer built $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"  # shiva only\\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \\\"$@\\\" >\\\"$LOG/$name.log\\\" 2>&1; rc=$?\\n         echo \\\"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"; }\\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\\nif [ -n \\\"$DART\\\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\\n  ( cd \\\"$DART\\\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \\\"$(mise env -s bash)\\\"\\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\\\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\\\" 2>&1\\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\\nfi\\nwait\\necho \\\"done $(ts)\\\" | tee -a \\\"$LOG/summary.txt\\\"\\n```\\n\\n```sh\\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \\\"bash -lc \\\\\\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\\\\\"\\\"'\\n```\\n\\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\\n(measured 2026-10-11).\\n\\n### Known differences from the laptop\\n\\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\\n  The assertion \\\"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\\\" gets no `bind-failed`\\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\\n\\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\\n\\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\\n\\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\\n\\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\\n\\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\\n\\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\\n\\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\\n\\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\\n\\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\\n\\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\\n   ```sh\\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\\n\\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\\n   Common patterns:\\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\\n\\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
